Guide.content._register("w14", {"id":"w14","order":14,"region":"senses","title":"Evaluation","short":"Evaluate","tagline":"Prove it works before launch, then keep proving it with live traffic.","story":"The answer came back, and it sounds right. Nobody on the team can say whether it is right, whether the last change made it worse, or whether it will be right tomorrow. This world turns opinions into measurements: golden sets, retrieval and answer metrics, judges you can trust, agent runs you can repeat and gates that block a bad release.","oneLiner":"Measure each stage on its own, calibrate your judges, quantify the noise, and gate releases on evidence.","youWillLearn":["Place component, end-to-end, offline and online checks so a failure points at one stage","Build a golden set from nothing and keep it versioned, labelled by origin and free of contamination","Choose between precision at k, recall at k, MRR, nDCG and judged context metrics, and say which failure each one catches","Set up an LLM judge, expose its position, verbosity and self-preference bias, and calibrate it against expert labels","Grade agents on outcomes and trajectories, and report pass^k and confidence intervals instead of one average","Design CI gates, red team scans, shadow runs and sampled live scoring, and say what each costs"],"minutes":120,"prereqs":["w05","w10"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w14-fc-001","front":"What does **recall at k** measure, and what does it need that precision at k does not?","back":"Relevant documents in the top k divided by all relevant documents. It needs a count of every relevant document per query, so the labels must be complete.","tags":["retrieval-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-002","front":"How is **MRR** computed? Worked example: first-relevant ranks 1, 3, none, 2.","back":"Reciprocal rank is 1 divided by the rank of the first correct result (0 if none). MRR is the mean over queries: (1 + 0.333 + 0 + 0.5) / 4 = 0.458.","tags":["retrieval-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-003","front":"When do you pick **nDCG** over MRR?","back":"When relevance is graded and several chunks matter. MRR looks only at the first correct result and ignores everything ranked after it.","tags":["retrieval-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-004","front":"Why can two libraries report different nDCG for the same ranking?","back":"Two gain formulas exist: linear rel and exponential 2^rel - 1. They match for binary labels but differ for graded ones (relevance 3, 2, 0, 1 gives 0.985 versus 0.993).","tags":["retrieval-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-005","front":"What are **holes** in the Foundry Document Retrieval evaluator?","back":"Retrieved documents that have no relevance judgment in your ground truth. `holes_ratio` shows how much of the rank metric rests on unlabelled documents.","tags":["retrieval-metrics","foundry"]},{"id":"w14-fc-006","front":"RAGAS **faithfulness**: what is divided by what?","back":"Claims in the response supported by the retrieved context, divided by total claims in the response. It measures consistency with context, not truth.","tags":["rag-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-007","front":"Why can an answer score high on faithfulness and still be wrong?","back":"The retrieved context can itself be wrong, stale or off topic. Faithfulness only checks support from that context. Pair it with context recall and a reference-based check.","tags":["rag-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-008","front":"Which RAGAS metric rewards putting relevant chunks first?","back":"Context precision. It averages precision at k over the ranks that hold a relevant chunk, so moving a relevant chunk earlier raises the score.","tags":["rag-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-009","front":"What does RAGAS **answer relevancy** actually compute?","back":"An LLM regenerates N questions from the response (default 3). The score is the mean cosine similarity of their embeddings to the user input. It does not judge facts.","tags":["rag-metrics"]},{"id":"w14-fc-010","front":"What is judge **position bias** and its standard fix?","back":"Judges favour an answer because of its slot, usually the first. Call twice with swapped order and count a win only if it holds both ways.","tags":["judge"]},{"id":"w14-fc-011","front":"How did reference-guided judging change math grading in MT-Bench?","back":"Judge failures fell from 14 of 20 with the default prompt to 6 with chain-of-thought and 3 with a reference-guided judge.","tags":["judge"]},{"id":"w14-fc-012","front":"Why report true positive and true negative rates when calibrating a judge?","back":"With imbalanced pass rates, raw agreement can look high while the judge misses the rare class. TPR and TNR show which class it gets wrong.","tags":["judge"]},{"id":"w14-fc-013","front":"What is **critique shadowing** for judge prompts?","back":"One accountable domain expert gives pass/fail judgments with written critiques. You iterate the judge prompt until it agrees with them, then keep checking.","tags":["judge"]},{"id":"w14-fc-014","front":"pass@k versus **pass^k**?","back":"pass@k: at least one of k trials succeeds. pass^k: all k trials succeed. Use pass^k when customers need consistency, and pass@k when one success is enough.","tags":["agents"]},{"id":"w14-fc-015","front":"Unbiased estimator of pass^k from n trials with c successes?","back":"C(c, k) / C(n, k), averaged over tasks. With n = 5, c = 4 and k = 3 the estimate is 4 / 10 = 0.4.","tags":["agents","stats"]},{"id":"w14-fc-016","front":"A 75% per-trial agent runs a task 3 times. Chance all 3 succeed?","back":"About 42 percent (0.75^3), assuming independent trials. Reliability decays quickly as k grows, which a single average hides.","tags":["agents","stats"]},{"id":"w14-fc-017","front":"95% interval half-width for an 80% pass rate at n = 100?","back":"About 7.8 points (1.96 x sqrt(0.8 x 0.2 / 100)). At n = 400 it is about 3.9. Small suites cannot see small changes.","tags":["stats"]},{"id":"w14-fc-018","front":"Why use clustered standard errors on eval results?","back":"Questions drawn from the same passage or user are not independent. Clustered standard errors can exceed 3 times the naive ones, so naive intervals look too tight.","tags":["stats"]},{"id":"w14-fc-019","front":"Capability evals versus regression evals?","back":"Capability evals start at a low pass rate and ask what the agent can do. Regression evals should sit near 100% and guard existing behaviour. Mastered capability tasks graduate.","tags":["golden-set","online-offline"]},{"id":"w14-fc-020","front":"How many tasks should a first golden set hold?","back":"Anthropic suggests 20 to 50 simple tasks drawn from real failures. Large early effects need few samples, while mature agents need larger and harder sets.","tags":["golden-set"]},{"id":"w14-fc-021","front":"Foundry synthetic datasets: task types and the row cap?","back":"Simple QnA (`query` and `ground_truth`) and Simulation seed for multi-turn. `max_samples` accepts 1 to 1,000. Generate a small batch first and review rows by hand.","tags":["golden-set","foundry"]},{"id":"w14-fc-022","front":"How does GAIA limit benchmark contamination?","back":"It withholds the answers to 300 of its 466 questions to power a leaderboard and publishes 166 as a development set.","tags":["golden-set"]},{"id":"w14-fc-023","front":"When does the OpenAI Evals platform go read-only and shut down?","back":"Announced 2026-06-03. Existing evals become read-only on 2026-10-31. The dashboard and API are scheduled to shut down on 2026-11-30. OpenAI points to Promptfoo for migration.","tags":["tools","online-offline"]},{"id":"w14-fc-024","front":"Foundry continuous evaluation: default cap and status?","back":"`max_hourly_runs` defaults to 100 per hour, and runs are skipped at the cap. Preview as of 2026-10-13. The project managed identity needs the Foundry User role.","tags":["foundry","online-offline"]},{"id":"w14-fc-025","front":"What does **Attack Success Rate** measure in the Foundry red teaming agent?","back":"Successful attacks divided by total attacks. A generative model scores it, so it can be non-deterministic with false positives. Review attacks before mitigating.","tags":["red-team","foundry"]},{"id":"w14-fc-026","front":"What do `builtin.output_quality` and `builtin.tool_use_quality` do?","back":"Each batches several criteria into one judge call (preview). Default pass thresholds are 3 on 1 to 5 scales and 1 on 0/1 scales. The primary result passes only if every component passes.","tags":["foundry","agents"]},{"id":"w14-fc-027","front":"When is shadow traffic safer than an A/B test?","back":"When a worse version could harm users or no quality signal is proven. Shadow copies requests without changing responses. Azure ML mirroring caps mirrored traffic at 50%.","tags":["online-offline"]},{"id":"w14-fc-028","front":"What differs about references between offline and online evals?","back":"Offline datasets have reference outputs, so they support reference-based metrics. Production traffic has none, so online scoring uses reference-free judges and safety checks.","tags":["online-offline"]},{"id":"w14-fc-029","front":"Why grade agent outcomes more than exact tool sequences?","back":"Several valid paths exist. A fixed expected trajectory punishes valid alternatives, and one model found a legitimate policy loophole and was marked wrong by a rigid grader.","tags":["agents"]},{"id":"w14-fc-030","front":"How does the Foundry compare view label significance?","back":"Against a baseline run, with t-tests: Strong (ImprovedStrong or DegradedStrong) when p <= 0.001 and Weak when 0.001 < p <= 0.05.","tags":["foundry","stats"]},{"id":"w14-t01-fc1","front":"Which three classic-testing assumptions do LLM apps break?","back":"Same input gives the same output, one expected output exists, and a failure points to one place. LLM apps vary run to run, accept many valid answers and fail in stacked layers.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t01"},{"id":"w14-t01-fc2","front":"Capability eval vs regression eval?","back":"Capability evals ask whether it can do this at all and start at a low pass rate. Regression evals ask whether it still does what it did and should sit near 100%.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t01"},{"id":"w14-t01-fc3","front":"Name the three grader types.","back":"Code-based for mechanical checks, model-based for meaning against a rubric, human for calibration and taste.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t01"},{"id":"w14-t02-fc1","front":"What can offline evaluation use that online cannot?","back":"Reference outputs. Offline examples carry gold answers, so reference-based metrics and exact comparisons work. Online traces have none.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t02"},{"id":"w14-t02-fc2","front":"How does Foundry label its three lifecycle stages?","back":"Base model selection, pre-production evaluation and post-production monitoring, which adds continuous evaluation, scheduled evaluation, scheduled red teaming and alerts.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t02"},{"id":"w14-t02-fc3","front":"What is the cost control for online scoring?","back":"Sampling rate and filters. Each scored trace costs judge tokens, so sample, dedupe and cap runs per hour.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t02"},{"id":"w14-t03-fc1","front":"Why keep both component and end-to-end evals?","back":"Component scores localise a fault. End-to-end scores say whether users are affected. Only the second catches interaction effects such as context order or truncation.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t03"},{"id":"w14-t03-fc2","front":"How do you test generation in isolation?","back":"Freeze the retrieved context (gold or recorded) and score only the answer, for example with groundedness and completeness.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t03"},{"id":"w14-t03-fc3","front":"What are Hamel Husain's three eval levels?","back":"Fast assertions on every change, model and human evaluation on a cadence, and A/B tests after significant product changes. Cost rises at each level.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t03"},{"id":"w14-t04-fc1","front":"How large should a first golden set be?","back":"20 to 50 simple tasks drawn from real failures is a strong start. Large early effects need small samples. Grow it as changes get subtler.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t04"},{"id":"w14-t04-fc2","front":"Two meanings of dataset contamination?","back":"Models may have seen a public benchmark in training, and your team may overfit its own set. Withhold answers or keep a held-out slice to control both.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t04"},{"id":"w14-t04-fc3","front":"What should each golden row record?","back":"Input, reference or rubric, source (hand, trace, synthetic), tags, split and dataset version.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t04"},{"id":"w14-t05-fc1","front":"Foundry synthetic dataset task types?","back":"Simple QnA (single turn, query and ground_truth) and Simulation seed (multi-turn scenarios with test_case_description). max_samples accepts 1 to 1,000.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t05"},{"id":"w14-t05-fc2","front":"Three bias risks of synthetic test data?","back":"Questions reuse chunk wording and flatter lexical retrieval, same-family generators write easy questions, and generated references can be wrong.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t05"},{"id":"w14-t05-fc3","front":"When must synthetic rows be regenerated?","back":"Whenever the system instructions change. Review a small batch first and combine synthetic with trace-derived data.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t05"},{"id":"w14-t06-fc1","front":"MRR in one line?","back":"Mean over queries of 1 divided by the rank of the first relevant result. Ranks 1, 3, none, 2 give 0.458.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t06"},{"id":"w14-t06-fc2","front":"Why is precision@k unstable?","back":"It depends on how many relevant documents exist per query. With 8 relevant, a perfect system scores at most 0.4 at k=20.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t06"},{"id":"w14-t06-fc3","front":"Why is nDCG not one number across tools?","back":"Linear gain uses rel_i, exponential gain uses 2^rel_i - 1. The same ranking can score 0.985 or 0.993.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t06"},{"id":"w14-t06-fc4","front":"What are holes in Foundry's Document Retrieval evaluator?","back":"Retrieved documents with no relevance judgment. Track holes_ratio because unjudged documents distort rank metrics.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t06"},{"id":"w14-t07-fc1","front":"Faithfulness formula?","back":"Claims in the response supported by the retrieved context divided by total claims in the response. It checks consistency with context, not truth.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t07"},{"id":"w14-t07-fc2","front":"How is answer (response) relevancy computed?","back":"An LLM generates N questions (default 3) from the response. The score is the mean cosine similarity of their embeddings to the user question. It needs an embedding model and ignores facts.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t07"},{"id":"w14-t07-fc3","front":"What does noise sensitivity measure?","back":"The share of response claims that are wrong against the reference yet attributable to retrieved context. Lower is better. Modes: relevant (default) and irrelevant.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t07"},{"id":"w14-t08-fc1","front":"Which two SDK paths exist for Foundry evaluation?","back":"Classic `azure-ai-evaluation` with `evaluate()`, and new cloud evaluation via `azure-ai-projects` 2.x with `AIProjectClient` and `get_openai_client()`. Docs mix both, so read the banner.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t08"},{"id":"w14-t08-fc2","front":"Default pass thresholds for composite agent evaluators?","back":"3 on 1 to 5 scales and 1 on 0/1 scales. The primary result passes only if every applicable component passes.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t08"},{"id":"w14-t08-fc3","front":"Which agent tools have limited evaluator support?","back":"Azure AI Search, Bing Grounding, Bing Custom Search, SharePoint Grounding, Code Interpreter, Fabric Data Agent and Web Search. Avoid four tool metrics and groundedness there.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t08"},{"id":"w14-t09-fc1","front":"Why prefer pass/fail or pairwise over a 1 to 5 score?","back":"Fine-grained scales are noisy and uncalibrated. Binary or pairwise decisions are easier to define, calibrate and gate on.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t09"},{"id":"w14-t09-fc2","front":"What is critique shadowing?","back":"One principal domain expert gives pass/fail judgments with written critiques, and the judge prompt is iterated until it agrees with them.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t09"},{"id":"w14-t09-fc3","front":"What does a reference-guided judge change?","back":"The judge sees the gold answer. In MT-Bench math it cut judge failures from 14 of 20 to 3 of 20.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t09"},{"id":"w14-t10-fc1","front":"Why is raw agreement misleading for judges?","back":"With imbalanced classes it is dominated by the majority. If 95% pass, an always-pass judge has 95% agreement and a 0% true negative rate. Report TPR and TNR.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t10"},{"id":"w14-t10-fc2","front":"How do you fix position bias in pairwise judging?","back":"Judge both orders and count a win only if it holds both ways, or randomise order. Cost is about double the calls.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t10"},{"id":"w14-t10-fc3","front":"What is self-preference bias?","back":"A judge favouring its own model family's outputs. MT-Bench saw about 10% (GPT-4) and 25% (Claude-v1) higher win rates but called it inconclusive. Use a different judge family.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t10"},{"id":"w14-t11-fc1","front":"Outcome vs process grading for agents?","back":"Outcome checks the end state. Process checks tool selection, arguments and output use. Grade outcome first, add process checks to localise failures.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t11"},{"id":"w14-t11-fc2","front":"Why avoid demanding one exact tool sequence?","back":"Several paths can be valid, and a rigid grader marks correct runs wrong. Anthropic cites a model finding a policy loophole on a tau2-bench task and being graded wrong.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t11"},{"id":"w14-t11-fc3","front":"Why isolate each trial in a clean environment?","back":"Shared state creates correlated failures between trials, so results stop being independent.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t11"},{"id":"w14-t12-fc1","front":"pass@k vs pass^k?","back":"pass@k: at least one of k trials succeeds. pass^k: all k trials succeed. pass^1 equals pass@1. Use pass^k when consistency matters.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t12"},{"id":"w14-t12-fc2","front":"95% half-width at n=100 for an 80% pass rate?","back":"About 7.8 points (1.96 x sqrt(0.8 x 0.2 / 100)). At n=400 it is 3.9 and at n=1,000 it is 2.5.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t12"},{"id":"w14-t12-fc3","front":"Why compare two models paired?","back":"Question-level differences on the same items cancel item difficulty and shrink variance. Anthropic's error-bar guidance recommends it, plus clustered standard errors and power analysis.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t12"},{"id":"w14-t13-fc1","front":"What is ASR?","back":"Attack success rate: successful attacks divided by total attacks. Scored by a generative model, so it can be non-deterministic and needs human review.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t13"},{"id":"w14-t13-fc2","front":"Which targets does the cloud agentic red teaming support?","back":"Foundry prompt agents and hosted container agents with Azure tool calls. Not workflow agents, non-Foundry agents, function tool calls, browser automation, connected agents or computer use.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t13"},{"id":"w14-t13-fc3","front":"What is a purple environment?","back":"A non-production environment with production-like resources, used to run red team scans safely.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t13"},{"id":"w14-t14-fc1","front":"Which suites should block a release?","back":"Safety and regression suites. Capability suites start at a low pass rate, so keep them advisory.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t14"},{"id":"w14-t14-fc2","front":"What does the rule of three say?","back":"With zero failures in n runs, the 95% upper bound on the failure rate is about 3/n. Thirty clean runs still allow roughly a 10% failure rate.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t14"},{"id":"w14-t14-fc3","front":"How does the microsoft/ai-agent-evals action report results?","back":"It compares agent versions (name:version, comma separated) and outputs confidence intervals and a significance test. The first agent is the baseline unless baseline-agent-id is set. Preview.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t14"},{"id":"w14-t15-fc1","front":"Shadow vs canary?","back":"Shadow mirrors requests to the new version and discards its answers, so users see nothing. Canary sends a small share of users to the new version, so some are exposed.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t15"},{"id":"w14-t15-fc2","front":"Azure ML traffic mirroring limits?","back":"Maximum mirrored share 50%, one shadow deployment per endpoint, not supported on Kubernetes online endpoints. No Foundry-native shadow feature found on 2026-10-13.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t15"},{"id":"w14-t15-fc3","front":"What are feedback signals good for?","back":"Triage. Thumbs, edits, retries and escalations are cheap but biased toward vocal users, so use them to find new test cases, not as the only metric.","tags":[],"topic":"w14-t15"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Observability in generative AI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/observability","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Lifecycle map: base model selection, pre-production evaluation, post-production monitoring.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"Built-in evaluators reference","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/built-in-evaluators","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Full evaluator catalog and suggested combinations. Mixed: several evaluators carry preview labels.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r3","title":"Agent evaluators","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/evaluation-evaluators/agent-evaluators","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"System versus process evaluators, composite evaluators, thresholds and tool support gaps. Mostly preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r4","title":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) evaluators","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/evaluation-evaluators/rag-evaluators","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Retrieval, Document Retrieval, Groundedness, Relevance and Response Completeness. Some are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r5","title":"AI Red Teaming Agent","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/ai-red-teaming-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Risk categories, Attack Success Rate, agentic categories and documented limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r6","title":"Run AI Red Teaming Agent locally","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/how-to/develop/run-scans-ai-red-teaming-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Working RedTeam and scan code, complexity groups and the num_objectives default.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r7","title":"Monitor agents with the Agent Monitoring Dashboard","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/how-to-monitor-agents-dashboard","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Continuous and scheduled evaluation, sampling, the hourly run cap and alerts. Preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r8","title":"Generate a synthetic evaluation dataset","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/evaluation-dataset-synthetic","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Simple QnA and simulation seed tasks, max_samples and the best practices list.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r9","title":"How to run an evaluation in GitHub Action","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/how-to/evaluation-github-action","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"CI gate for Foundry agents with confidence intervals and significance tests. Preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r10","title":"Evaluation (Agent Framework)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/agent-framework/agents/evaluation","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"evaluate_agent, LocalEvaluator and FoundryEvals for agent code.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r11","title":"Evaluate generative AI applications (learning path)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/paths/evaluate-generative-ai-apps/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Structured study path for the Microsoft stack.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r12","title":"Run evaluations and generate synthetic datasets (module)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/training/modules/run-evaluations-generate-synthetic-datasets/","type":"course","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Hands-on with the Azure AI Evaluation SDK and synthetic data.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r13","title":"Evaluating AI Agents","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/evaluating-ai-agents/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Short course on agent evaluation loops and trajectory checks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"Building and Evaluating Advanced RAG","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/building-evaluating-advanced-rag/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"RAG pipeline evaluation in practice.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r15","title":"Red Teaming LLM Applications","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/short-courses/red-teaming-llm-applications/","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI","note":"Red teaming concepts and exercises.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r16","title":"Using LLM-as-a-Judge for evaluation","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/llm-judge/","type":"blog","publisher":"Hamel Husain","note":"Critique shadowing, binary judgments and calibration, published 2024-10-29.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r17","title":"Demystifying evals for AI agents","url":"https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/demystifying-evals-for-ai-agents","type":"blog","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Graders, capability versus regression evals, pass@k versus pass^k, published 2026-01-09.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r18","title":"Evaluation best practices","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/evaluation-best-practices","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Judge and dataset guidance. The Evals platform it sits in is being shut down, but the advice stands.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"deprecated"},{"id":"r19","title":"Deprecations (Evals platform entry)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/deprecations","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Exact shutdown dates for the Evals platform: 2026-10-31 read-only, 2026-11-30 shutdown.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"RAGAS available metrics","url":"https://docs.ragas.io/en/stable/concepts/metrics/available_metrics/","type":"doc","publisher":"Ragas","note":"Metric definitions and formulas for faithfulness, relevancy, context precision and recall.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r21","title":"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.05685","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Zheng et al.)","note":"Position, verbosity and self-enhancement bias numbers and the 80 percent agreement result.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"G-Eval: NLG Evaluation using GPT-4 with Better Human Alignment","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16634","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Liu et al.)","note":"Rubric plus chain-of-thought judging and its bias toward LLM-generated text.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r23","title":"tau-bench: Tool-Agent-User Interaction in Real-World Domains","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Yao et al., Sierra)","note":"Defines pass^k and end-state grading.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r24","title":"Adding Error Bars to Evals","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00640","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Anthropic)","note":"Confidence intervals, clustered standard errors, paired differences and power analysis.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"Evaluation of ranked retrieval results","url":"https://nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/html/htmledition/evaluation-of-ranked-retrieval-results-1.html","type":"course","publisher":"Stanford (Manning, Raghavan, Schutze)","note":"Precision at k, MAP and NDCG from first principles.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"PyRIT repository","url":"https://github.com/microsoft/PyRIT","type":"repo","publisher":"Microsoft","note":"The open-source red teaming framework behind the Foundry agent.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r27","title":"Promptfoo in CI/CD","url":"https://www.promptfoo.dev/docs/integrations/ci-cd/","type":"tool","publisher":"Promptfoo","note":"Config-driven evals and red teaming in pipelines.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r28","title":"DeepEval metrics introduction","url":"https://deepeval.com/docs/metrics-introduction","type":"tool","publisher":"DeepEval","note":"Pytest-style metric catalog including agent trajectory metrics.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r29","title":"LangSmith evaluation concepts","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/evaluation-concepts","type":"doc","publisher":"LangChain","note":"Offline versus online evaluation, dataset versions and splits, sampling and pairwise evaluators.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r30","title":"Braintrust: evaluate systematically","url":"https://www.braintrust.dev/docs/evaluate","type":"tool","publisher":"Braintrust","note":"Immutable experiments, CI runs and asynchronous online scoring.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r31","title":"Large Language Models are not Fair Evaluators","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17926","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Wang et al.)","note":"Shows answer order flipping a ranking and proposes calibration fixes.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r32","title":"LLM Evaluators Recognize and Favor Their Own Generations","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.13076","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Panickssery et al.)","note":"Self-recognition correlates with self-preference in judges.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r33","title":"GAIA: a benchmark for General AI Assistants","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.12983","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Mialon et al.)","note":"466 questions with withheld answers as a contamination control.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r34","title":"SWE-bench Verified dataset card","url":"https://huggingface.co/datasets/princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified","type":"repo","publisher":"Hugging Face (Princeton NLP)","note":"Human-validated subset of 500 samples drawn from SWE-bench.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r35","title":"tau2-bench repository (README brands it tau3-bench)","url":"https://github.com/sierra-research/tau2-bench","type":"repo","publisher":"Sierra Research","note":"Version 1.0.1 (July 2026) changed grading on banking_knowledge tasks.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r36","title":"Safe rollout of online endpoints (traffic mirroring)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/machine-learning/how-to-safely-rollout-online-endpoints","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Shadow testing by mirroring live traffic, with its 50 percent cap.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r37","title":"Evaluation regions, limits and virtual network support","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/evaluation-regions-limits-virtual-network","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Row and batch limits, rate limiting and region lists for evaluation features.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r38","title":"Run cloud evaluations with the Foundry SDK","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/cloud-evaluation","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"The new azure-ai-projects path for cloud evaluation.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r39","title":"See evaluation results in Foundry","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/how-to/evaluate-results","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Compare view with baseline run, t-tests and the target cost estimate.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r40","title":"Evaluate deployed interactions and traces","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/cloud-evaluation-deployed-interactions","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Scoring stored responses and traces, including Intelligent sampling. Preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r41","title":"Custom evaluators","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/evaluation-evaluators/custom-evaluators","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Code-based, prompt-based and endpoint-based evaluators. Preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r42","title":"BEIR: A Heterogeneous Benchmark for Zero-shot Evaluation of Information Retrieval Models","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08663","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Thakur et al.)","note":"18 datasets, BM25 as a strong baseline and the cost of re-ranking.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r43","title":"HarmBench: A Standardized Evaluation Framework for Automated Red Teaming","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.04249","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Mazeika et al.)","note":"18 red-teaming methods compared on 33 target models and defenses.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r44","title":"Justice or Prejudice? Quantifying Biases in LLM-as-a-Judge","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.02736","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (CALM)","note":"Quantifies 12 judge biases, beyond position and verbosity.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r45","title":"Promptfoo is joining OpenAI","url":"https://www.promptfoo.dev/blog/promptfoo-joining-openai/","type":"blog","publisher":"Promptfoo","note":"Announcement dated 2026-03-09 that the open-source suite continues.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r46","title":"Discounted cumulative gain","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discounted_cumulative_gain","type":"doc","publisher":"Wikipedia","note":"Linear and exponential gain variants and why scores differ across tools.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r47","title":"AgentDojo: A Dynamic Environment to Evaluate Prompt Injection Attacks and Defenses for LLM Agents","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13352","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Debenedetti et al.)","note":"97 tasks and 629 security test cases for injection against tool-using agents.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r48","title":"Why SWE-bench Verified was retired (secondary write-up)","url":"https://blog.pebblous.ai/blog/swe-bench-verified-retired/en/","type":"blog","publisher":"Pebblous","note":"Secondary source for the reported February 2026 retirement. The primary page blocked automated fetch, so treat the numbers as reported.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Risk and Safety Evaluators for Generative AI","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/concepts/evaluation-evaluators/risk-safety-evaluators","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Safety evaluator list, agent-only evaluators and the defect rate. Prohibited actions and sensitive data leakage are labelled preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Planning red teaming for large language models (LLMs) and their applications","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/red-teaming","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"How to staff, scope and sequence manual red teaming before systematic measurement.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr3","title":"Who Validates the Validators? Aligning LLM-Assisted Evaluation of LLM Outputs with Human Preferences","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12272","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Shankar et al.)","note":"Names criteria drift: grading outputs helps people define the criteria they grade by.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"Testset Generation (Ragas)","url":"https://docs.ragas.io/en/stable/concepts/test_data_generation/","type":"doc","publisher":"Ragas","note":"Characteristics of an ideal test dataset and the RAG and agent generators.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Evaluate your AI agents (Foundry)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/observability/how-to/evaluate-agent","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Rubric evaluator as the recommended primary agent measure, generated from the agent's context. Rubric evaluators are preview.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Your AI Product Needs Evals","url":"https://hamel.dev/blog/posts/evals/","type":"blog","publisher":"Hamel Husain","note":"Three levels of evaluation, assertions on every change, and pass rate as a product decision.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Benchmark Data Contamination of Large Language Models: A Survey","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.04244","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Why contamination makes benchmark results unreliable and what alternatives exist.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.03374","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Chen et al.)","note":"Codex and HumanEval: the abstract gives 28.8% with one sample against 70.2% with 100 samples per problem; Table 2 lists 72.3% at 100 samples for the 12B model.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr9","title":"Binomial proportion confidence interval","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_proportion_confidence_interval","type":"doc","publisher":"Wikipedia","note":"Wilson score interval for small samples and the rule of three for zero observed failures.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr10","title":"Mean reciprocal rank","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_reciprocal_rank","type":"doc","publisher":"Wikipedia","note":"Definition and the worked example with ranks 1, 3, none and 2.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"}],"glossary":[{"term":"Golden set","def":"A versioned collection of test cases with expected outputs or relevance labels, used as the fixed yardstick for release decisions."},{"term":"Qrels","def":"Query relevance judgments: for each query, which documents are relevant and, for graded metrics, how relevant."},{"term":"MRR","def":"Mean reciprocal rank. The average over queries of 1 divided by the rank of the first correct result."},{"term":"nDCG","def":"Normalized discounted cumulative gain. Rank quality with graded relevance, divided by the score of the ideal ordering. Gain can be linear or `2^rel - 1`."},{"term":"Faithfulness","def":"Share of claims in a response that the retrieved context supports. It measures consistency with context, not truth."},{"term":"Position bias","def":"A judge preference for an answer because of where it appears in the prompt, not because of its quality."},{"term":"Self-preference","def":"A judge favouring outputs that resemble its own, which inflates scores when judge and generator share a model family."},{"term":"pass^k","def":"The probability that all k independent trials of a task succeed. It measures reliability, while pass@k measures whether any trial succeeds."},{"term":"Trajectory","def":"The ordered record of an agent run: model messages, tool calls, arguments and tool results."},{"term":"Contamination","def":"Test items or answers leaking into training data or prompts, so scores reflect memory instead of ability."},{"term":"Attack Success Rate","def":"In red teaming, successful attacks divided by total attacks. Foundry scores it with a generative model, so review results by hand."},{"term":"Shadow traffic","def":"Copies of live requests sent to a candidate version whose responses are scored but never shown to users."},{"term":"Continuous evaluation","def":"Scoring a sample of live traffic with evaluators on an ongoing basis, usually with reference-free judges."},{"term":"Held-out set","def":"Cases kept out of tuning and prompt iteration so they still measure generalization at release time."},{"term":"Hit rate@k","def":"Share of queries with at least one relevant result in the top k. A conventional definition; no vendor evaluator page in scope names it."},{"term":"Recall@k","def":"Relevant results in the top k divided by all relevant documents for the query. Shows whether the evidence reached the prompt."},{"term":"Precision@k","def":"Relevant results in the top k divided by k. Unstable across queries because it depends on how many relevant documents exist."},{"term":"Holes","def":"Retrieved documents with no relevance judgment. Foundry reports `holes` and `holes_ratio` because unjudged items distort rank metrics."},{"term":"Context precision","def":"RAGAS metric: the mean of precision at k over ranks that hold a relevant chunk, so putting relevant chunks first raises it."},{"term":"Context recall","def":"RAGAS metric: claims in the reference answer attributable to the retrieved context divided by all claims in the reference. Needs a reference."},{"term":"Noise sensitivity","def":"RAGAS metric: share of response claims that are wrong against the reference while attributable to retrieved context. Lower is better."},{"term":"LLM-as-judge","def":"A model that scores or compares outputs against a rubric. Reliable only after calibration against human labels."},{"term":"Pairwise comparison","def":"Asking a judge which of two answers is better. Suited to ranking two candidates and run in both orders to cancel position bias."},{"term":"Critique shadowing","def":"Hamel Husain's method: one principal expert gives pass/fail verdicts with written critiques, then the judge prompt is iterated until it agrees."},{"term":"Criteria drift","def":"People need criteria to grade outputs, but grading outputs changes the criteria. Expect to revise a rubric while labelling."},{"term":"Rubric evaluator","def":"A Foundry evaluator (preview) made of weighted scoring dimensions applied by an LLM judge, written by hand or generated from the agent's context."},{"term":"Composite evaluator","def":"A Foundry evaluator such as `builtin.output_quality` that batches several criteria into one judge call and passes only if every applicable component passes."},{"term":"Defect rate","def":"The aggregate share of responses in which a safety evaluator detects undesired content."},{"term":"Purple environment","def":"A non-production environment with production-like resources, used to run red team scans without real side effects."},{"term":"Wilson interval","def":"A confidence interval for a proportion that behaves better than the normal approximation for small samples and rates near 0 or 100%."},{"term":"Rule of three","def":"With zero failures in n independent runs, the 95% upper bound on the failure rate is about 3/n."},{"term":"Canary release","def":"Sending a small share of users to a new version, with stop-loss metrics, before wider rollout."},{"term":"Synthetic dataset","def":"Test cases generated by a model from documents or an agent definition. Needs review, a provenance tag and regeneration when instructions change."},{"term":"Regression eval","def":"A suite that checks the system still does what it did. It should sit near 100% and can block a release, unlike a capability eval."}],"levels":[{"id":"w14-l1","order":1,"title":"Draw the map before you pick a metric","kind":"concept","minutes":12,"brief":"A team asks how it will know the assistant works. The stage, the traffic, the risk and the label budget are all unspecified. Decide which checks run where, when they run and what they are allowed to cost.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Three questions before any metric","level":2,"id":"three-questions-before-any-metric"},{"t":"p","md":"Every evaluation answers three questions. **Unit**: do you score one component (retriever, tool selector, judge) or the whole path? **Time**: do you run before release against a fixed set, or after release against live traffic? **Reference**: do you hold an expected answer, or only the question and the output?\n\nFoundry frames the lifecycle as base model selection, pre-production evaluation and post-production monitoring [[r1]]. LangSmith draws the same line from the data side: offline runs use dataset examples that carry reference outputs, while online runs score production traces that have none [[r29]]. Capability evals start at a low pass rate and ask what the agent can do at all. Regression evals should sit near 100% and guard what already works [[r17]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-l1-d1","title":"Where each check lives in the lifecycle","alt":"A loop from model selection to pre-production evaluation, release, post-production monitoring and failing traces that feed back into the pre-production set.","src":"flowchart LR\n  M[\"Choose base model\"] --> P[\"Pre-production evals\"]\n  P --> G[\"CI gate\"]\n  G --> R[\"Release\"]\n  R --> O[\"Post-production monitoring\"]\n  O --> T[\"Failing traces\"]\n  T --> P","caption":"Pre-production holds the golden set, red team scans and the CI gate. Foundry lists post-production monitoring as operational metrics, continuous evaluation, scheduled evaluation, scheduled red teaming and Azure Monitor alerts."},{"t":"compare","title":"Four layers, four jobs","intro":"These layers stack. The question is which one carries the most weight for your dials.","options":[{"name":"Component evals","summary":"Score one stage alone: retriever ranking, tool selection, parser output, the judge itself.","pros":["A failure points at one stage","Mostly deterministic or cheap, so it runs on every commit"],"cons":["Parts can pass while the whole path fails","Needs labels at the stage boundary, such as relevant chunk ids"],"bestWhen":"You change one stage often, or a regression must be localized in minutes.","avoidWhen":"It is your only check, because interactions between stages stay invisible.","meta":[{"k":"Cost","v":"low"},{"k":"Signal","v":"localizes the fault"}]},{"name":"End-to-end offline suite","summary":"Run golden tasks through the full system and grade the outcome.","pros":["Tests the path users actually take","Gives a stable baseline for a release gate"],"cons":["Judge calls multiply: items x evaluators x trials","A drop does not say which stage moved"],"bestWhen":"Before every release and after any prompt, model or tool change.","avoidWhen":"You need to know why it failed and have no component scores beside it.","meta":[{"k":"Cost","v":"medium to high"},{"k":"Signal","v":"does the whole path work"}]},{"name":"Online sampled scoring","tag":"Preview","summary":"Score a sample of live traces with reference-free judges and safety evaluators, off the request path.","pros":["Sees real queries and drift","Needs no reference answers","Scoring logged traces adds no user-facing latency"],"cons":["Detects problems after users saw the answer","Judge tokens per trace, so sampling and caps control spend","Reads real content, so privacy rules apply"],"bestWhen":"Traffic exists and you need drift and safety coverage.","avoidWhen":"You are pre-launch, or it is the only guard on high-harm output.","meta":[{"k":"Foundry cap","v":"100 runs per hour by default"},{"k":"Status","v":"Foundry preview, 2026-10-13"}]},{"name":"Expert trace review","summary":"A named domain expert reads sampled traces and writes pass/fail verdicts with critiques.","pros":["Finds failure types no metric names yet","Produces the labels that calibrate judges"],"cons":["Slowest and most expensive per item","Reviewers drift without a shared rubric"],"bestWhen":"Outputs are high-harm, or you need labels to calibrate a judge.","avoidWhen":"Volume is high and errors are cheap to undo.","meta":[{"k":"Cost","v":"high per item"},{"k":"Signal","v":"ground truth for judges"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w14-l1-mix","title":"Pick the evaluation mix","prompt":"Flip the constraints. The ranking shows which layer carries the most weight, and why.","dials":[{"id":"stage","label":"Stage","values":[{"id":"pre","label":"Before launch, no traces"},{"id":"live","label":"Live, traffic exists"}]},{"id":"harm","label":"Cost of a wrong answer","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Cheap to undo"},{"id":"high","label":"Causes real harm"}]},{"id":"budget","label":"Judge and expert budget","values":[{"id":"tight","label":"Scarce"},{"id":"ample","label":"Available"}]}],"options":[{"id":"comp","name":"Component evals","summary":"Score retriever, tool selection and parsers alone."},{"id":"e2e","name":"End-to-end golden suite","summary":"Whole-path tasks as a release gate."},{"id":"online","name":"Online sampled scoring","summary":"Reference-free judges on live traces."},{"id":"human","name":"Expert trace review","summary":"A named reviewer reads sampled traces."},{"id":"shadow","name":"Shadow run","summary":"Score a candidate on mirrored live requests."}],"scores":{"comp":{"stage":{"pre":{"s":2,"why":"Runs on synthetic and hand-written cases, so no traffic is needed."},"live":{"s":1,"why":"Still localizes a regression, though live traces show more."}},"harm":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Cheap, narrow checks are enough when mistakes are cheap."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Necessary but not sufficient, because parts can pass while the whole path fails."}},"budget":{"tight":{"s":2,"why":"Mostly code checks and rank metrics, with little judge spend."},"ample":{"s":0,"why":"Keeps its value, but extra budget buys more elsewhere."}}},"e2e":{"stage":{"pre":{"s":2,"why":"The only way to test the whole path before users do."},"live":{"s":1,"why":"Still the release gate, kept fresh by failing production traces."}},"harm":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Worth having, though not urgent when errors are cheap."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Whole-path failures such as a wrong tool sequence only show up here."}},"budget":{"tight":{"s":-1,"why":"Items x evaluators x trials adds up in judge calls."},"ample":{"s":1,"why":"Affords more trials and harder cases."}}},"online":{"stage":{"pre":{"s":-2,"why":"There is no traffic to sample."},"live":{"s":2,"why":"Real queries, and judge scorers need no reference answers."}},"harm":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"A cheap way to spot drift."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"It detects problems after users saw them, so it cannot be the only guard."}},"budget":{"tight":{"s":-1,"why":"Every scored trace costs judge tokens. Sampling is the control."},"ample":{"s":1,"why":"Allows a higher sampling rate and more scorers."}}},"human":{"stage":{"pre":{"s":-1,"why":"Few real traces exist, so reviewers read synthetic rows at best."},"live":{"s":1,"why":"Reading real traces finds failures no metric names."}},"harm":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"Expert time is wasted on cheap mistakes."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"A named reviewer is accountable for high-stakes output."}},"budget":{"tight":{"s":-2,"why":"Expert hours are the scarcest budget."},"ample":{"s":1,"why":"Reviews also produce the labels that calibrate judges."}}},"shadow":{"stage":{"pre":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs a live baseline and traffic to mirror."},"live":{"s":2,"why":"Real requests, and users never see the candidate."}},"harm":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"A direct rollout may be fine, so the extra safety buys little."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Users never see the candidate's answers, so a bad version costs nothing."}},"budget":{"tight":{"s":-1,"why":"It doubles inference on mirrored traffic and adds scoring."},"ample":{"s":1,"why":"Allows mirroring a larger share of traffic."}}}},"takeaway":"No layer wins on every dial. Say which dial drove your pick, then say which layer you would add next."},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes of the map","items":[{"symptom":"The score fell 6 points and nobody knows which stage moved","cause":"Only an end-to-end score exists","fix":"Add component scores at each stage boundary (ranked chunks, tool selection, judge agreement) and log them in the same run."},{"symptom":"The offline suite is green while production quality falls","cause":"The golden set stopped matching real queries","fix":"Sample failing production traces into the golden set on a schedule, tag them by origin, and watch online scores for drift."},{"symptom":"Every release is blocked by a suite that never passes","cause":"Capability tasks sit inside the release gate","fix":"Gate on regression and safety suites. Keep capability suites advisory until their tasks graduate."},{"symptom":"Online dashboards look healthy and users disagree","cause":"The reference-free judge was never compared with human labels","fix":"Sample traces for expert review, compare verdicts with the judge, and recalibrate before trusting the dashboard."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"preview","title":"Preview status, checked 2026-10-13","md":"Foundry continuous evaluation, recurring evaluations, red team scans, alerts and many agent evaluators carry preview labels. Preview features come without a service-level agreement, so plan for change and keep a code-first fallback [[r7]] [[r2]]."},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: the tool landscape on 2026-10-13","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Need","Fits","Watch for"],"rows":[["Azure-native, portal results, red teaming, continuous evaluation","Foundry evaluators with `azure-ai-evaluation` and `azure-ai-projects`","Many evaluators and runs are preview. Two SDK paths exist, so check the banner at the top of each docs page [[r2]] [[r38]]."],["Metric definitions you can read","RAGAS","Open source. The API moved to a collections API in 0.4, and the legacy `SingleTurnSample` code is deprecated [[r20]]."],["Pytest-style tests with trajectory metrics","DeepEval","Over 50 metrics, almost all LLM-judged. Default `threshold` is 0.5 [[r28]]."],["Config-file evals and red teaming in CI","promptfoo","Open source, driven by `promptfooconfig.yaml`. OpenAI agreed to acquire it on 2026-03-09 and the open-source suite continues [[r27]] [[r45]]."],["Tracing, datasets and online evaluators in one product","LangSmith, Braintrust","LangSmith sets sampling rate, filters and spend limits per attached project or dataset, not per evaluator. Braintrust experiments are immutable snapshots [[r29]] [[r30]]."],["Agent Framework applications","`evaluate_agent()` with `LocalEvaluator` and `FoundryEvals`","Local checks for CI, cloud judges for depth [[r10]]."]]}]},{"title":"Go deeper: the OpenAI Evals shutdown","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"OpenAI announced on 2026-06-03 that the Evals platform is deprecated. Existing evals become read-only on 2026-10-31, and the dashboard and API are scheduled to shut down on 2026-11-30. Graders documented for eval workflows are part of the transition, and OpenAI points to Promptfoo as the migration path [[r19]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Export what you need and move to a code-first harness. The Learn pages checked on 2026-10-13 show no matching retirement notice for Azure OpenAI graders, so treat the effect on Foundry's OpenAI-style evaluation API as unconfirmed. The judge and dataset advice in OpenAI's guide still holds [[r18]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1","q":"After a retriever swap the end-to-end pass rate fell 6 points, and nobody can say whether retrieval or generation is at fault. Which earlier choice would have located the fault fastest?","options":["Add several hundred more golden tasks, scored only end to end, to tighten the interval","Switch to a stronger judge model for the final answer","Score the ranked chunks with a retrieval evaluation before generation runs","Lower the generator temperature so runs repeat"],"answer":2,"explain":"A separate retrieval score shows whether the ranked chunks changed, which splits the fault between retrieval and generation. More end-to-end tasks tighten the interval but still give one number for the whole path. A stronger judge improves how the answer is graded, not where the fault sits. Lower temperature reduces run-to-run noise but does not localize a regression."},{"id":"w14-l1-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"ref":"r30","q":"Select every statement about online evaluation of live traffic that holds.","options":["Production traces carry no reference outputs, so it leans on reference-free judges and safety checks","Scoring can run asynchronously on logged traces without adding user-facing latency","It makes the offline golden set unnecessary because live queries are more realistic","Sampling rate and filters are the main lever on judge cost"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Live traces have no expected answer, so online scoring uses reference-free judges and safety checks [[r29]]. Braintrust scores logged traces asynchronously with no latency impact [[r30]]. Judge tokens scale with the number of scored traces, so sampling is the cost control. The third statement fails: online scoring cannot run reference-based metrics or gate a release against a fixed baseline, so the offline set stays."},{"id":"w14-l1-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":1,"q":"Put the evaluation feedback loop in order.","items":["Write cases from real failures","Run the suite as a release gate","Release and score sampled live traffic","Add failing production traces to the golden set","Rerun the gate on the enlarged set"],"explain":"Cases come first because the gate needs something to run. Release and live scoring follow the gate. Failing traces only exist after live scoring, and the enlarged set is what the next gate run uses. This is the loop Foundry draws between pre-production evaluation and post-production monitoring."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r7","r10","r11","r17","r18","r19","r20","r27","r28","r29","r30","r38","r45"],"takeaways":["Name the unit, the timing and the reference before you name a metric.","Component scores localize a fault. End-to-end scores say whether users are affected. You need both.","Offline sets gate releases, online scoring watches drift, and failing traces flow back into the offline set.","Capability suites stay advisory. Regression and safety suites block the release.","Label every preview feature with its date and keep a code-first fallback."]},{"id":"w14-l2","order":2,"title":"Plant the golden set","kind":"problem","minutes":14,"brief":"You have no labelled data and one domain expert with a free afternoon. The stage, the traffic and the drift you can tolerate are unspecified. Decide where cases come from and how you keep them honest.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four sources, four failure profiles","level":2,"id":"four-sources-four-failure-profiles"},{"t":"compare","title":"Where golden cases come from","intro":"Most strong sets mix sources. Pick the first source by stage, then add the others as traffic and budget appear.","options":[{"name":"Expert hand-written","summary":"A domain expert writes questions, expected answers and, for retrieval, the relevant chunks.","pros":["Highest label quality","Targets the cases the business cares about"],"cons":["Expert time is the scarcest resource","Small sets carry the author's blind spots"],"bestWhen":"The first 20 to 50 cases, drawn from real failures, or any high-stakes domain.","avoidWhen":"You need hundreds of cases by next week.","meta":[{"k":"Label cost","v":"high"},{"k":"Drift","v":"none"}]},{"name":"Mined from production traces","summary":"Pick real queries from logged traces, then label them.","pros":["Reflects how users actually behave","Failing traces become regression cases"],"cons":["Needs privacy and retention rules before reuse","Moves with users, so scores across refreshes are not comparable"],"bestWhen":"Traffic exists and you want the set to follow real use.","avoidWhen":"You are pre-launch, or you need a frozen baseline.","meta":[{"k":"Label cost","v":"medium"},{"k":"Drift","v":"tracks users"}]},{"name":"Synthetic from prompt and documents","summary":"Foundry data generation from an agent definition, an inline prompt or a reference file, as Simple QnA or multi-turn Simulation seed.","pros":["Works before launch","Stable baseline that does not drift with traffic","Expands edge-case coverage"],"cons":["Mirrors the generating prompt and can look uniform","Goes stale when instructions change","Needs hand review"],"bestWhen":"You are pre-launch, traffic is low, or you need a regression baseline.","avoidWhen":"It is the only source, or nobody reviews the rows.","meta":[{"k":"max_samples","v":"1 to 1,000"},{"k":"SDK","v":"azure-ai-projects 2.8.0 or later"}]},{"name":"Public benchmark","summary":"A published suite such as GAIA, SWE-bench Verified or tau-bench used as a sanity check.","pros":["Comparable across systems","No labelling effort"],"cons":["Saturates and can leak into training data","Does not cover your data or tools","Versions can change grading"],"bestWhen":"You are choosing a base model or tracking field-level capability.","avoidWhen":"It stands in for a test on your own data.","meta":[{"k":"GAIA","v":"466 questions, 300 answers withheld"}]}]},{"t":"flow","title":"Golden set lifecycle","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Seed","md":"Write 20 to 50 cases from real failures. Large early effects need few samples.","tag":"hand"},{"title":"Generate","md":"Make a small synthetic batch from the agent definition and reference files. Review rows by hand, then scale.","tag":"synthetic"},{"title":"Label","md":"One accountable expert adds expected answers, relevant chunk ids and written critiques."},{"title":"Version","md":"Tag each dataset version and record how each row was made: hand, trace or synthetic.","tag":"versioned"},{"title":"Split","md":"Separate regression, capability and held-out slices. Nobody tunes on the held-out slice."},{"title":"Refresh","md":"Add failing production traces. Regenerate synthetic rows and bump the version when instructions change.","tag":"traces"}]},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"One golden row that serves retrieval and answer metrics","code":"{\n  \"id\": \"q-0142\",\n  \"user_input\": \"How long do I have to request a refund on an annual plan?\",\n  \"reference\": \"Annual plans can be refunded within 30 days of purchase.\",\n  \"retrieval_ground_truth\": [\n    { \"document_id\": \"refund-policy#c2\", \"query_relevance_label\": 2 },\n    { \"document_id\": \"refund-policy#c5\", \"query_relevance_label\": 1 }\n  ],\n  \"origin\": \"trace\",\n  \"slice\": \"regression\",\n  \"dataset_version\": \"2026-10-13.1\"\n}","caption":"Illustrative row. The first three keys follow the field names RAGAS and the Foundry Document Retrieval evaluator read. `origin`, `slice` and `dataset_version` are your own bookkeeping, and the policy text is invented."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Starter set","value":"20 to 50","note":"Simple tasks drawn from real failures"},{"label":"Synthetic rows per job","value":"1 to 1,000","note":"The max_samples range"},{"label":"Batch evaluation limits","value":"100,000 rows","note":"At most 2 MB per row"},{"label":"GAIA withheld answers","value":"300 of 466","note":"166 form the public development set"},{"label":"SWE-bench Verified","value":"500 samples","note":"Human-validated subset. The original SWE-bench has 2,294 problems from 12 Python repositories"}]},{"t":"failure","title":"Failure modes of golden sets","items":[{"symptom":"A benchmark score is high and the product disappoints","cause":"Public suites saturate, leak into training data and do not cover your data","fix":"Keep a private, held-out slice built from your own failures. Use public suites for model selection only."},{"symptom":"Synthetic cases all look alike and every system passes","cause":"One generator prompt, never reviewed","fix":"Generate a small batch, review rows by hand, steer difficulty with a prompt plus a reference file, and add trace-derived cases."},{"symptom":"Scores jump after a prompt edit with no real behaviour change","cause":"The synthetic set mirrors the old instructions","fix":"Regenerate and version the set when instructions change materially, and rerun the baseline on both versions."},{"symptom":"The gate passes, then production regresses","cause":"The gate set doubled as the tuning set","fix":"Keep a held-out slice nobody tunes on, and refresh it from new traces."},{"symptom":"A leaderboard result cannot be reproduced","cause":"The benchmark changed grading between versions","fix":"Pin the benchmark version. Scores from before tau-bench v1.0.1 (July 2026) are not comparable on banking_knowledge tasks."}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Benchmarks age","md":"OpenAI reportedly stopped evaluating on SWE-bench Verified in February 2026. The reasons given were score saturation (the top score moved from 74.9% to 80.9% in six months) and an audit of 138 hard tasks in which 59.4% had flawed tests or problem statements, plus signs of training-data leakage. The primary page blocked automated fetch on 2026-10-13, so these numbers come from a secondary write-up [[r48]]. GAIA took the opposite approach and withholds the answers to 300 of its 466 questions [[r33]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-l2-q1","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":1,"ref":"r8","q":"You launch in two weeks, have no production traces, and need a baseline that will not drift as users change behaviour. Which source should supply the first set?","options":["Synthetic cases from the agent definition and reference files, reviewed by hand","Traces mined from production logs, labelled by a domain expert after the first week","A public benchmark score for the chosen base model","Thumbs-up and thumbs-down ratings collected after launch"],"answer":0,"explain":"Synthetic generation works before launch and gives a stable regression baseline that does not move with traffic [[r8]]. Production traces do not exist yet and would drift once they do. A public benchmark score describes the base model, not your data or tools. Ratings arrive after launch, are biased toward vocal users and are not labelled test cases."},{"id":"w14-l2-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","difficulty":2,"ref":"r8","q":"A teammate plans to submit these illustrative job settings to generate a first dataset. Which problem matters most?","code":{"lang":"json","code":"{\n  \"task\": \"simple_qna\",\n  \"source\": \"agent_definition\",\n  \"max_samples\": 2500\n}"},"options":["The job exceeds the 100,000-row limit that applies to batch evaluation runs and will be rejected outright","max_samples is outside the documented 1 to 1,000 range, and a first batch should be reviewable","Simple QnA cannot take an agent definition as its source, only an inline prompt or a file","Synthetic generation requires an uploaded reference file in every job"],"answer":1,"explain":"Evaluation jobs accept max_samples from 1 through 1,000, and the guidance is to start small and review rows manually before scaling [[r8]]. The 100,000-row figure is a different limit, on batch evaluation runs [[r37]]. Simple QnA needs an agent or prompt source, so an agent definition is allowed, and the reference file is optional."},{"id":"w14-l2-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"q":"Match each golden-set source to its defining trade-off.","pairs":[{"l":"Expert hand-written","r":"Highest label quality, scarcest expert time"},{"l":"Mined from production traces","r":"Follows real users but needs privacy rules and moves over time"},{"l":"Synthetic from prompt and documents","r":"Stable baseline that must be regenerated when instructions change"},{"l":"Public benchmark","r":"Comparable across systems but can saturate or leak into training data"}],"explain":"Each source pays for its strength somewhere else. Expert labels cost scarce hours. Traces bring realism and privacy duties, and the set drifts with users. Synthetic rows give a stable baseline but mirror the prompt that made them. Public suites compare systems but saturate, as the reported SWE-bench Verified retirement and GAIA's withheld answers show."},{"id":"w14-l2-q4","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"ref":"r8","q":"Select the practices Foundry guidance gives for synthetic evaluation datasets.","options":["Generate a small batch first and review the rows by hand before scaling","Mirror the instructions your production agent actually runs","Regenerate and version the dataset when the agent's instructions change materially","Generate the maximum rows first, because a strong generator makes review unnecessary"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"The guidance lists all three: a small first batch with manual review, instructions that match production because drift weakens the signal, and regeneration with a new version when the prompt changes [[r8]]. The fourth option fails because generator strength does not remove the need to spot-check rows, and an unreviewed maximum batch hides uniform or off-topic cases."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r8","r12","r17","r18","r29","r33","r34","r35","r37","r48"],"takeaways":["Start with 20 to 50 cases from real failures. Expert time, not volume, is the constraint.","Record how each row was made and which dataset version it belongs to.","Synthetic rows give a stable pre-launch baseline and traces give realism. Use both.","Keep a held-out slice nobody tunes on, and pin benchmark versions.","Public benchmarks help pick a base model. Your own set gates your releases."]},{"id":"w14-l3","order":3,"title":"Score the retrieval, then the answer","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"A RAG answer is wrong. The labels you hold, the number of chunks an answer needs and the completeness of your judgments are all unspecified. Pick the metrics that say which half broke before you touch the prompt.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Rank metrics in one table","level":2,"id":"rank-metrics-in-one-table"},{"t":"table","caption":"Rank metrics for retrieval, from the Manning information retrieval text and common practice [[r25]]","head":["Metric","Question it answers","Needs","Blind spot"],"highlightCol":1,"rows":[["Precision at k","Of the top k, how many are relevant?","Binary labels","Least stable standard measure. A perfect system scores at most 0.4 at k = 20 when only 8 chunks are relevant."],["Recall at k","How many of all relevant chunks reached the top k?","A complete list of relevant chunks per query","Ignores order inside the top k. Incomplete labels distort it."],["Hit rate at k","Did at least one relevant chunk reach the top k?","One known relevant chunk per query","Says nothing about the rest or the order. A conventional definition, not a named Foundry evaluator."],["MRR","How early did the first correct chunk appear?","One known correct chunk","Ignores everything ranked after the first hit."],["MAP","Average precision at each relevant rank, averaged over queries","Complete binary labels","Good discrimination and stability, but binary relevance only."],["nDCG at k","Are highly relevant chunks ranked above partly relevant ones?","Graded labels","Two gain formulas exist, so scores differ across tools."]]},{"t":"formula","title":"nDCG at k with two gain choices","expr":"DCG@k = sum over i = 1..k of gain(rel_i) / log2(i + 1)\nnDCG@k = DCG@k / IDCG@k","vars":[{"k":"gain, linear","v":"rel_i"},{"k":"gain, exponential","v":"2^rel_i - 1, common in industry and stresses highly relevant chunks"},{"k":"IDCG@k","v":"DCG@k of the ideal ordering, so a perfect ranking scores 1"}],"example":"Ranked relevance 3, 2, 0, 1. Linear gain gives **0.985**. Exponential gain gives **0.993**. Same ranking, different library, different number [[r46]]. MRR for first-relevant ranks 1, 3, none, 2 is (1 + 0.333 + 0 + 0.5) / 4 = **0.458**."},{"t":"dials","id":"w14-l3-metric","title":"Pick the retrieval metric","prompt":"Flip what you know about labels, evidence needs and judgment coverage. The ranking and the reasons update.","dials":[{"id":"labels","label":"Relevance labels","values":[{"id":"qrels","label":"Judged per query"},{"id":"none","label":"None, only questions"}]},{"id":"need","label":"Evidence the answer needs","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One chunk is enough"},{"id":"many","label":"Several chunks combine"}]},{"id":"cover","label":"Judgment coverage","values":[{"id":"complete","label":"Every retrieved chunk judged"},{"id":"holes","label":"Many chunks unjudged"}]}],"options":[{"id":"mrr","name":"MRR","summary":"Rank of the first correct chunk."},{"id":"hit","name":"Hit rate at k","summary":"Share of queries with a relevant chunk in the top k."},{"id":"recall","name":"Recall at k","summary":"Share of all relevant chunks found in the top k."},{"id":"ndcg","name":"nDCG at k","summary":"Graded relevance with a rank discount."},{"id":"ctx","name":"Judged context metrics","summary":"RAGAS context precision and recall, or the Foundry Retrieval evaluator."}],"scores":{"mrr":{"labels":{"qrels":{"s":2,"why":"It needs a known correct chunk per query, which qrels supply."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"Without a known correct chunk there is nothing to rank against."}},"need":{"one":{"s":2,"why":"It rewards the first correct chunk, which is all the answer needs."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"It ignores every chunk after the first hit."}},"cover":{"complete":{"s":1,"why":"The first judged-relevant rank is trustworthy."},"holes":{"s":-1,"why":"An unjudged but relevant chunk ranked first counts as a miss."}}},"hit":{"labels":{"qrels":{"s":1,"why":"One known relevant chunk per query is enough to score it."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"It still needs at least one known relevant chunk."}},"need":{"one":{"s":2,"why":"The question is whether a good chunk made it into the prompt."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"It says nothing about the other chunks the answer needs."}},"cover":{"complete":{"s":1,"why":"Hits are counted correctly."},"holes":{"s":0,"why":"Less sensitive, since only one judged hit is required."}}},"recall":{"labels":{"qrels":{"s":2,"why":"Counts judged relevant chunks that were found."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"The denominator is the list of relevant chunks, which you do not have."}},"need":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Overkill when a single chunk answers the question."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"It measures whether every needed chunk arrived."}},"cover":{"complete":{"s":1,"why":"The denominator is trustworthy."},"holes":{"s":-2,"why":"Unjudged relevant chunks are invisible, so the denominator is a partial list."}}},"ndcg":{"labels":{"qrels":{"s":1,"why":"Needs graded labels, which cost more than binary ones."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"There are no grades to discount."}},"need":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Fine, but MRR gives the same signal with fewer labels."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"It rewards ranking highly relevant chunks above partial ones."}},"cover":{"complete":{"s":1,"why":"Grades cover the ranking, so the discount is meaningful."},"holes":{"s":-1,"why":"Holes drag the score down. Check holes_ratio first."}}},"ctx":{"labels":{"qrels":{"s":0,"why":"Works, but rank metrics are cheaper and deterministic."},"none":{"s":2,"why":"A judge reads the retrieved chunks directly, so no qrels are needed."}},"need":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"Judged against the question."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Context recall checks reference claims against the retrieved chunks."}},"cover":{"complete":{"s":0,"why":"No advantage over rank metrics here."},"holes":{"s":2,"why":"It does not depend on pre-judged documents, only on judge quality."}}}},"takeaway":"Rank metrics are cheap and deterministic but need labels. Judged metrics need no labels but add judge noise. Say which you lack, then pick."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-l3-d1","title":"Score each half where it happens","alt":"A RAG pipeline from query to retriever, top-k chunks, generator and answer, with retrieval metrics attached to the chunks and groundedness and relevance attached to the answer and the chunks.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Query] --> R[Retriever]\n  R --> C[Top k chunks]\n  C --> G[Generator]\n  G --> A[Answer]\n  C -.-> E1[Rank and context metrics]\n  A -.-> E2[Groundedness and relevance]\n  C -.-> E2","caption":"Retrieval metrics read the chunks. Answer metrics read the answer against the chunks. Foundry labels groundedness the precision aspect and response completeness the recall aspect."},{"t":"table","caption":"Which failure each metric catches","head":["Failure","RAGAS metric","Foundry evaluator","Blind spot"],"rows":[["Needed facts never retrieved","Context recall (needs a reference). Context entities recall for fact-heavy domains.","Retrieval (LLM judge on context, no ground truth). Document Retrieval (needs relevance labels).","Recall ignores order, so a needed chunk can sit at rank 9."],["Relevant chunks ranked too low","Context precision, which is order sensitive","Document Retrieval: `ndcg@3`, `xdcg@3`, `top1_relevance`","Needs labels. Check `holes_ratio`."],["Answer claims lack support in the chunks","Faithfulness","Groundedness (1 to 5). Groundedness Pro (preview, True/False, no model deployment).","A wrong chunk produces a faithful wrong answer."],["Answer leaves out part of what the reference says","Compare with the reference answer","Response Completeness (preview, needs `ground_truth`)","Needs a reference answer for every row."],["Answer does not address the question","Answer relevancy (regenerated questions plus embeddings)","Relevance","Does not judge facts."],["Wrong claims traced to irrelevant retrieved chunks","Noise sensitivity (lower is better)","No named evaluator for it in the pages checked on 2026-10-13","Needs a reference and the retrieved contexts."]]},{"t":"failure","title":"Retrieval and RAG evaluation pitfalls","items":[{"symptom":"Recall at 10 is 0.92 and answers still miss facts","cause":"k is larger than the number of chunks the prompt receives","fix":"Set k to the number of chunks the generator sees, for example 4, and report recall at that k."},{"symptom":"A new index scores lower although the results look better","cause":"New documents have no relevance judgments, so they count as non-relevant","fix":"Track holes_ratio, judge the new top results and extend the qrels before comparing."},{"symptom":"Two libraries report different nDCG for one run","cause":"Linear gain versus 2^rel - 1 gain","fix":"Fix one definition in the harness and store it next to the score."},{"symptom":"A dense retriever tops a public benchmark and loses on exact identifiers","cause":"BEIR shows BM25 is a strong baseline, and your queries differ from the benchmark","fix":"Score on your own queries and keep identifier-heavy cases as their own slice."},{"symptom":"Answer relevancy shifts after an embedding change","cause":"The metric embeds the user input and regenerated questions, so the embedder changes the score","fix":"Pin the embedding model and the number of generated questions (default 3) in the harness."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: the Foundry Document Retrieval evaluator","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"`builtin.document_retrieval` is a composite evaluator. It returns Fidelity, NDCG, XDCG, Max Relevance N and Holes, with outputs such as `ndcg@3`, `xdcg@3`, `fidelity`, `top1_relevance`, `top3_max_relevance`, `holes` and `holes_ratio`. Inputs are `retrieval_ground_truth` (a `document_id` plus a `query_relevance_label`) and `retrieved_documents`. Holes count documents that were retrieved without a relevance judgment [[r4]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: RAGAS API and definitions","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"PyPI `ragas` was at 0.4.3 (released 2026-01-13) when checked. The docs show the collections API (`ragas.metrics.collections`, with `ascore` and `score`). The legacy `SingleTurnSample` API is deprecated in 0.4 and removed in 1.0. The docs title says Response Relevancy while the class is `AnswerRelevancy` [[r20]]."},{"t":"p","md":"`ContextPrecision` compares chunks with a reference answer, while `ContextUtilization` uses the generated response instead. Noise sensitivity takes `mode=\"relevant\"` (default) or `mode=\"irrelevant\"` [[r20]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: what BEIR says about retrievers","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"BEIR bundles 18 public datasets for zero-shot retrieval. BM25 is a strong baseline. Re-ranking and late-interaction models score best on average but at high compute cost, so a benchmark win is not a latency or cost win [[r42]]. The DeepLearning.AI course on advanced RAG shows the same metrics applied end to end [[r14]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-l3-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":1,"ref":"r25","q":"A retriever is scored on four queries. The first relevant chunk appears at ranks 1, 3, never and 2. What is the MRR? Give the answer to three decimals.","answer":0.458,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"MRR","hint":"Reciprocal rank is 1 divided by the rank of the first relevant chunk, and 0 when none is retrieved.","explain":"Reciprocal ranks are 1, 1/3, 0 and 1/2. Their sum is 1.833 and the mean over four queries is 0.458. Treating the missing query as rank infinity gives the 0, and dropping it would wrongly average over three queries and give 0.611."},{"id":"w14-l3-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"ref":"r4","q":"Which cause best explains these numbers?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"recall@10          = 0.92\nchunks_in_prompt   = 4\nanswer_completeness = low"},"options":["The embedding model is too small to represent these queries well and needs replacing","The judge is biased toward long answers, so it under-scores the shorter answers it receives","The chunk overlap is too low, so facts are split across neighbouring chunks","k is 10 but the prompt receives 4 chunks, so ranks 5 to 10 never reach the generator"],"answer":3,"explain":"Recall at 10 counts chunks the generator never sees. Score recall at the k the prompt actually uses. A weak embedder would also lower recall at 10, which here is high. Judge length bias affects answer scoring, not the retrieval number. Low overlap could split facts, but the high recall at 10 shows the relevant chunks were found."},{"id":"w14-l3-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"ref":"r25","q":"Questions need three chunks combined, and experts graded every chunk from 0 to 3. Which metric is the best primary retrieval score?","options":["MRR, because it rewards the earliest correct chunk in every ranked list","Hit rate at 1, because it is the strictest check on the top result","nDCG at k, where k is the number of chunks in the prompt","Precision at 20, because it is the simplest metric to explain to stakeholders"],"answer":2,"explain":"Graded labels and multi-chunk evidence point to nDCG at the k the prompt uses, because it rewards ranking highly relevant chunks above partial ones. MRR and hit rate at 1 look only at the first correct chunk and ignore the others the answer needs. Precision at 20 is unstable when few chunks are relevant, and it ignores grades and order."},{"id":"w14-l3-q4","type":"single","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"ref":"r46","q":"Two libraries report nDCG@4 of 0.985 and 0.993 for the same ranking with relevance 3, 2, 0, 1. Which explanation fits?","options":["One uses linear gain and the other uses 2^rel - 1, which weights highly relevant chunks more","One library accidentally leaks the ideal ordering into the predicted ranking, which inflates its score","One of them computes MRR because the labels are graded","Rounding error at the fourth decimal place"],"answer":0,"explain":"Linear gain gives DCG 4.693 against an ideal 4.762, so 0.985. Exponential gain uses 7, 3, 0 and 1, giving 9.324 against 9.393, so 0.993. The two coincide for binary labels and diverge for graded ones. Leaking the ideal order would give 1.0, MRR is a different metric with a different range, and rounding cannot move the result by 0.008."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r4","r14","r20","r25","r42","r46"],"takeaways":["Pick the retrieval metric by your labels: rank metrics need qrels, judged context metrics do not.","Tie k to the number of chunks the prompt actually receives.","Score retrieval and generation separately. Faithfulness checks support, not truth.","Fix one nDCG definition and track holes_ratio before comparing indexes.","Public retrieval benchmarks say little about your identifiers and paraphrases, so test your own queries."]},{"id":"w14-l4","order":4,"title":"Judge the judge","kind":"problem","minutes":18,"brief":"Your scorer is a model, and models have habits. The decision you are making, the availability of reference answers and the cost of a wrong verdict are unspecified. Set up the judge so that a change in score means something.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"A judge is an instrument with measurable habits","level":2,"id":"a-judge-is-an-instrument-with-measurable-habits"},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Agreement with humans","value":"over 80%","note":"GPT-4 reached 85% against 81% among humans in the non-tie setup. Measured on 2023 models."},{"label":"Consistency after swapping order","value":"65.0%, 46.2%, 23.8%","note":"GPT-4, GPT-3.5 and Claude-v1 with the default prompt. Newer judges are likely better, but no measurement was found on 2026-10-13."},{"label":"Math grading failures, out of 20","value":"14, then 6, then 3","note":"Default judge, then chain-of-thought, then a reference-guided judge"},{"label":"Order flipped a ranking","value":"66 of 80","note":"Vicuna-13B beat ChatGPT on 66 of 80 queries once answer order changed, with ChatGPT as judge"},{"label":"Few-shot judge","value":"65.0% to 77.5%","note":"GPT-4 consistency gain, at about 4 times the prompt cost"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Judge formats and where each is exposed [[r21]] [[r18]] [[r16]]","head":["Format","Strength","Exposed to","Cost"],"highlightCol":1,"rows":[["Pointwise 1 to 5 score","Cheap, one call per item","Scale drift and compression, verbosity bias, uncalibrated scales","1 call"],["Pairwise comparison","More reliable than absolute scores when ranking two systems","Position bias, fixed by swapping order","2 calls per pair with swap"],["Reference-guided grading","Checkable tasks: math failures fell from 14 to 3 of 20","Needs a reference for every item","1 call, longer prompt"],["Binary pass/fail with critique, calibrated","OpenAI guidance prefers pairwise or pass/fail to fine-grained scores. Husain calibrates with one expert's critiques.","Needs an accountable expert and a labelled sample","1 call plus calibration time"],["Panel of judges from different families","Dilutes self-preference, and disagreement flags items for review","Multiplies calls and still shares some biases","n calls"]]},{"t":"dials","id":"w14-l4-judge","title":"Pick the judge setup","prompt":"Flip the decision, the reference and the stakes. The ranking shows which setup carries the least risk.","dials":[{"id":"decision","label":"Decision","values":[{"id":"rank","label":"Rank two systems"},{"id":"gate","label":"Gate one system"}]},{"id":"ref","label":"Reference answers","values":[{"id":"yes","label":"Exist"},{"id":"no","label":"Do not exist"}]},{"id":"stakes","label":"Cost of a wrong verdict","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Low, budget matters"},{"id":"high","label":"High"}]}],"options":[{"id":"point","name":"Pointwise 1 to 5","summary":"One absolute score per answer."},{"id":"pair","name":"Pairwise with order swap","summary":"Compare two answers in both orders."},{"id":"refjudge","name":"Reference-guided pass/fail","summary":"Grade against a known correct answer."},{"id":"calib","name":"Calibrated binary judge","summary":"Pass/fail tuned to an expert's critiques."},{"id":"panel","name":"Panel from different families","summary":"Several judges, disagreement flagged."}],"scores":{"point":{"decision":{"rank":{"s":-1,"why":"Absolute scores drift and compress, so close systems tie."},"gate":{"s":0,"why":"Workable with a threshold, though unanchored 1 to 5 scales are noisy."}},"ref":{"yes":{"s":-1,"why":"A reference is available and this format does not use it."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"Works with no reference, which is why it is the default."}},"stakes":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Cheapest: one call per item."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"Uncalibrated scores are risky when verdicts matter."}}},"pair":{"decision":{"rank":{"s":2,"why":"Pairwise is more reliable for ranking, and swapping removes position bias."},"gate":{"s":-1,"why":"It needs a comparison partner, not a threshold."}},"ref":{"yes":{"s":0,"why":"References can be added, but are not required."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"Compares two answers, so no reference is needed."}},"stakes":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Swapping doubles the calls."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Counting only wins that hold in both orders is conservative."}}},"refjudge":{"decision":{"rank":{"s":0,"why":"Works, though it scores each system alone."},"gate":{"s":2,"why":"Pass/fail against a reference maps cleanly to a threshold."}},"ref":{"yes":{"s":2,"why":"Reference-guided grading cut math failures from 14 to 3 of 20."},"no":{"s":-2,"why":"Nothing to guide the judge with."}},"stakes":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Longer prompts cost a little more."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Strong on verifiable tasks, where errors are checkable."}}},"calib":{"decision":{"rank":{"s":0,"why":"Binary verdicts lose fine ordering, though win counts still rank."},"gate":{"s":2,"why":"Pass/fail with a threshold and written critiques is the OpenAI and Husain advice."}},"ref":{"yes":{"s":0,"why":"A reference is optional here."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"Anchored by expert critiques instead of references."}},"stakes":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"Calibration needs expert time that low stakes do not repay."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Measured true positive and true negative rates are what high stakes need."}}},"panel":{"decision":{"rank":{"s":1,"why":"Averaging across families dilutes self-preference."},"gate":{"s":0,"why":"Needs a rule for combining verdicts into one threshold."}},"ref":{"yes":{"s":0,"why":"Each judge can use the reference, with no special gain."},"no":{"s":1,"why":"Different families cross-check each other when nothing else anchors them."}},"stakes":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Several calls on every item."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Disagreement between judges flags items for human review."}}}},"takeaway":"Rank with pairwise, gate with calibrated pass/fail, and use references wherever the answer is checkable."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-l4-d1","title":"The judge calibration loop","alt":"An expert labels a sample, a judge prompt is drafted and run, true positive and true negative rates are computed, disagreements drive prompt refinement until agreement is acceptable, then the judge is frozen and re-checked on a fresh human sample.","src":"flowchart TB\n  L[\"Expert labels a sample\"] --> D[\"Draft judge prompt\"]\n  D --> R[\"Run judge on the sample\"]\n  R --> M[\"Compute TPR and TNR\"]\n  M --> Q{\"Agree enough?\"}\n  Q -->|No| X[\"Read disagreements, refine prompt\"]\n  X --> R\n  Q -->|Yes| F[\"Freeze judge version\"]\n  F --> W[\"Re-check on a fresh sample\"]","caption":"Husain's critique shadowing reached over 90% agreement in three iterations on his example. Report TPR and TNR, not raw agreement, when classes are imbalanced."},{"t":"failure","title":"Judge failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The pairwise winner flips when the answer slots swap","cause":"Position bias: most judges favour the first slot","fix":"Run both orders and count a win only when it holds both ways. Report the share of inconsistent pairs."},{"symptom":"The longest answer keeps winning","cause":"Verbosity bias. In the MT-Bench repetitive-list attack, Claude-v1 and GPT-3.5 failed 91.3% of the time and GPT-4 8.7%.","fix":"Control for length, penalize padding in the rubric and test the judge with padded answers."},{"symptom":"Your own model scores high on a judge from its own family","cause":"Self-preference: judges recognize their own outputs and favour them","fix":"Use a different family from the generator or a panel, and spot check against a human sample."},{"symptom":"Agreement is 92% and the judge never flags failures","cause":"Imbalanced labels, so raw agreement hides a low true negative rate","fix":"Report TPR and TNR, and keep failure examples in the calibration sample."},{"symptom":"A move from 4.2 to 4.4 is celebrated","cause":"No interval, and an uncalibrated 1 to 5 scale","fix":"Use binary criteria with a threshold, a paired comparison and a confidence interval before acting."},{"symptom":"Scores shift after the judge model is upgraded","cause":"A different model brings different habits","fix":"Pin the judge version, recalibrate against the human sample and rerun the baseline whenever the judge changes."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: G-Eval and rubric judges","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"G-Eval combines chain-of-thought with a form-filling prompt that ends in a score. With GPT-4 it reached a Spearman correlation of 0.514 with humans on summarization, and its authors flag a bias toward LLM-generated text. DeepEval implements it as `GEval` [[r22]] [[r28]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: more than three biases","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"The CALM framework quantifies 12 biases in LLM judges, not only position and verbosity [[r44]]. MT-Bench found GPT-4 favoured itself with about a 10% higher win rate and Claude-v1 about 25%, but called the evidence inconclusive [[r21]]. A later study found judges can recognize their own outputs, and self-recognition correlated linearly with self-preference after fine-tuning [[r32]]. Fixes proposed for order effects include Multiple Evidence Calibration, Balanced Position Calibration and human-in-the-loop calibration [[r31]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: judge cost arithmetic and custom judges","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Swapping order doubles the calls, and a few-shot judge costs about 4 times the prompt tokens. OpenAI advises validating agreement against human labels before optimizing cost or latency, asking the judge to reason before it scores, and preferring pairwise comparison or pass/fail for more reliability [[r18]]."},{"t":"p","md":"In Foundry, `builtin.output_quality` and `builtin.tool_use_quality` batch several criteria into one judge call (preview, 2026-10-13) [[r3]]. A prompt-based custom evaluator (preview) returns JSON with a `result` and a `reason`, which lets you ship a calibrated judge as a first-class evaluator [[r41]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-l4-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":2,"ref":"r21","q":"The stored answers are identical in both runs. Only the slot order changed. Which cause best fits?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"run 1: prompt B in slot 1 -> B wins 68% of 200 comparisons\nrun 2: prompt B in slot 2 -> B wins 49% of 200 comparisons"},"options":["Position bias: the judge favours whichever answer sits in slot 1","Verbosity bias: prompt B writes longer answers than prompt A","Self-preference: the judge shares a model family with the generator","Sampling temperature in the generator changed between the two runs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only the slot moved, and B's win rate fell from 68% to about chance, so the slot drove the verdict. Verbosity bias and self-preference follow the answer, so B would keep winning after the swap. The generator is not involved because the stored answers were re-judged unchanged. The swap-and-agree rule exists to catch exactly this."},{"id":"w14-l4-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16","q":"Select the mitigations that research or vendor guidance supports for judge reliability.","options":["Run each pair in both orders and count a win only when it holds in both","Give the judge a reference answer on tasks with a checkable solution","Calibrate against pass/fail labels and critiques from one accountable domain expert","Use a judge from the same model family as the generator so that style matches"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Swapping order addresses position bias, and a reference-guided judge cut math failures from 14 to 3 of 20 [[r21]]. Calibrating to one expert's critiques is the critique shadowing method [[r16]]. The fourth option backfires: a judge that recognizes its own family's style favours it, which is self-preference [[r32]]."},{"id":"w14-l4-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16","q":"A judge is checked on 100 items. 90 are truly passing and 10 are truly failing. It labels 88 of the 90 passing items correctly and 3 of the 10 failing items correctly. Treating failing as the negative class, what is its true negative rate, in percent?","answer":30,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"percent","hint":"True negative rate = failing items the judge flagged divided by all truly failing items.","explain":"The judge flags 3 of 10 true failures, so the true negative rate is 30%. Raw agreement is (88 + 3) / 100 = 91%, which looks healthy while 7 of 10 real failures slip through. This is why imbalanced classes need TPR and TNR."},{"id":"w14-l4-q4","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":3,"ref":"r18","q":"You must gate one prompt change against a threshold. A domain expert has one day to give labels and there are no reference answers. Which judge setup fits best?","options":["A pointwise 1 to 10 score averaged over the whole set and compared with the last release's average","A pairwise judge comparing the new prompt with the old one, always in the same fixed order, as the gate","A binary pass/fail judge calibrated to the expert's labelled critiques, with a stated threshold","Two pointwise judges from different families whose fine-grained scores are averaged into one gate value"],"answer":2,"explain":"Gating one system favours pass/fail with a threshold, and the expert's critiques are the input that calibrates it [[r16]] [[r18]]. Averaged 1 to 10 scores are the uncalibrated scale Husain warns against. A fixed-order pairwise judge is exposed to position bias and compares rather than gates. Averaging two fine-grained scores reduces noise a little but leaves the scale uncalibrated."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r3","r16","r18","r21","r22","r28","r31","r32","r41","r44"],"takeaways":["A judge is a model with measurable habits: position, verbosity and self-preference bias.","Swap order, use references where answers are checkable, and calibrate against one accountable expert.","Report true positive and true negative rates, not raw agreement.","Rank two systems with pairwise comparison. Gate one system with calibrated pass/fail.","Pin the judge version and recalibrate when it changes."]},{"id":"w14-l5","order":5,"title":"Grade the agent, then trust the number","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"An agent can reach the right result by several tool sequences, and it can fail on the second attempt of a task it passed the first time. The side effects of its tools, your need to localize failures and the size of your suite are unspecified. Decide what to grade and how many times to run it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Two questions: what to grade, how often to run","level":2,"id":"two-questions-what-to-grade-how-often-to-run"},{"t":"p","md":"An agent run is a path through tools, not one answer. Anthropic's guidance is to grade the **outcome**, meaning the end state of the environment, and the **transcript** where it helps, using code-based, model-based or human graders [[r17]]. tau-bench grades by comparing the final database state with an annotated goal state, and it defines pass^k as the chance that all k independent trials succeed [[r23]].\n\nGrading answers what you measure. Repetition and statistics answer whether the measurement can be trusted. Most weak agent evals fail on the second question."},{"t":"table","caption":"Foundry agent evaluators, preview unless noted, checked 2026-10-13 [[r3]]","head":["Level","Evaluators","Detail","Use it to"],"rows":[["System","Task Completion, Task Adherence, Intent Resolution","Preview. Intent Resolution is scored 1 to 5 against a threshold.","Judge the whole run"],["System","Customer Satisfaction","Preview. Six dimensions, each 1 to 5.","Estimate how the conversation felt"],["System","Task Navigation Efficiency","Needs a ground truth tool sequence.","Compare with one known best path, only fair when one path is truly right"],["Process","Tool Call Accuracy","Scored 1 to 5 against a threshold.","Check that calls match the request"],["Process","Tool Selection, Tool Input Accuracy, Tool Output Utilization, Tool Call Success","Tool Input Accuracy runs six strict checks.","Point a failure at one step"]]},{"t":"formula","title":"pass@k and pass^k from n trials","expr":"pass^k = average over tasks of C(c, k) / C(n, k)\npass@k = 1 - average over tasks of C(n - c, k) / C(n, k)","vars":[{"k":"n","v":"trials run per task"},{"k":"c","v":"trials that succeeded"},{"k":"k","v":"attempts a user would make: all must succeed for pass^k, any one for pass@k"}],"example":"With n = 5, c = 4 and k = 3, pass^3 = 4 / 10 = **0.4** and pass@3 = **1**. If every trial succeeds with the same probability p, pass^k is p^k: at p = 0.90 that is 0.729 for k = 3, 0.590 for k = 5 and 0.430 for k = 8. At p = 0.75 it is 0.422, 0.237 and 0.100. Real tasks vary in difficulty, so measured values differ. In 2024, gpt-4o with function calling scored about 61% retail and 35% airline pass^1 on tau-bench, and about 25% retail pass^8 [[r23]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"n = 50","value":"plus or minus 11.1","note":"95% interval half-width in points at an 80% pass rate. Computed estimate."},{"label":"n = 100","value":"plus or minus 7.8","note":"1.96 x sqrt(0.8 x 0.2 / 100)"},{"label":"n = 200","value":"plus or minus 5.5","note":"Computed estimate"},{"label":"n = 400","value":"plus or minus 3.9","note":"Computed estimate"},{"label":"n = 1,000","value":"plus or minus 2.5","note":"Computed estimate. Clustered questions can widen this by over 3 times."}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w14-l5-grade","title":"Pick how to grade the agent","prompt":"Flip the task shape, the side effects and the debugging need. The ranking shows which grader fits.","dials":[{"id":"paths","label":"Valid solution paths","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One correct path"},{"id":"many","label":"Many valid paths"}]},{"id":"effects","label":"Tool side effects","values":[{"id":"none","label":"Read-only tools"},{"id":"real","label":"Tools change state"}]},{"id":"need","label":"What you need from the eval","values":[{"id":"gate","label":"A release verdict"},{"id":"locate","label":"Which step failed"}]}],"options":[{"id":"outcome","name":"Outcome check","summary":"Grade the end state of the environment."},{"id":"exact","name":"Exact trajectory match","summary":"Compare tool calls with an expected sequence."},{"id":"process","name":"Process evaluators","summary":"Tool selection, input accuracy and output use."},{"id":"transcript","name":"Transcript judge","summary":"A rubric over the full conversation."}],"scores":{"outcome":{"paths":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"Works, though it does not use the known path."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Any valid path passes, so alternatives are not punished."}},"effects":{"none":{"s":0,"why":"The outcome is the final answer, which a judge or code can check."},"real":{"s":2,"why":"The resulting state is ground truth, like the database state in tau-bench."}},"need":{"gate":{"s":2,"why":"A clean pass or fail is what a gate needs."},"locate":{"s":-1,"why":"It says the run failed, not where."}}},"exact":{"paths":{"one":{"s":2,"why":"Fine when a single sequence is truly correct."},"many":{"s":-2,"why":"Punishes valid alternatives, so good runs are marked wrong."}},"effects":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"Deterministic and cheap to run."},"real":{"s":0,"why":"Checks the calls, not what they changed."}},"need":{"gate":{"s":0,"why":"Brittle as a gate, because harmless variation fails it."},"locate":{"s":1,"why":"Shows the first step that diverged."}}},"process":{"paths":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"Tool checks line up with the one correct path."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"Strict input checks can fire on valid variants."}},"effects":{"none":{"s":0,"why":"Useful, but errors cost little."},"real":{"s":1,"why":"Catches wrong arguments before they change state."}},"need":{"gate":{"s":0,"why":"More signals than a gate needs."},"locate":{"s":2,"why":"Points at the step: wrong tool, bad input or ignored output."}}},"transcript":{"paths":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"No particular gain."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"A rubric judges reasoning on any path, at the cost of judge calls."}},"effects":{"none":{"s":1,"why":"Good for text-only agents."},"real":{"s":-1,"why":"It reads claims about actions instead of checking state."}},"need":{"gate":{"s":0,"why":"Needs calibration like any judge."},"locate":{"s":1,"why":"A rubric can name where the plan went wrong."}}}},"takeaway":"Grade outcomes when paths vary or tools change state, and add process checks only where you need to localize a failure."},{"t":"failure","title":"Agent evaluation failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The agent passes in the lab and fails for customers","cause":"An average success rate hides inconsistency, and pass^k decays as k grows","fix":"Run n trials per task and report pass^k for the number of attempts a customer makes."},{"symptom":"Pass rates look too stable, and failures cluster by task","cause":"Trials share state such as files or database rows left behind","fix":"Isolate each trial in a clean environment."},{"symptom":"A correct run is marked wrong","cause":"A rigid grader. A model found a policy loophole on a tau2-bench flight task and was marked wrong.","fix":"Grade the end state, read a sample of failed transcripts, and fix the grader before blaming the agent."},{"symptom":"tool_call_accuracy looks unreliable on some conversations","cause":"Azure AI Search, Bing Grounding, Bing Custom Search, SharePoint Grounding, Code Interpreter, Fabric Data Agent and Web Search have limited evaluator support","fix":"Avoid tool_call_accuracy, tool_input_accuracy, tool_output_utilization, tool_call_success and groundedness for those conversations, and use outcome checks."},{"symptom":"Intervals look tight but grouped questions share a source","cause":"Clustered questions are not independent, and clustered standard errors can exceed 3 times the naive ones","fix":"Use clustered standard errors and paired question-level differences when comparing two models."},{"symptom":"A 2-point gain on 100 tasks gets shipped","cause":"The interval half-width is about 7.8 points at an 80% pass rate","fix":"Run a power analysis, enlarge the set or pair the runs before treating a small gain as real."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: evaluation levels and composite evaluators","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"`evaluation_level` is `turn` (the default, scoring each response) or `conversation` (the whole multi-turn exchange). The conversation level needs the `messages` data format and skips turn-only evaluators [[r2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"`builtin.output_quality` batches Fluency, Coherence, Intent Resolution, Task Adherence, Groundedness and Task Completion into one judge call. `builtin.tool_use_quality` batches Tool Call Accuracy, Tool Call Success, Tool Input Accuracy, Tool Output Utilization and Tool Selection. Default pass thresholds are 3 on 1 to 5 scales and 1 on 0/1 scales. The primary result passes only if every applicable component passes. Both are preview as of 2026-10-13 [[r3]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: hooks for Agent Framework and open-source suites","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Agent Framework exposes `EvalItem`, `Evaluator` and `EvalResults`, plus Python `evaluate_agent()`, `evaluate_workflow()` and a `LocalEvaluator` with checks such as `keyword_check` and `tool_called_check`. `FoundryEvals` defaults to relevance, coherence and task adherence, and adds tool call accuracy when tool definitions exist [[r10]]."},{"t":"p","md":"DeepEval ships trajectory metrics (Task Completion, Step Efficiency, Plan Adherence, Plan Quality) and component metrics (Tool Correctness, Argument Correctness). Most of its metrics are LLM-judged, so the calibration rules from the previous level apply [[r28]]. The DeepLearning.AI course covers the evaluation loop end to end [[r13]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: benchmark facts worth knowing","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"tau-bench has 115 retail and 50 airline tasks and grades the final database state [[r23]]. The repository is `sierra-research/tau2-bench`, and its README brands the project tau3-bench as of 2026-10-13. Version 1.0.1 (July 2026) fixed task errors in `banking_knowledge`, so earlier results on that domain are not comparable, and it needs Python 3.12 or 3.13 and `uv` [[r35]]. GAIA has 466 questions in three levels. At publication humans scored 92% and GPT-4 with plugins 15% [[r33]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-l5-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"ref":"r23","q":"An agent is run 5 times on a task and succeeds 4 times. Using the unbiased estimator, what is pass^3 for this task?","answer":0.4,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"probability","hint":"pass^k is C(c, k) / C(n, k).","explain":"C(4, 3) is 4 and C(5, 3) is 10, so pass^3 = 0.4. pass@3 for the same data is 1 - C(1, 3) / C(5, 3) = 1, because any 3 trials must include a success. The gap between 1 and 0.4 is why the choice of pass@k or pass^k depends on whether one success is enough."},{"id":"w14-l5-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17","q":"Several tool sequences can solve the task, and the tools change state in an external system. Which grading is best?","options":["Match the exact expected tool call sequence, because any deviation from the reference path signals a defect","Grade the end state of the environment in clean sandboxes, and add selected process checks","Ask a judge to rate the final message only, because that message is all the user ever reads and acts on","Count tool calls and prefer runs with fewer calls, because fewer calls always means better planning"],"answer":1,"explain":"With several valid paths and real side effects, the end state is the ground truth, and clean sandboxes stop trials from contaminating each other [[r17]]. An exact sequence punishes valid alternatives. A judge on the final message can accept a claim about an action that never happened. Fewer calls can mean skipped checks, so call count is not quality."},{"id":"w14-l5-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":2,"ref":"r24","q":"A suite of 100 independent tasks shows an 80% pass rate. What is the half-width of the 95% confidence interval, in percentage points?","answer":7.8,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"points","hint":"1.96 x sqrt(p x (1 - p) / n)","explain":"The standard error is sqrt(0.8 x 0.2 / 100) = 0.04, and 1.96 x 0.04 = 0.0784, so about 7.8 points. A true rate anywhere from about 72% to 88% fits the data, which is why a 2-point gain on 100 tasks is noise. If the questions are clustered, the real interval is wider."},{"id":"w14-l5-q4","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":3,"ref":"r3","q":"What is the most likely cause of these scores?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"tools in conversation: bing_grounding, azure_ai_search\ntool_call_accuracy:    2.0  (fail)\ntool_input_accuracy:   1.0  (fail)\nmodel or prompt change since last run: none"},"options":["The agent regressed because the underlying model changed between the two runs of this conversation","The judge deployment is too small to score tool calls, so a larger deployment should be selected","The pass threshold of 3 is too strict for 1 to 5 scales, so it should be lowered for these runs","Those tools have limited evaluator support, so tool-level scores are unreliable there"],"answer":3,"explain":"The docs list Azure AI Search, Bing Grounding, Bing Custom Search, SharePoint Grounding, Code Interpreter, Fabric Data Agent and Web Search as limited support, and advise avoiding tool_call_accuracy, tool_input_accuracy, tool_output_utilization, tool_call_success and groundedness for them [[r3]]. Nothing changed in the model or prompt, a bigger judge does not fix unsupported tool formats, and lowering the threshold hides the signal instead of explaining it."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r2","r3","r10","r13","r17","r23","r24","r28","r33","r35"],"takeaways":["Grade outcomes first. Exact trajectories punish valid alternatives.","Add process evaluators so a failure points at a step, and know which tools they do not cover.","Report pass^k for reliability and pass@k for capability, from n isolated trials per task.","A pass rate without an interval is a guess. At n = 100 and 80%, the half-width is 7.8 points.","Use clustered errors and paired comparisons when questions share a source."]},{"id":"w14-l6","order":6,"title":"Gate it, attack it, watch it","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"A change is ready to ship. The harm if it is worse, the traffic you have and the quality signal you trust are all unspecified. Design the gate that blocks it, the attack that probes it and the live watch that follows it.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"A gate is a rule plus a noise budget","level":2,"id":"a-gate-is-a-rule-plus-a-noise-budget"},{"t":"p","md":"A CI gate needs a rule and a noise budget. The rule is a fixed threshold or a baseline comparison with a significance test. Foundry's compare view reports Improved or Degraded as Strong when p <= 0.001 and Weak when 0.001 < p <= 0.05 against a baseline run [[r39]]. The preview GitHub Action `microsoft/ai-agent-evals` evaluates Foundry agents given as `name:version` against an optional `baseline-agent-id`, and its output includes confidence intervals and a significance test [[r9]].\n\nThe noise budget comes from suite size and trials. Block on safety and regression suites, keep capability suites advisory, and run a small suite on every pull request with a large one nightly."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-l6-d1","title":"From pull request to production and back","alt":"A pull request runs a fast subset, compares with a baseline, and passes through a gate that blocks or merges. After merge a nightly full suite runs, then online sampling, and failing traces return to the golden set.","src":"flowchart LR\n  PR[Pull request] --> F[Fast subset]\n  F --> C[Compare to baseline]\n  C --> G{Gate}\n  G -->|safety or regression fails| B[Block merge]\n  G -->|pass| M[Merge]\n  M --> N[Nightly full suite]\n  N --> O[Online sampling]\n  O --> T[Failing traces to golden set]\n  T --> F","caption":"Foundry evaluations are preview in several places, so keep a code-first harness such as pytest-style or promptfoo runs as the fallback."},{"t":"dials","id":"w14-l6-online","title":"Pick the online method","prompt":"Flip the harm, the traffic and the signal you trust. The ranking shows which online method fits.","dials":[{"id":"harm","label":"If the new version is worse","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Users barely notice"},{"id":"high","label":"Users are harmed"}]},{"id":"traffic","label":"Traffic volume","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Low"},{"id":"high","label":"High"}]},{"id":"signal","label":"Live quality signal","values":[{"id":"none","label":"None trusted yet"},{"id":"trusted","label":"Calibrated judge or business metric"}]}],"options":[{"id":"shadow","name":"Shadow traffic","summary":"Mirror requests to the candidate, show users nothing."},{"id":"canary","name":"Canary rollout","summary":"Send a small share of users to the candidate."},{"id":"ab","name":"A/B test","summary":"Split users between versions and compare a metric."},{"id":"cont","name":"Continuous sampled evaluation","summary":"Score a sample of live traces."},{"id":"feedback","name":"User feedback triage","summary":"Use ratings, edits, retries and escalations."}],"scores":{"shadow":{"harm":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"A direct rollout may be acceptable, so the extra safety buys little."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Users never see the candidate's answers."}},"traffic":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Mirroring all requests is cheap at low volume."},"high":{"s":-1,"why":"Duplicate inference adds up. Azure ML mirroring caps mirrored traffic at 50%."}},"signal":{"none":{"s":0,"why":"You can still read paired outputs, but reading is slow."},"trusted":{"s":2,"why":"Score both versions on the same requests and compare them paired."}}},"canary":{"harm":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Limited exposure is acceptable."},"high":{"s":-1,"why":"Real users see a possibly worse version."}},"traffic":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"A small slice yields too few samples."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Even a small percentage gives many samples."}},"signal":{"none":{"s":-1,"why":"Without a trusted metric you cannot tell whether the canary is worse."},"trusted":{"s":2,"why":"Watch the metric and roll back on a drop."}}},"ab":{"harm":{"low":{"s":2,"why":"The cleanest causal read when exposure is acceptable."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"Half the users may get the worse version."}},"traffic":{"low":{"s":-2,"why":"Detecting a small gap needs about a thousand samples per arm."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Enough users to detect small differences."}},"signal":{"none":{"s":-1,"why":"It needs a primary metric such as task success or a business outcome."},"trusted":{"s":2,"why":"A trusted primary metric is exactly what it tests."}}},"cont":{"harm":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Monitors without changing behaviour."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"A safety net after release, though not a pre-release guard."}},"traffic":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Low volume lets you score most traces."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Sampling rate and run caps bound coverage, 100 runs per hour by default in Foundry."}},"signal":{"none":{"s":-1,"why":"Reference-free judges need calibration before you trust them."},"trusted":{"s":2,"why":"Scores every sampled trace with no reference answers."}}},"feedback":{"harm":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"A cheap signal for triage."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"It arrives after the harm happened."}},"traffic":{"low":{"s":-1,"why":"Few voters, and a vocal minority dominates."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"More voters smooth the bias a little."}},"signal":{"none":{"s":2,"why":"Works when you have nothing else. Treat it as triage, not a metric."},"trusted":{"s":0,"why":"Biased toward vocal users, so use it to find new golden cases."}}}},"takeaway":"Shadow first when a worse version could harm users, A/B when exposure is fine and traffic is large, and sampled scoring always, once the judge is calibrated."},{"t":"compare","title":"Red teaming approaches","intro":"Microsoft's guidance is to combine automated scans with expert human analysis. The Attack Success Rate is scored by a generative model, so a person reviews findings before anyone mitigates.","options":[{"name":"Local PyRIT scan","tag":"Preview","summary":"`azure-ai-evaluation[redteam]` with `RedTeam(...)` and `await scan(target=...)`, run from your own code [[r6]]. The engine underneath is PyRIT [[r26]].","pros":["Scriptable and repeatable in CI","Attack strategies grouped by complexity: EASY (Base64, Flip, Morse), MODERATE (Tense), DIFFICULT (Tense composed with Base64)"],"cons":["Text-only risk categories, synthetic data, mock tools that return data only","Preview, and not compatible with the new Foundry portal and SDK","Python 3.10 to 3.13 only"],"bestWhen":"You want repeatable scans of a model or an app callback early, in a purple environment (non-production with production-like resources).","avoidWhen":"You need agentic risk categories, or you work on the new Foundry portal path.","meta":[{"k":"num_objectives","v":"10 by default"},{"k":"Example size","v":"4 categories x 1 objective x 4 strategies = 20 pairs"}]},{"name":"Cloud red teaming of Foundry agents","tag":"Preview","summary":"Adds agentic categories: Prohibited actions, Sensitive data leakage, Task adherence and indirect prompt injection (XPIA).","pros":["Targets agent-specific risks","Can be scheduled as part of post-production monitoring"],"cons":["Only Foundry prompt agents and hosted container agents with Azure tool calls are supported","Not supported: workflow agents, non-Foundry agents, function tool calls, browser automation, connected agents, computer use","Sensitive data leakage is single turn, English only and synthetic","Two Learn pages list different regions"],"bestWhen":"Your agent is a Foundry prompt or hosted agent that uses Azure tools.","avoidWhen":"Your agent relies on function tool calls, workflows or browser automation.","meta":[{"k":"SDK","v":"azure-ai-projects 2.0.0 or later"},{"k":"REST","v":"api-version 2025-11-15-preview"}]},{"name":"Manual expert red team","summary":"People with domain and security knowledge probe the system with creative, contextual attacks.","pros":["Finds attacks no template contains","Judges real business harm"],"cons":["Slow and costly","Hard to repeat release after release"],"bestWhen":"Pre-launch for high-risk systems, and to review automated findings.","avoidWhen":"It is the only recurring control.","meta":[{"k":"Cost","v":"high per finding"}]},{"name":"Public suites: HarmBench and AgentDojo","summary":"HarmBench compares 18 red-teaming methods on 33 target LLMs and defenses [[r43]]. AgentDojo has 97 tasks and 629 security test cases for prompt injection against tool-using agents [[r47]].","pros":["Standardized and comparable across systems","AgentDojo targets injection through tool data"],"cons":["Public attacks are known and can be trained against","They do not cover your tools and data"],"bestWhen":"You compare models or defenses.","avoidWhen":"They stand in for tests on your own tools and documents.","meta":[{"k":"Use","v":"benchmarking, not sign-off"}]}]},{"t":"formula","title":"Cost of one evaluation run","expr":"judge_calls = items x evaluators x trials\njudge_tokens = judge_calls x tokens_per_call","vars":[{"k":"items","v":"rows in the dataset"},{"k":"evaluators","v":"criteria scored by separate judge calls"},{"k":"trials","v":"repeats per item for pass^k or noise control"}],"example":"300 items x 4 evaluators x 3 trials = **3,600** judge calls. At about 2,500 tokens each that is about **9 million** tokens per run. This is an illustrative estimate, not a vendor formula. Foundry's target cost view excludes evaluator model usage, so budget the judge separately [[r39]]."},{"t":"failure","title":"Gate, red team and online failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"The same commit passes, then fails","cause":"Non-determinism with too few items or trials, and thresholds that sit inside the noise","fix":"Compare with a baseline using a confidence interval and a significance test, add trials, and enlarge the set before tightening thresholds."},{"symptom":"A harmless change is blocked at p = 0.04","cause":"Weak significance across many metrics gives many chances of a false alarm","fix":"Gate on a few chosen metrics and treat weak results as a prompt for review."},{"symptom":"Continuous evaluation results go missing","cause":"`max_hourly_runs` defaults to 100, and runs are skipped when the cap is reached","fix":"Raise `max_hourly_runs` or use Intelligent sampling, and alert on skipped runs."},{"symptom":"A red team scan reads 6% and the team treats it as exact","cause":"A generative model scores ASR, so it can be non-deterministic with false positives","fix":"Review the successful attacks by hand before mitigating, rescan after the fix and compare trends."},{"symptom":"The scan is clean and an indirect injection still works","cause":"Local scans use text-only categories, synthetic data and mock tools","fix":"Add the cloud XPIA category where supported, add AgentDojo-style tasks, and test real tools in a purple environment."},{"symptom":"Shadow testing drops requests or doubles cost","cause":"Azure ML mirroring caps mirrored traffic at 50%, allows one shadow deployment per endpoint and is not supported on Kubernetes online endpoints. No Foundry-native shadow feature was found on 2026-10-13.","fix":"Mirror at the gateway for LLM endpoints, mirror a sample, and budget the duplicate inference."},{"symptom":"The thumbs-down rate looks fine while quality falls","cause":"Feedback is biased toward vocal users","fix":"Use feedback as triage input for new golden cases, not as the only metric."}]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: sample sizes for an A/B test","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"For two arms, unpaired, with alpha 0.05 and power 0.80, the samples per arm are about n = 2 x (1.96 + 0.84)^2 x p x (1 - p) / d^2, where d is the gap you want to detect. Near an 80% pass rate that gives about **251** per arm for a 10-point gap, about **1,004** for 5 points and about **2,788** for 3 points. Paired designs need fewer. These are computed estimates that use the same variance in both arms (the formula in the error-bars paper [[r24]] with that simplification); an unpooled 80% against 70% comparison needs about 290 per arm."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: keeping evaluation cost under control","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Batch several criteria into one judge call with `builtin.output_quality` or `builtin.tool_use_quality` (preview).","Sample live traffic with Random or Intelligent sampling. Intelligent sampling dedupes, filters broken traces, aggregates, then picks diverse traces with MinHash farthest-first selection on local compute, with no extra model calls (preview) [[r40]].","Use a cheaper judge only after you validate agreement with human labels.","Run a small suite on pull requests and a large one nightly.","Cache unchanged targets so only changed systems are re-run."]}]},{"title":"Go deeper: continuous evaluation and Foundry limits","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Continuous evaluation uses `ContinuousEvaluationRuleAction(eval_id=..., max_hourly_runs=100)`. The project managed identity needs the Foundry User role. Recurring evaluation, red team scans and alerts are also preview as of 2026-10-13 [[r7]]. A batch evaluation run accepts at most 100,000 rows and 2 MB per row, and run creation is rate limited at tenant, subscription and project level with a `retry-after` header [[r37]]."}]},{"title":"Go deeper: the GitHub Action inputs","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["`azure-ai-project-endpoint` and `deployment-name` select the project and the judge model.","`data-path` points at the dataset of queries.","`agent-ids` lists agents as `name:version`, comma separated.","`baseline-agent-id` is optional and turns the output into a comparison with confidence intervals and a significance test.","An Azure DevOps version is also preview [[r9]]. Promptfoo offers config-driven evals and red teaming in CI if you prefer a vendor-neutral harness [[r27]]."]}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-l6-q1","type":"order","tag":"order","difficulty":2,"ref":"r9","q":"Put the steps of a CI gate and its feedback loop in order.","items":["A pull request triggers a small fast suite","Results are compared with a baseline run using a confidence interval and significance test","Safety and regression suites block the merge when they degrade","A nightly job runs the full suite with more trials","Failing cases are added to the golden set"],"explain":"The fast subset runs first because it is cheap and runs on every change. A baseline comparison turns raw scores into a decision with noise accounted for. Blocking applies to safety and regression suites, while capability suites stay advisory. The nightly full suite catches what the subset misses, and failing cases feed the golden set for the next cycle."},{"id":"w14-l6-q2","type":"single","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"ref":"r36","q":"The candidate might be worse, the harm could be serious, traffic is moderate, and no calibrated live metric exists yet. Which online step comes first?","options":["A 5% canary rollout, because real users give the cleanest and fastest signal about whether it is worse","Shadow the candidate on mirrored requests and compare outputs before any user sees it","A 50/50 A/B test for two weeks, chosen to maximize statistical power on a business metric","Full release with a thumbs-up and thumbs-down widget so users report any problems quickly"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shadow traffic copies live requests to the candidate without changing what users receive, so a worse version costs nothing in exposure [[r36]]. A canary and an A/B test both expose real users to a possibly worse version, and without a trusted metric you cannot tell whether it is worse. A thumbs widget on a full release arrives after the harm and is biased toward vocal users."},{"id":"w14-l6-q3","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","difficulty":3,"ref":"r24","q":"About how many tasks per arm do you need to detect a 5-point gap near an 80% pass rate, unpaired, with alpha 0.05 and power 0.80?","answer":1004,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"tasks per arm","hint":"n = 2 x (1.96 + 0.84)^2 x p x (1 - p) / d^2, with p = 0.8 and d = 0.05.","explain":"(1.96 + 0.84)^2 is 7.84, and p x (1 - p) is 0.16. So n = 2 x 7.84 x 0.16 / 0.0025, which is about 1,004. Halving the gap to detect roughly quadruples the sample. This is why small suites cannot gate small changes, and why paired designs and larger gaps matter."},{"id":"w14-l6-q4","type":"single","tag":"security","difficulty":3,"ref":"r5","q":"A local red team scan reports an Attack Success Rate of 6% across text-only categories. What is the right next step?","options":["Report 6% as the exact vulnerability rate and ship, since the scanner is Microsoft tooling","Discard the scan, because its data is synthetic and so carries no information","Rerun the scan until the rate reads 0%, then ship the build that produced it","Review the successful attacks by hand, because a model scores ASR and can produce false positives, then mitigate and rescan"],"answer":3,"explain":"The documentation says ASR is scored by a generative model, so it can be non-deterministic with false positives, and findings need review before mitigation [[r5]]. Treating 6% as exact ignores that. Synthetic data limits coverage but still reveals real weaknesses. Rerunning until the number is zero optimizes the metric rather than the system. Combine scans with expert analysis, as Microsoft advises."}],"defend":null,"refs":["r5","r6","r7","r9","r15","r24","r26","r27","r36","r37","r39","r40","r43","r47"],"takeaways":["A gate needs a rule and a noise budget. A baseline comparison with an interval beats a fixed threshold.","Block on safety and regression suites, keep capability suites advisory, and run a small suite per pull request with a large one nightly.","Red team scores come from a model judge. Review attacks by hand and combine scans with expert analysis.","Shadow first when a worse version could harm users. Use A/B when exposure is fine and traffic is large.","Budget the judge as items x evaluators x trials x tokens, and sample live traffic."]},{"id":"w14-l7","order":7,"title":"Boss: ship it with evidence","kind":"boss","minutes":18,"brief":"An interviewer asks you to build an evaluation strategy for an AI assistant, before launch and after. Every dial is unspecified. Practise the five steps of Dial-and-Defend out loud, then compare with the model answer.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Open with the dials, not the tools","level":2,"id":"open-with-the-dials-not-the-tools"},{"t":"scenario","title":"The interviewer's prompt","known":["A new assistant retrieves documents and calls a few tools","It is about to launch, and you must design its evaluation before and after launch","You are expected to compare options and defend a choice"],"ask":["What can go wrong, and how bad is it: a wrong answer, a data leak or an unwanted action?","Which stages exist (retrieval, tools, generation), and do relevance labels or reference answers exist?","Is there any production traffic or trace history, and how many expert hours are available?","How much traffic is expected, and what is the cost ceiling for judge and inference calls?","How fast can a bad release be stopped, and which platform features are preview?"],"tension":"A heavy pre-launch gate delays the launch and costs expert time. A light gate ships risk. Online scoring catches drift, but only after users saw the answer."},{"t":"flow","title":"Dial-and-Defend applied to evaluation","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Clarify","md":"Harm, stages, labels, traffic, expert time, budget, rollback speed.","tag":"1"},{"title":"List options","md":"Heavy gate, light gate with online scoring, and the layered plan. Name the boring one.","tag":"2"},{"title":"Pick and flip","md":"Choose for the stated dials. Say what would move you to another plan.","tag":"3"},{"title":"Failure modes","md":"Judge bias, stale set, noisy gate, contamination, false positives. Say how you detect each.","tag":"4"},{"title":"Metrics and rollout","md":"Name the numbers you report and the order of release.","tag":"5"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"A layered plan across the lifecycle","head":["Stage","Check","Report"],"rows":[["Before launch","Golden set (hand plus reviewed synthetic), component evals, calibrated judge, red team in a purple environment","Recall at k or nDCG, groundedness and completeness, pass^k with interval, judge TPR and TNR, ASR with manual review"],["Pull request and nightly","Small suite on every change, full suite nightly, baseline comparison with significance","Delta against baseline with confidence interval"],["Release","Shadow or mirrored traffic when harm is high, then canary","Paired scores, rollback trigger on a safety evaluator"],["After launch","Sampled continuous evaluation, scheduled evaluation and red teaming, feedback triage","Online score drift, skipped runs, judge spend"],["Learning loop","Failing traces added to the golden set, synthetic rows regenerated when instructions change","Dataset version, origin mix, held-out score"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What interviewers listen for","md":"They want to hear that you separate retrieval from generation, calibrate the judge, quantify noise with intervals and repeated trials, and label preview features with a fallback. Naming a tool before asking about risk is the most common way to lose the room."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-l7-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","difficulty":3,"ref":"r8","q":"An assistant with retrieval and three tools launches in four weeks. Harm from a wrong action is high, 12 golden cases exist and an expert has two days. Which pre-launch plan gives the most signal for that time?","options":["Rerun the existing 12 cases ten times and report the average score, since repetition removes the need for new cases","Grow to about 40 cases with reviewed synthetic rows, score stages separately, and calibrate a pass/fail judge","Adopt a public agent benchmark as the release gate, because it has thousands of tasks and is already labelled","Ship to 5% of users and judge by thumbs ratings, because real traffic is always better than curated test cases"],"answer":1,"explain":"Starting sets of 20 to 50 cases from real failures, a reviewed synthetic batch for coverage, stage-level scores and a calibrated judge use two expert days well [[r17]] [[r8]]. Repeating 12 cases lowers sampling noise but adds no coverage. A public benchmark does not test your tools or data and can be saturated or contaminated. A 5% launch with thumbs ratings exposes users to high harm and gives a biased signal."},{"id":"w14-l7-q2","type":"multi","tag":"flip","difficulty":2,"ref":"r8","q":"Select every event that should trigger a re-check of the evaluation setup.","options":["The agent's system prompt or instructions change materially","The judge model is upgraded or replaced","The retriever's chunking changes, so relevance labels keyed to chunk ids no longer match","The dashboard theme is switched from light to dark"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"A synthetic dataset made from one version of the instructions goes stale when they change, so regenerate and version it [[r8]]. A different judge model has different habits, so recalibrate its true positive and true negative rates against the human sample [[r16]]. Labels keyed to old chunk ids score the wrong chunks after a re-chunk. A theme change touches no evaluation input."},{"id":"w14-l7-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","difficulty":3,"ref":"r1","q":"Which explanation is best supported, and what is the first action?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"offline regression suite:     97% pass\nsampled online judge score:   falling for 2 weeks\nnew product feature shipped:  3 weeks ago"},"options":["The online judge is miscalibrated, so the 97% offline pass rate should be trusted instead","The offline suite is too small to mean anything, so its 97% pass rate carries no information","The model provider silently degraded quality, which breaks the service agreement","The golden set no longer covers the new feature's queries, so mine failing traces into it"],"answer":3,"explain":"The decline began after a feature changed what users ask, which points to a golden set that stopped matching real queries. The first action is to sample the failing traces, confirm with a human-labelled sample that the judge agrees, and add the cases to the set [[r1]]. Ignoring the online judge skips that check. A small suite can still be informative for the cases it covers. A provider regression is possible but nothing in the data points to it."},{"id":"w14-l7-q4","type":"match","tag":"concept","difficulty":2,"ref":"r5","q":"Match each situation to the evaluation layer that fits it best.","pairs":[{"l":"Block a prompt change that breaks citations","r":"CI gate on a regression suite"},{"l":"Detect drift in live answer quality without labels","r":"Continuous evaluation with sampled reference-free judges"},{"l":"Probe for indirect prompt injection before release","r":"Automated red team scan plus expert review"},{"l":"Compare a new model on real requests with no user exposure","r":"Shadow traffic"}],"explain":"A regression suite with a baseline comparison blocks a known behaviour from breaking. Live traces have no references, so drift needs reference-free judges on a sample. Injection probing needs attacks, which automated scans supply and experts review. Only mirrored traffic lets a candidate see real requests without users seeing its answers."}],"defend":{"prompt":"We are about to launch an AI assistant that retrieves company documents and calls a few tools. How will you evaluate it before launch and after? Walk me through your options.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["What can go wrong and how bad is it: a wrong answer, a data leak or an unwanted action? That sets the risk tier.","Which stages exist (retrieval, tools, generation), and do relevance labels or reference answers exist?","Is there any production traffic or trace history yet, and how many expert hours are available?","How much traffic will it get, and what is the cost ceiling for judge and inference calls?","How quickly can a bad release be stopped, and which platform features I would rely on are preview?"],"options":[{"name":"Heavy pre-launch gate","when":"Harm is high, traffic is unknown and rollback is slow.","tradeoff":"The strongest protection before exposure, but it delays launch, spends scarce expert time and a frozen set drifts from real users."},{"name":"Light gate plus online scoring","when":"Harm is low, errors are cheap to undo, traffic is large and a calibrated judge exists.","tradeoff":"Fast and realistic, but users see problems first and reference-free judges can mislead."},{"name":"Layered: gate, shadow, then sampled scoring","when":"Most real systems: moderate to high harm, mixed stages and some traffic after a soft launch.","tradeoff":"The most parts to maintain, and several Foundry features are preview, so it needs a code-first fallback."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**Clarify.** I would first ask what can go wrong and how bad it is, which stages exist, what labels and traces I have, how many expert hours and how much budget there are, and how fast I can stop a bad release. Those are my dials."},{"t":"p","md":"**Options and pick.** There are three plans: a heavy pre-launch gate, a light gate with online scoring, and a layered plan. For a system with retrieval and tools and moderate to high harm I would choose the layered plan. Here is what it contains."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Golden set: 20 to 50 cases from real failures plus a reviewed synthetic batch. Version it, tag each row by origin and hold out a slice nobody tunes on.","Stage scores: retrieval with rank or judged context metrics at the k the prompt uses, answers with groundedness and completeness, tools with outcome checks plus selected process evaluators.","Judge: one accountable expert labels a sample with critiques. I report true positive and true negative rates, swap order for pairwise, use references where answers are checkable and use a different model family from the generator.","Noise: repeated trials on agent tasks, pass^k and confidence intervals, with the suite sized for the effect I need to see.","Red team: automated scans (preview) plus expert review in a purple environment, including indirect injection.","Gate: a small suite per pull request, a full suite nightly, baseline comparison with significance, blocking on safety and regression suites and advisory capability suites.","Release and after: shadow first when harm is high, then canary, then sampled continuous scoring with caps, scheduled evaluation for drift, feedback as triage and failing traces back into the golden set."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Failure modes and how I detect them","md":"Judge bias shows as swapped-order disagreement and low TNR on a fresh human sample. A stale golden set shows as a widening gap between offline and online scores. A noisy gate shows as run-to-run variance on the same commit. Contamination shows as a held-out slice scoring below the tuned set. Red team false positives show up in manual review."},{"t":"table","caption":"What would flip the plan","head":["Dial change","Flip to"],"rows":[["Harm is very high and rollback is slow","Heavy gate with a named human sign-off before any exposure"],["No budget for expert labels","Judged context metrics and a panel judge, with the weaker evidence stated"],["Traffic too low for an A/B test","Shadow runs and paired offline comparisons instead"],["A needed Foundry feature is preview","A code-first harness as the fallback, with the date of the last check"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Metrics and rollout.** I report recall at k or nDCG, groundedness and completeness, pass^k with intervals, judge TPR and TNR, ASR trend, online score drift and cost per run. Rollout goes from a staff soft launch to shadow, canary and full release, with a rollback trigger tied to a safety evaluator."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks about harm, stages, labels, traffic, expert time and budget before naming tools","w":3},{"pt":"Offers at least two plans and picks one for the stated dials","w":2},{"pt":"Scores retrieval and generation separately, with k tied to what the prompt receives","w":2},{"pt":"Calibrates the judge against an expert and names position, verbosity and self-preference bias","w":3},{"pt":"Handles non-determinism with repeated trials, pass^k and confidence intervals","w":2},{"pt":"Designs the CI gate and the online loop, including shadow or canary and sampled scoring","w":2},{"pt":"Names failure modes with detection, plus metrics and a rollout order","w":2},{"pt":"States flip conditions and labels preview features with a fallback","w":1}],"followUps":[{"q":"The judge agrees with your expert 92% of the time. Do you trust it?","a":"Not yet. With imbalanced classes raw agreement can hide a judge that misses most failures, so check true positive and true negative rates. Swap order for pairwise, test on a fresh sample and recalibrate whenever the judge model changes."},{"q":"Your gate score moved from 4.2 to 4.4. Do you ship?","a":"Not on that alone. Without an interval, a paired comparison and a calibrated scale it is probably noise. At 100 tasks and an 80% pass rate the 95% half-width is about 7.8 points. Use a baseline comparison with a significance test, more trials or a larger set."},{"q":"There are no traces and launch is in two weeks. What do you build first?","a":"Twenty to fifty hand-written cases from known failure types, a reviewed synthetic batch from the agent definition and documents, component evals for retrieval and tools, a calibrated pass/fail judge and a held-out slice. Then a soft launch with sampled scoring so real traces start feeding the set."}],"redFlags":["Names a tool or metric before asking about risk, stages and labels","Treats a single judge score as ground truth with no calibration or interval","Uses faithfulness alone and calls the answer correct","Lets one run decide a release with non-deterministic output"]},"refs":["r1","r3","r5","r8","r16","r17","r24"],"takeaways":["Open with the dials: harm, stages, labels, traffic, expert time, budget and rollback speed.","Layer the checks: component, end-to-end, gate, red team, shadow and sampled live scoring.","Say how you calibrate the judge and how you quantify noise.","Close with failure modes, metrics, rollout order and what would flip your plan."]}],"topics":[{"id":"w14-t01","order":1,"group":"Foundations","title":"Why LLM evaluation is different","minutes":6,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Classic tests assert one exact output. LLM features vary run to run, accept many valid answers and fail in stacked layers, so evaluation becomes measurement: a dataset, a scorer with known error, repeated trials and a decision rule.","useIf":"You are about to ship or change an LLM feature and must decide how you will know it works.","avoidIf":"The output is fully mechanical (a schema, a lookup, a regex) and a plain unit test already covers it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A classic test asserts that one input gives one exact output. An LLM feature breaks that contract in three ways, and each break changes how you measure."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**The output varies.** Sampling makes two runs differ. Lowering `temperature` narrows the spread but does not remove it, so one run is a sample, not a fact.","**Many outputs are valid.** An exact match fails a correct answer phrased differently. You need a scorer: code for what can be checked mechanically, a judge model for meaning and tone, a human for taste. Anthropic names these three grader types: code-based, model-based and human [[r17]].","**Failures stack in layers.** A wrong answer can come from retrieval, the prompt, the model, a tool call or the orchestration. One end-to-end score cannot say which."]},{"t":"p","md":"So evaluation is measurement. You need a dataset, a scorer whose error you have measured, enough repeated trials to see past noise, and a decision rule. Quality is also several numbers at once: correctness, groundedness, safety, latency and cost."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-t01-d1","title":"The measurement loop","alt":"A dataset is run through the system several times, scored by code, judge or human, aggregated with an interval, and a decision rule chooses to ship or to fix and add cases.","src":"flowchart LR\n  D[Dataset] --> R[Run n trials]\n  R --> S[Score: code, judge, human]\n  S --> A[Aggregate with interval]\n  A --> X{Decision rule}\n  X -->|ship| P[Release]\n  X -->|fix| D"},{"t":"p","md":"Set the pass bar as a product decision. Hamel Husain notes that, unlike unit tests, you do not need a 100% pass rate: it depends on the failures you are willing to tolerate [[tr6]]. Anthropic separates capability evals, which start at a low pass rate, from regression evals, which should sit near 100% and protect what already works [[r17]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"One-line answer","md":"LLM evals differ because the system is stochastic, the answer space is open and the faults are layered. So you score by layer, repeat trials and report an interval."}],"whereToUse":["Before the first release of any LLM feature, to get a baseline","On every prompt, model, retriever or tool change as a regression check","When a vendor retires or updates a model version and you must prove parity","When product, risk and engineering disagree and need a shared number"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The output is free text, a ranking, a plan or a tool sequence","Several components can each cause a bad answer","A wrong answer has a cost you can name","You will change the system repeatedly"],"avoid":["The result is fully checkable by code and a unit test already exists","The feature is a throwaway demo that no one will rely on","A fixed, enumerable input set already has exact expected outputs that code can compare"],"flips":["Harm per wrong answer rises: add safety evaluators, red teaming and human review before launch","A reference answer exists for every case: prefer code checks over a judge","Traffic is large and labels are scarce: lean on online scoring of sampled traces"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Layered evaluation (this topic)","Quality matters and the system changes often","The output is fully mechanical","Dataset upkeep and judge cost"],["Manual spot checks","A prototype with one developer","More than one person edits the system","Not repeatable, memory based"],["Exact-match unit tests","Structured outputs and tool arguments","Free text with many valid phrasings","False failures on correct answers"],["Public benchmark scores","Shortlisting base models","You need to know about your data and tasks","Saturation and training-data leakage"],["Production monitoring only","Low harm and instant rollback","Harm is costly before you notice","Users find the failures"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The score rose 0.2 but users still complain","cause":"The metric does not measure the failing behaviour, or an uncalibrated judge is scoring it","fix":"Start from real failure cases, pick a scorer per failure and check judge agreement with a human sample"},{"symptom":"The same commit passes on Monday and fails on Tuesday","cause":"A single run of a stochastic system, compared with a fixed threshold","fix":"Repeat trials, report an interval and compare paired against a baseline"},{"symptom":"One blended quality score hides a safety regression","cause":"Dimensions were averaged into a single number","fix":"Gate each dimension separately and block on safety on its own"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t01-s1","title":"Two-person prototype, no traffic","situation":"A two-person team has a working assistant that drafts support replies. A human reviews and sends every reply. There is no production traffic, and one domain expert can give two hours a week.","constraints":["No traces exist","Expert time is the scarce resource","A human sends every reply, so errors are reversible"],"unknowns":["How many real failure examples already exist?","Can the reviewers' edits be logged?","What would a wrong reply cost the customer?"],"question":"How do you start measuring?","options":[{"name":"20 to 50 cases from real failures, code checks plus a pass/fail judge","verdict":"best","why":"It matches the expert budget and gives a regression baseline from day one. Anthropic calls 20 to 50 tasks drawn from real failures a great start [[r17]].","tradeoff":"A small set cannot see small changes, and the judge needs calibrating against the expert."},{"name":"Ship with human review and log the edits as the signal","verdict":"good","why":"Risk is low and reversible, and every edit is a free label.","tradeoff":"There is no pre-release gate, so regressions appear after users meet them."},{"name":"Track a public benchmark score","verdict":"poor","why":"It measures other tasks on other data and may be saturated or leaked.","tradeoff":"It is right at the model selection stage, when you shortlist base models, never as proof for your product."}],"flips":[{"if":"Replies go out without human review","then":"Harm is no longer reversible: add safety evaluators and red teaming before launch."},{"if":"Thousands of real traces already exist","then":"Mine them for the set instead of writing cases by hand."}],"takeaway":"Start small from real failures, then grow with traces."},{"id":"w14-t01-s2","title":"Regulated guidance, weekly releases","situation":"An assistant gives account guidance to retail customers from retrieved policy documents and one tool lookup. Wrong answers create regulatory exposure. Releases ship weekly and compliance wants evidence per release.","constraints":["High harm per wrong answer","Weekly release cadence","Budget exists for judge calls","Per-release evidence is required"],"unknowns":["Which layer changed in each release?","Are labelled policy answers available?","What failure rate is tolerable for each harm class?"],"question":"What evaluation design do you propose?","options":[{"name":"Layered suite: component checks, end-to-end golden set, safety evaluators, sampled human review","verdict":"best","why":"Each layer localises faults and the safety and human layers supply the evidence compliance asks for.","tradeoff":"More upkeep and judge spend every week."},{"name":"Platform content filters plus one manual red team before launch","verdict":"good","why":"It finds real harms early.","tradeoff":"It is not repeatable for weekly releases and gives no regression signal."},{"name":"One end-to-end LLM-judge score on a 1 to 5 scale","verdict":"poor","why":"It hides the failing layer, uses an uncalibrated scale and has no safety signal.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a low-stakes early prototype where speed matters more than evidence."}],"flips":[{"if":"Releases move to monthly","then":"Heavier human review per release becomes affordable."},{"if":"The assistant is internal only and advisory","then":"A lighter safety layer is defensible."}],"takeaway":"Raise the stakes and the layers multiply, with each dimension gated on its own."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t01-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Why do exact-match assertions fail for most LLM answers?","options":["Many different outputs can be correct, so one string cannot represent the expected result","Tokenizers make string comparison unreliable","Models cannot repeat any earlier output","Exact match is slower than a judge"],"answer":0,"explain":"Open-ended outputs have many valid phrasings. Tokenization does not affect string comparison, models can repeat output, and speed is not the issue."},{"id":"w14-t01-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which are real reasons one end-to-end score is not enough? Select all that apply.","options":["It cannot say which layer failed","It can average away a safety or cost regression","Scores from model graders are always wrong","Run-to-run noise can hide a small change"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Layer blindness, averaging and noise are real. Model graders are imperfect but can be calibrated, so 'always wrong' is false."},{"id":"w14-t01-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each grader type to the job it does best.","pairs":[{"l":"Code-based","r":"Mechanical checks such as end state or a required tool call"},{"l":"Model-based","r":"Meaning and tone judged against a rubric"},{"l":"Human","r":"Calibrating the model grader and judging taste"}],"explain":"Use code where the answer is checkable, a judge model where meaning matters and humans to calibrate and to decide what good means."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which three classic-testing assumptions do LLM apps break?","back":"Same input gives the same output, one expected output exists, and a failure points to one place. LLM apps vary run to run, accept many valid answers and fail in stacked layers."},{"front":"Capability eval vs regression eval?","back":"Capability evals ask whether it can do this at all and start at a low pass rate. Regression evals ask whether it still does what it did and should sit near 100%."},{"front":"Name the three grader types.","back":"Code-based for mechanical checks, model-based for meaning against a rubric, human for calibration and taste."}],"related":["w14-t02","w14-t03","w14-t12","w14-l1","w01-t05"],"refs":["r17","tr6","r1"]},{"id":"w14-t02","order":2,"group":"Foundations","title":"Offline vs online evaluation","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Offline evaluation runs a fixed dataset with reference answers before release. Online evaluation scores sampled live traffic without references after release. You need both, joined by a loop that turns production failures into new test cases.","useIf":"You must decide which checks gate a release and which keep watching after it.","avoidIf":"You are tempted to use either one alone: offline alone misses drift, online alone lets users find regressions.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Offline** evaluation runs a candidate over dataset examples that carry reference outputs. It suits benchmarking, regression checks, unit tests and backtesting, and it is repeatable, so two versions can be compared on identical inputs. **Online** evaluation scores production runs and threads as they arrive. They have no reference outputs, so it checks quality patterns, safety and anomalies [[r29]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Online scoring runs on logged traces asynchronously, so it adds no latency to the user request. Without ground truth it relies on reference-free judges [[r30]]. Foundry frames three lifecycle stages: base model selection, pre-production evaluation and post-production monitoring. The last covers operational metrics, continuous evaluation of sampled live traffic, scheduled evaluation on a test set to detect drift, scheduled red teaming and Azure Monitor alerts [[r1]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-t02-d1","title":"The loop between offline and online","alt":"An offline golden set gates a release, live traffic is scored online, and failing production traces flow back into the offline set.","src":"flowchart LR\n  G[Offline golden set] --> Q{Gate}\n  Q -->|pass| L[Live traffic]\n  L --> O[Online scoring]\n  O --> F[Failing traces]\n  F --> G"},{"t":"table","caption":"What each side can and cannot do","head":["Question","Offline","Online"],"rows":[["Reference answers?","Yes, so reference-based metrics work","No, so reference-free judges and safety checks"],["Inputs","Fixed, curated","Real and shifting"],["User exposure","None","Scoring is passive, but content is read"],["Catches","Regressions before release","Drift, new query types, rare failures"],["Cost driver","Dataset size x evaluators x trials","Sampling rate x judge tokens"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Foundry continuous evaluation is preview as checked on 2026-10-13. Its documented default is 100 runs per hour (`max_hourly_runs`), and runs are skipped once that cap is reached [[r7]]. Intelligent sampling dedupes, drops broken traces and picks diverse ones without extra model calls [[r40]]."}],"whereToUse":["Release gates in CI, where a fixed dataset makes versions comparable","Model or prompt upgrades, where you backtest on a set built from past traffic","Production dashboards that sample traces and score groundedness and safety","Drift detection on a schedule when the input mix changes","Triage queues that feed low-scoring traces back into the golden set"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Offline when you can write or mine reference answers","Online when traffic is large and real inputs differ from your set","Both when a regression is costly and the user mix moves"],"avoid":["Online-only when harm happens before anyone notices","Offline-only when the corpus or users change weekly","Scoring every live trace with a large judge when sampling would do"],"flips":["Privacy rules forbid reading live content: keep online scoring to metadata and redacted text, or skip it","Traffic is tiny: online samples are too few, so lean on offline sets and human review","No traces exist yet (pre-launch): offline only, built from hand-written and synthetic cases"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Offline golden set","Gating a change with references","Inputs drift from your set","Stale set gives false comfort"],["Continuous online scoring","Watching real traffic without references","Content cannot be read, or traffic is tiny","Judge tokens, privacy review"],["Shadow traffic","Testing a new version with zero user impact","No gateway can mirror requests","Doubled model cost, side effects"],["Canary or A/B","Measuring real user outcomes","Harm is high and exposure unacceptable","Users see the worse version"],["Human review of sampled traces","Calibrating judges and finding new failure types","Volume is large and reviewers are few","Slow and costly"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Online scores stop updating on busy days","cause":"The continuous evaluation hourly cap was hit and runs were skipped","fix":"Raise `max_hourly_runs`, lower the sampling rate on purpose, or alert on skipped runs"},{"symptom":"Offline score stays green while users complain","cause":"The golden set no longer resembles real queries","fix":"Feed failing and low-feedback traces back into the set and refresh on a schedule"},{"symptom":"A privacy review blocks online scoring","cause":"Judges read real prompts and responses","fix":"Redact before scoring, restrict access to results, set retention, and sample rather than score everything"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t02-s1","title":"New assistant, nothing in production","situation":"A team is two weeks from the first release of a document Q&A assistant. There is no traffic, a corpus of a few thousand documents and one subject expert.","constraints":["No traces","Reference answers can be written for a small set","Launch date is fixed"],"unknowns":["How harmful is a wrong answer?","Can the first users be a small pilot group?"],"question":"Which checks do you put in place before launch?","options":[{"name":"Offline set of hand-written and synthetic cases, plus a pilot with sampled online scoring after launch","verdict":"best","why":"It gates the release with references and prepares the online loop that will catch what the set misses.","tradeoff":"The first set is guessed, so early scores overstate how real users will fare."},{"name":"Online scoring only, starting at launch","verdict":"poor","why":"There would be no regression gate, so the first users find the failures.","tradeoff":"Online-only fits an internal tool with instant rollback and low harm."},{"name":"A 2,000-case synthetic set judged by five judges","verdict":"good","why":"Broad coverage and judge agreement.","tradeoff":"Large cost, and unreviewed synthetic rows can be easy or wrong."}],"flips":[{"if":"The pilot users are named staff who can report errors","then":"A lighter offline set is enough because feedback is quick."},{"if":"Wrong answers carry legal exposure","then":"Add red teaming and a human-reviewed safety slice before launch."}],"takeaway":"Before launch you only have offline. Plan the online loop before launch."},{"id":"w14-t02-s2","title":"Mature assistant, heavy traffic, sensitive content","situation":"An assistant handles millions of conversations a month over confidential documents. Quality has drifted twice after content updates. Security restricts who may read stored prompts.","constraints":["Very high volume","Content is confidential","Drift has already happened twice","Judge budget is capped"],"unknowns":["Can redacted traces be scored?","How quickly must drift be seen?","Who may read stored content?"],"question":"How do you monitor quality without exposing content or overspending?","options":[{"name":"Sample and redact traces, score with reference-free judges, schedule an offline drift run","verdict":"best","why":"Sampling controls cost, redaction answers security, and the scheduled offline run catches drift with references.","tradeoff":"Rare failures can fall between samples and redaction may blur some context."},{"name":"Score every trace with the strongest judge","verdict":"poor","why":"The cost is large and it reads all confidential content.","tradeoff":"It is right only for a low-volume, high-stakes service with approved content access."},{"name":"Rely on thumbs up and down alone","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and needs no content reading.","tradeoff":"Feedback is sparse and biased toward vocal users, so quality can drift unseen."}],"flips":[{"if":"Volume falls to a few hundred requests a day","then":"Review all or most traces by hand and drop sampling."},{"if":"Content may not be stored at all","then":"Score inside the request path on metadata and safety signals only."}],"takeaway":"At scale, sampling and redaction are the cost and privacy controls."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t02-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Why can online evaluation not use reference-based metrics?","options":["Online runs are too slow for metrics","Reference-based metrics only support English","Live requests have no reference output to compare against","The judge cannot see the response"],"answer":2,"explain":"Production runs carry no gold answer, so online scoring uses reference-free judges and safety checks. Online scoring is asynchronous, so speed is not the issue."},{"id":"w14-t02-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Online quality scores flatline during a traffic peak, yet nothing in the app changed. What do you check first?","options":["Whether the golden set has been versioned","Whether the continuous evaluation hourly run cap was reached and runs were skipped","Whether temperature is set to 0","Whether the judge prompt has few-shot examples"],"answer":1,"explain":"The documented default is 100 runs per hour and runs are skipped at the cap. The others do not explain a flatline tied to traffic."},{"id":"w14-t02-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the loop that keeps an evaluation set fresh.","items":["Gate the release on the offline set","Release and sample live traffic","Score sampled traces online","Triage the failing traces","Add confirmed failures to the offline set"],"explain":"The gate comes first, then live sampling and scoring, then human triage so only confirmed failures enter the set."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What can offline evaluation use that online cannot?","back":"Reference outputs. Offline examples carry gold answers, so reference-based metrics and exact comparisons work. Online traces have none."},{"front":"How does Foundry label its three lifecycle stages?","back":"Base model selection, pre-production evaluation and post-production monitoring, which adds continuous evaluation, scheduled evaluation, scheduled red teaming and alerts."},{"front":"What is the cost control for online scoring?","back":"Sampling rate and filters. Each scored trace costs judge tokens, so sample, dedupe and cap runs per hour."}],"related":["w14-t01","w14-t15","w14-t14","w15-t11","w14-l1"],"refs":["r1","r29","r30","r7","r40"]},{"id":"w14-t03","order":3,"group":"Foundations","title":"Component vs end-to-end evaluation","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Component evals score one stage with the other stages held fixed, so a fault points at a layer. End-to-end evals score the whole system on the user's task, so they say whether users are affected. Use many cheap component checks and fewer expensive end-to-end ones.","useIf":"A pipeline has several stages (retrieve, rerank, generate, call tools) and you need to know which one broke.","avoidIf":"The system is a single model call with no retrieval or tools, where one end-to-end score is the component.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**End-to-end** evaluation treats the system as a black box: a question goes in, an answer comes out, and you grade the answer against the user's task. It is what users feel, but a low score does not say where the fault is. **Component** evaluation fixes the inputs to one stage and scores only that stage: retrieval with rank metrics, generation given a fixed context, tool selection given a fixed plan, a router or a guardrail as a classifier."},{"t":"p","md":"Think of a test pyramid. Hamel Husain describes three levels: fast assertions run on every code change, model and human evaluation on a set cadence, and A/B tests after significant product changes, with cost rising at each level [[tr6]]. Foundry's suggested combinations follow the same idea: RAG is Retrieval, Groundedness, Relevance and Content Safety; agents are Output Quality, Tool Use Quality, Rubric and Content Safety [[r2]]. For agents, Foundry separates system-level evaluators from process-level ones that point at a step [[r3]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-t03-d1","title":"Where each score attaches","alt":"A query passes through retrieval, generation and tool use to an answer. Rank metrics score retrieval, groundedness scores generation, tool accuracy scores tool use, and a correctness score judges the final answer.","src":"flowchart LR\n  Q[Query] --> R[Retrieve]\n  R --> G[Generate]\n  G --> T[Tools]\n  T --> A[Answer]\n  R -.-> M1[Rank metrics]\n  G -.-> M2[Groundedness]\n  T -.-> M3[Tool accuracy]\n  A -.-> M4[End-to-end score]"},{"t":"table","caption":"Isolating the fault (practitioner heuristic)","head":["Symptom","Likely layer","Component check to run first"],"rows":[["Answer misses a fact that exists in the corpus","Retrieval","Recall@k with k set to the chunks you send"],["Right context, answer contradicts it","Generation","Groundedness with the context fixed"],["Right tool, wrong arguments","Tool use","Tool input accuracy on the call"],["Wrong tool for the intent","Planning or routing","Tool selection against labelled intents"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Freeze the upstream","md":"To test generation alone, feed it gold or recorded contexts. To test retrieval alone, score the ranked list and stop. Both isolate a fault without waiting for a full run."}],"whereToUse":["RAG systems, where retrieval and generation fail for different reasons","Agents with several tools, where a single bad call can ruin the outcome","Pipelines with a router, reranker or guardrail that has its own error rate","CI, where cheap component checks gate every change and end-to-end runs nightly"],"whenToUse":{"use":["More than one stage can cause the same bad answer","You change one stage at a time (new retriever, new prompt)","You need a fast check on every commit"],"avoid":["Treating component scores as proof users are happy","Building a metric for every stage when one stage has never failed","Skipping an end-to-end check because every component score looks healthy"],"flips":["Interaction effects dominate (context order, truncation): raise the share of end-to-end checks","Few labels exist: lean on end-to-end rubric judges and add component labels as failures appear","A stage is a managed black box you cannot isolate: test it end to end with fixed inputs"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Component evals only","You change one stage and need quick fault location","Stages interact (ordering, truncation)","Green parts, bad whole"],["End-to-end evals only","You need the user-visible verdict","You must find which stage failed","Slow, costly, no localisation"],["Layered (both)","The system has stages and real stakes","A tiny prototype with one call","Upkeep of two sets of labels"],["Manual trace review","Discovering new failure types","Many releases per week","Reviewer time"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Every component score is green but the answers are poor","cause":"Interaction between stages, such as context order or prompt truncation, which no isolated test exercises","fix":"Keep an end-to-end set and compare its trend with component trends"},{"symptom":"The team chases ten dashboards and false alarms","cause":"Too many component metrics, so some move by chance","fix":"Choose one primary metric per layer and gate on those"},{"symptom":"A generation fix appears to fail","cause":"Retrieval was also changing, so the test was not isolated","fix":"Freeze retrieved contexts when testing generation"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t03-s1","title":"Quality dropped after a retriever swap","situation":"A RAG assistant's end-to-end pass rate fell from 82% to 74% the day the team replaced its retriever. Nothing else shipped. Both versions are in a staging environment.","constraints":["Only one component changed","A labelled query set with relevant document ids exists","Fix needed this week"],"unknowns":["Did recall at the sent k drop, or ranking?","Did context length change?","Are the failures concentrated in some query types?"],"question":"What do you run first?","options":[{"name":"Retrieval metrics on the labelled queries for old and new retriever","verdict":"best","why":"Only retrieval changed, so a recall and ranking comparison at the real k localises the fault in minutes and at low cost.","tradeoff":"It shows retrieval quality, not whether a different prompt could compensate."},{"name":"Rerun the full end-to-end suite with more judges","verdict":"poor","why":"It repeats the symptom and costs more without naming the layer.","tradeoff":"More judges help when the question is whether the final answer is acceptable, not where it broke."},{"name":"Tune the generation prompt to recover the score","verdict":"good","why":"A prompt can mask weaker context.","tradeoff":"It hides a retrieval regression and may fail on queries the new retriever misses entirely."}],"flips":[{"if":"Retrieval metrics are unchanged","then":"Look at generation with contexts frozen, then at context formatting and truncation."},{"if":"No relevance labels exist","then":"Use judged context metrics while you label a small query set."}],"takeaway":"Change one stage, check that stage first."},{"id":"w14-t03-s2","title":"Tool-using agent in CI","situation":"An agent with six tools runs in CI on every pull request. A full multi-turn evaluation takes 40 minutes and costs real judge tokens. Engineers merge 30 times a day.","constraints":["Tight CI time budget","Judge spend is capped","Tool calls have stable, checkable arguments"],"unknowns":["Which tools carry risk?","Is there an outcome check available in a sandbox?","How flaky is the full run?"],"question":"How do you split checks between pull requests and nightly runs?","options":[{"name":"Code-based tool and argument checks on a small set per PR, full end-to-end nightly","verdict":"best","why":"Cheap deterministic checks give fast feedback on the most common breakage, and the expensive run stays off the critical path.","tradeoff":"Some interaction failures appear a day late."},{"name":"Full end-to-end on every PR","verdict":"poor","why":"It blocks 30 merges a day for 40 minutes and burns budget.","tradeoff":"Fine for a weekly release branch with few merges."},{"name":"Only manual review of a few traces each week","verdict":"good","why":"Finds new failure types.","tradeoff":"No regression gate, and it does not scale with merges."}],"flips":[{"if":"Merges drop to a few a week","then":"Run the end-to-end suite on every PR."},{"if":"A tool can change real data","then":"Add an outcome check in an isolated environment before merge."}],"takeaway":"Run cheap component checks often and end-to-end checks on a slower cadence."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Recall@5 is high and groundedness is high, yet users report wrong answers. What is the most plausible gap?","options":["Retrieval returns nothing relevant","The judge ignores the context","Tokenization of the query failed","The retrieved documents may be wrong or outdated, so a faithful answer can still be incorrect"],"answer":3,"explain":"Groundedness checks consistency with the retrieved context, not truth. If the context is wrong or stale, the answer is faithful and wrong. High recall rules out option one, and the others are not implied."},{"id":"w14-t03-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"You can afford one new check this sprint. Retrieval and generation both look fine in spot checks, but the agent sometimes calls the wrong tool. Which check?","options":["A larger end-to-end LLM-judge suite","Tool selection accuracy on labelled intents","More retrieval metrics","A longer system prompt"],"answer":1,"explain":"The suspected fault is tool selection, so a component check on it localises the problem cheaply. A bigger end-to-end suite repeats the symptom, and retrieval is not suspected."},{"id":"w14-t03-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each stage to a component-level check.","pairs":[{"l":"Retriever","r":"Recall@k on labelled queries"},{"l":"Generator with fixed context","r":"Groundedness"},{"l":"Tool call","r":"Argument and tool selection accuracy"},{"l":"Safety guardrail","r":"Attack success rate on a probe set"}],"explain":"Each component check holds the other stages fixed so a drop is attributable to that stage."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why keep both component and end-to-end evals?","back":"Component scores localise a fault. End-to-end scores say whether users are affected. Only the second catches interaction effects such as context order or truncation."},{"front":"How do you test generation in isolation?","back":"Freeze the retrieved context (gold or recorded) and score only the answer, for example with groundedness and completeness."},{"front":"What are Hamel Husain's three eval levels?","back":"Fast assertions on every change, model and human evaluation on a cadence, and A/B tests after significant product changes. Cost rises at each level."}],"related":["w14-t01","w14-t06","w14-t11","w05-t17","w14-l1"],"refs":["tr6","r2","r3","r4"]},{"id":"w14-t04","order":4,"group":"Data","title":"Golden datasets","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A golden dataset is a versioned set of inputs with expected behaviour (reference answers, relevant document ids, tool sequences or a rubric) that every candidate is scored against. Its value comes from who labelled it, how each row was made and whether it stays held out.","useIf":"You need a repeatable yardstick to compare versions of a prompt, retriever, model or agent.","avoidIf":"You treat a public benchmark as your yardstick, or you let the team tune on the same rows it reports on.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **golden set** is the fixed set of cases you trust. Each row holds an input and a way to judge the result: a reference answer, relevant document ids for retrieval, an expected tool sequence, or a rubric. Four sources feed it, and each has a different failure profile."},{"t":"table","caption":"Four sources","head":["Source","Wins when","Loses when","Watch for"],"rows":[["Hand-curated by an expert","Quality of labels matters most","Expert time is scarce","Narrow coverage, one author's bias"],["Mined from production traces","The product is live","Before launch, or privacy blocks reuse","Skewed to common queries, PII"],["Synthetic from documents or prompts","Cold start, broad coverage","Rows are unreviewed","Easy or wrong rows"],["Public benchmark","Shortlisting models","You need your own data","Saturation and leakage"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Start small. Anthropic advises that 20 to 50 simple tasks drawn from real failures is a great start, because early changes have large effects; mature agents need larger and harder sets to detect small ones [[r17]]. Record **how each row was made** (hand, trace, synthetic) and the **dataset version**. LangSmith creates dataset versions automatically when examples change, supports tags so CI can target one, and uses splits to group examples [[r29]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"One row, with provenance","code":"{\"id\": \"c-0142\", \"input\": \"Can I carry over unused leave?\", \"reference\": \"Up to 5 days, expiring 31 March.\", \"relevant_docs\": [\"hr-leave-v3#4\"], \"source\": \"trace\", \"tags\": [\"policy\", \"edge\"], \"split\": \"heldout\", \"dataset_version\": \"2026-10-01\"}"},{"t":"p","md":"**Contamination** has two meanings. Models can have seen a public benchmark in training. GAIA withholds the answers to 300 of its 466 questions to power a leaderboard [[r33]], and SWE-bench Verified is a 500-sample human-validated subset of a 2,294-problem set [[r34]]. A survey describes benchmark data contamination as a significant issue that makes evaluation results unreliable [[tr7]]. Separately, your team can overfit its own set. Keep a **held-out** slice that no one tunes against and report on it."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Secondary source, checked 2026-10-13","md":"A secondary write-up reports that OpenAI stopped evaluating on SWE-bench Verified in February 2026 after saturation and flawed tests [[r48]]. The primary page could not be fetched, so treat the numbers as unconfirmed."}],"whereToUse":["A regression suite that gates every prompt, retriever or model change","Model selection and vendor-upgrade parity checks on your own tasks","A calibration set for judges, labelled by one accountable expert","The seed of an agent evaluation, with outcome checks per row","Foundry batch runs, within 2 MB per row and 100,000 rows per run [[r37]]"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You can name real failures to seed it","An expert can own the labelling guideline","You will compare versions repeatedly"],"avoid":["Growing it to thousands of unreviewed rows before it has an owner","Reporting on rows you also tuned against","Mixing synthetic and real rows with no source tag"],"flips":["The product goes live: shift weight from hand-written to trace-mined rows","Instructions or corpus change: regenerate and re-version the synthetic rows","A public benchmark saturates or leaks: rely on your private held-out slice"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Private golden set (this topic)","You need a trusted, repeatable yardstick","You have no expert time at all","Upkeep and labelling cost"],["Public benchmark","Comparing base models","Judging your product on your data","Leakage, saturation"],["Live trace sampling with a judge","Large traffic, no references","You need a stable baseline","The baseline moves with users"],["Rubric-only evaluation, no references","Open-ended tasks with no single answer","You need exact regression numbers","Judge noise"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Dev scores climb but production quality is flat","cause":"The team tuned prompts against the same rows it reports on","fix":"Keep a held-out split, tune on dev only and refresh the held-out slice on a schedule"},{"symptom":"Two experts disagree on half the labels","cause":"The guideline is ambiguous and no one owns it","fix":"Name one accountable expert, write the rubric, and keep disagreements as tagged ambiguous cases"},{"symptom":"The set stops matching real queries","cause":"No refresh path from production","fix":"Add triaged failing traces on a cadence and drop retired cases with a version bump"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t04-s1","title":"Pre-launch, two expert hours a week","situation":"A team is building an internal policy assistant. There are no traces, a corpus of 3,000 documents, and one policy expert with two hours a week.","constraints":["No production data","Scarce expert time","Launch in a month"],"unknowns":["Which questions caused trouble in manual trials?","Which documents change often?","Is the expert willing to own the guideline?"],"question":"How do you build the first golden set?","options":[{"name":"30 expert-reviewed cases from observed failures, plus reviewed synthetic cases, with a held-out slice","verdict":"best","why":"It uses scarce expert time on the hardest rows and borrows synthetic breadth, with provenance tags so you can tell them apart.","tradeoff":"Coverage is a guess until traces arrive."},{"name":"2,000 synthetic cases with no review","verdict":"poor","why":"Unreviewed rows can be easy or wrong, so scores mean little.","tradeoff":"Synthetic is fine at volume once a reviewed sample shows the generator is sound."},{"name":"Adopt a public question-answering benchmark","verdict":"good","why":"Free and comparable across models.","tradeoff":"It says little about your policies and may be leaked or saturated."}],"flips":[{"if":"Launch slips and a pilot produces traces","then":"Mine the pilot traces for new rows."},{"if":"Several experts are available","then":"Double-label a sample and measure agreement."}],"takeaway":"Few expert rows beat many unreviewed ones."},{"id":"w14-t04-s2","title":"Live product, large trace volume, privacy rules","situation":"A customer assistant has run for six months with a million logged conversations. Content includes personal data, and the policy allows reuse only after redaction.","constraints":["Plenty of traces","PII must be removed before reuse","The query mix shifts monthly"],"unknowns":["How reliable is redaction?","Which segments matter most to the business?","Are there known failure clusters?"],"question":"How do you refresh the golden set?","options":[{"name":"Stratified sample of redacted traces, expert-labelled, versioned monthly, with a frozen held-out core","verdict":"best","why":"Stratifying covers rare segments, redaction satisfies policy, and the frozen core keeps a stable baseline across months.","tradeoff":"Ongoing labelling effort and redaction review."},{"name":"Use the most frequent queries only","verdict":"poor","why":"It over-represents easy repeated queries and hides tail failures.","tradeoff":"Right for a latency or caching study where frequency is what matters."},{"name":"Keep the original set unchanged","verdict":"good","why":"A perfectly stable baseline.","tradeoff":"It drifts away from real users and gives false comfort."}],"flips":[{"if":"Redaction cannot be trusted","then":"Write synthetic look-alike cases from failure clusters instead of reusing text."},{"if":"The product shifts to a new domain","then":"Start a new set and retire the old one."}],"takeaway":"Stratify, redact, version and keep a frozen core."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t04-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What is the purpose of a held-out slice?","options":["To report results on rows nobody tuned against","To make the dataset larger","To store the synthetic rows","To speed up judge calls"],"answer":0,"explain":"A held-out slice detects overfitting to your own set. Size, storage and speed are unrelated."},{"id":"w14-t04-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"GAIA has 466 questions and withholds answers to 300 for its leaderboard. What percentage of questions is public developer data?","answer":35.6,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"%","hint":"Public = total minus withheld.","explain":"466 - 300 = 166 public questions. 166 / 466 = 0.356, so about 35.6%."},{"id":"w14-t04-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which practices keep a golden set trustworthy over time? Select all that apply.","options":["Tag each row as hand, trace or synthetic","Version the dataset when examples change","Add every production trace automatically","Keep a held-out slice out of tuning"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Provenance, versioning and a held-out slice protect meaning. Adding every trace without triage fills the set with noise and PII."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How large should a first golden set be?","back":"20 to 50 simple tasks drawn from real failures is a strong start. Large early effects need small samples. Grow it as changes get subtler."},{"front":"Two meanings of dataset contamination?","back":"Models may have seen a public benchmark in training, and your team may overfit its own set. Withhold answers or keep a held-out slice to control both."},{"front":"What should each golden row record?","back":"Input, reference or rubric, source (hand, trace, synthetic), tags, split and dataset version."}],"related":["w14-t05","w14-t12","w14-t14","w14-l2","w04-t01"],"refs":["r17","r29","r33","r34","r37","r48","tr7"]},{"id":"w14-t05","order":5,"group":"Data","title":"Synthetic data generation","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A model generates test inputs, and sometimes reference answers, from your documents or your agent's definition. It solves the cold start and widens coverage, but only after human review and with a tag that keeps it apart from real rows.","useIf":"You have no traces yet, expert time is scarce, or you need more edge-case coverage than hand writing allows.","avoidIf":"You plan to use unreviewed generated rows as the only evidence of quality, or to keep them after the prompt changed.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In **synthetic data generation** an LLM reads a source and writes cases. Foundry offers two task types: **Simple QnA**, single turn with `query` and `ground_truth`, and **Simulation seed**, multi-turn scenarios described by `test_case_description`. Inputs can be an agent definition, an inline prompt or a reference file, and `max_samples` accepts 1 to 1,000 [[r8]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Foundry's own practice advice is the checklist to follow: generate a small batch first and review the rows by hand, mirror the production system prompt, regenerate and version the dataset whenever instructions change, and combine synthetic with trace-derived data [[r8]]. RAGAS states the target as a dataset with high-quality samples, wide variety, enough rows for significant conclusions, and continual updates against drift [[tr4]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"A review-first pipeline","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Generate small","md":"Ten to twenty rows from a document or agent definition.","tag":"batch"},{"title":"Review by hand","md":"Check the question is answerable and the reference is correct.","tag":"expert"},{"title":"Filter and dedupe","md":"Drop near duplicates and trivial rows. Add hard and multi-hop cases.","tag":"quality"},{"title":"Tag and version","md":"Mark `source: synthetic`, record the generator and prompt version.","tag":"provenance"},{"title":"Scale and refresh","md":"Generate more once a reviewed sample is sound. Regenerate on prompt change.","tag":"operate"}]},{"t":"p","md":"Mind three biases (reasoning, not a vendor claim). A question written from a chunk reuses that chunk's words, so lexical retrieval looks better than it will on real user wording. A generator from the same model family tends to write questions that model finds easy. And a generated reference can be wrong, which then punishes a correct system."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Region support","md":"Synthetic data generation is supported only in a listed set of regions, for example East US 2, Sweden Central and West Europe, per the limits page checked on 2026-10-13 [[r37]]. Check before you plan on it."}],"whereToUse":["Cold start before launch, when there are no traces","Coverage of edge cases and rare document types that hand writing would miss","Multi-turn simulation seeds for conversational agents","Stress cases built from a knowledge base after a large content update","Augmenting a small expert-labelled set, never replacing it"],"whenToUse":{"use":["No traces exist or traffic is private","A reviewer can check a sample of rows","You can regenerate when instructions change"],"avoid":["Using it as the only evidence for a high-harm launch","Skipping review because generation was cheap","Keeping stale rows after the system prompt changed"],"flips":["Traces become available: shift weight to trace-mined rows and keep synthetic for gaps","Instructions or corpus change: regenerate and re-version","Rows are used to rank retrievers: add paraphrased and trace-style queries to reduce lexical overlap"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Synthetic generation (this topic)","Cold start, breadth, edge cases","Nobody reviews the rows","Easy or wrong rows, lexical overlap"],["Hand-written by an expert","Accuracy matters most","You need hundreds of rows fast","Expert time"],["Mined from traces","The product is live","Before launch or under strict privacy rules","Redaction work, skew to common queries"],["Paraphrasing existing rows","Testing robustness to wording","You need new content coverage","Near duplicates inflate size"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Retrieval scores look excellent on synthetic rows and poor in production","cause":"Questions were generated from the chunks and share their vocabulary","fix":"Add paraphrased and trace-style queries, and judge retrieval on a held-out real slice"},{"symptom":"A correct system fails many synthetic rows","cause":"The generated reference answer is wrong","fix":"Review a sample, compute an error rate for the generator, and drop or correct bad rows"},{"symptom":"The agent behaves differently from what the dataset assumes","cause":"The set was generated against an old system prompt","fix":"Mirror the production prompt and regenerate and version the set on every instruction change"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t05-s1","title":"New corpus, no users, one expert","situation":"A team indexes 5,000 product manuals for a support assistant. There is no traffic and one engineer who can review about 40 rows an hour.","constraints":["Cold start","Review capacity of about 40 rows an hour","Manuals change quarterly"],"unknowns":["How multi-hop are real questions?","How often do manuals change?","Is lexical overlap a risk for this corpus?"],"question":"How do you generate the first set?","options":[{"name":"Generate a small batch, review it, then scale with hard and multi-hop prompts and tag every row as synthetic","verdict":"best","why":"The review step measures the generator's error rate before volume, and tags keep synthetic apart from real data.","tradeoff":"Review time up front and a quarterly regeneration chore."},{"name":"Generate 3,000 rows and use them as-is","verdict":"poor","why":"Unreviewed rows carry wrong references and easy questions, so scores are unreliable.","tradeoff":"At volume it is fine only after a sampled review shows a low error rate."},{"name":"Write 40 rows by hand and stop","verdict":"good","why":"High accuracy and fast.","tradeoff":"Narrow coverage, and the set cannot detect small regressions."}],"flips":[{"if":"Real pilot queries arrive","then":"Add them and shrink the synthetic share."},{"if":"Manuals change monthly","then":"Automate regeneration and review a rolling sample."}],"takeaway":"Review before you scale."},{"id":"w14-t05-s2","title":"Multi-turn agent, tool budget limited","situation":"A scheduling agent handles multi-turn conversations and calls booking tools. Real transcripts contain personal data, so no one will approve reuse for a few months.","constraints":["Multi-turn behaviour","No approved real transcripts","Tool calls cost money in a sandbox"],"unknowns":["Which intents and edge cases matter most?","Can tools run against a sandbox?","How are success and failure defined?"],"question":"How do you create test conversations?","options":[{"name":"Simulation seeds describing scenarios, reviewed, run against sandboxed tools with outcome checks","verdict":"best","why":"Seeds cover multi-turn flows without real PII, and outcome checks avoid judging fragile wording.","tradeoff":"Simulated users are cleaner than real ones, so real transcripts remain the later target."},{"name":"Single-turn question and answer rows only","verdict":"poor","why":"They cannot exercise clarification, memory or tool sequences.","tradeoff":"Right for a stateless lookup assistant."},{"name":"Wait for approved transcripts","verdict":"good","why":"Most realistic data.","tradeoff":"Months with no regression gate."}],"flips":[{"if":"Transcripts are approved after redaction","then":"Mine them and use seeds for gaps."},{"if":"Tools cannot be sandboxed","then":"Mock tool responses and grade the call arguments only."}],"takeaway":"For multi-turn agents, seed scenarios, not single questions."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t05-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Retrieval metrics are very high on generated questions but weak on real user queries. Most likely cause?","options":["The embedding model is too small","The judge is biased toward long answers","The index is stale","Generated questions reuse the source chunks' wording, favouring lexical matching"],"answer":3,"explain":"Questions written from a chunk echo its vocabulary, which flatters retrieval. Model size, judge length bias and a stale index are possible in general but do not explain this gap."},{"id":"w14-t05-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A reviewer checks 40 generated rows and finds 6 with a wrong reference. What is the sample error rate, in percent?","answer":15,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"%","hint":"Wrong divided by reviewed.","explain":"6 / 40 = 0.15, so 15%. With only 40 rows the interval is wide, but 15% is too high to scale without improving the generator prompt."},{"id":"w14-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"The production system prompt was rewritten last week. What should you do with the synthetic set?","options":["Keep it, synthetic rows never go stale","Regenerate and version it against the new prompt","Delete all trace-derived rows","Only change the judge model"],"answer":1,"explain":"Foundry advises mirroring the production prompt and regenerating when instructions change. The old set describes old behaviour."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Foundry synthetic dataset task types?","back":"Simple QnA (single turn, query and ground_truth) and Simulation seed (multi-turn scenarios with test_case_description). max_samples accepts 1 to 1,000."},{"front":"Three bias risks of synthetic test data?","back":"Questions reuse chunk wording and flatter lexical retrieval, same-family generators write easy questions, and generated references can be wrong."},{"front":"When must synthetic rows be regenerated?","back":"Whenever the system instructions change. Review a small batch first and combine synthetic with trace-derived data."}],"related":["w14-t04","w14-t06","w14-t11","w14-l2","w05-t01"],"refs":["r8","r12","r37","tr4"]},{"id":"w14-t06","order":6,"group":"Metrics","title":"Retrieval metrics","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Rank metrics score a retriever against labelled relevant documents: precision@k, recall@k, hit rate, MRR and nDCG. Pick by label type and by whether one chunk or several are needed, and tie k to the chunks your prompt really receives.","useIf":"You are choosing or tuning a retriever, reranker or chunking strategy and have, or can build, relevance labels.","avoidIf":"You want to know whether the final answer is good. Retrieval metrics say nothing about generation.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **relevance judgment set** (qrels) lists, per query, which documents are relevant, binary or graded. Then, for the top k results:"},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**Precision@k**: relevant results in the top k divided by k. It needs no count of relevant documents but is the least stable standard measure: with only 8 relevant documents a perfect system scores at most 0.4 at k=20 [[r25]].","**Recall@k**: relevant results in the top k divided by all relevant documents. It shows whether the evidence reached the prompt.","**Hit rate@k**: share of queries with at least one relevant result in the top k. This is a conventional definition. No vendor evaluator page checked on 2026-10-13 defines it as a named evaluator.","**MRR**: mean of 1/rank of the first relevant result. Ranks 1, 3, none and 2 give (1 + 0.333 + 0 + 0.5) / 4 = 0.458 [[tr10]].","**nDCG@k**: for graded relevance, discounts gain by log2 of the rank and divides by the best possible ordering [[r25]]."]},{"t":"formula","title":"nDCG@k","expr":"nDCG@k = DCG@k / IDCG@k, with DCG@k = sum over i=1..k of gain(rel_i) / log2(i + 1)","vars":[{"k":"gain, linear","v":"rel_i"},{"k":"gain, exponential","v":"2^rel_i - 1, stresses highly relevant items"}],"example":"Relevance 3, 2, 0, 1 scores 0.985 with linear gain and 0.993 with exponential gain. Libraries differ, so never compare scores across tools without checking [[r46]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Foundry's Document Retrieval evaluator (`builtin.document_retrieval`) reports `ndcg@3`, `xdcg@3`, `fidelity`, `top1_relevance`, `top3_max_relevance`, `holes` and `holes_ratio`. It takes `retrieval_ground_truth` (document_id plus `query_relevance_label`) and `retrieved_documents`, with the label range defaulting to 0 to 4 [[r4]]. **Holes** are retrieved documents that have no judgment, and they distort rank metrics, so watch `holes_ratio`. BEIR spans 18 public datasets, and BM25 is a strong baseline [[r42]]."},{"t":"dials","id":"w14-t06-pick","title":"Pick the retrieval metric","prompt":"Flip the label type and the answer need. The ranking updates.","dials":[{"id":"labels","label":"Relevance labels","values":[{"id":"graded","label":"Graded"},{"id":"binary","label":"Binary"},{"id":"none","label":"None yet"}]},{"id":"need","label":"Evidence needed","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One chunk"},{"id":"many","label":"Several chunks"}]}],"options":[{"id":"hitmrr","name":"Hit rate and MRR","summary":"How early the first right chunk appears."},{"id":"recall","name":"Recall@k","summary":"Did the evidence reach the prompt?"},{"id":"ndcg","name":"nDCG@k","summary":"Order quality with graded relevance."},{"id":"prec","name":"Precision@k","summary":"Share of the top k that is relevant."},{"id":"judged","name":"Judged context metrics","summary":"LLM judges relevance without qrels."}],"scores":{"hitmrr":{"labels":{"graded":{"s":0,"why":"Works on a binary cut, ignores grades."},"binary":{"s":2,"why":"Fits one right answer per query."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs labels."}},"need":{"one":{"s":2,"why":"FAQ lookup cares about the first hit."},"many":{"s":-1,"why":"Ignores the second and third chunk."}}},"recall":{"labels":{"graded":{"s":1,"why":"Usable after a relevance cut."},"binary":{"s":2,"why":"Direct fit."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs the full relevant set."}},"need":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Works but overstates need."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Measures coverage of all evidence."}}},"ndcg":{"labels":{"graded":{"s":2,"why":"Built for graded relevance."},"binary":{"s":1,"why":"Reduces to a rank-aware binary score."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs labels."}},"need":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Rewards more than you need."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Rewards good ordering of many chunks."}}},"prec":{"labels":{"graded":{"s":0,"why":"Throws away the grades."},"binary":{"s":1,"why":"Simple and readable."},"none":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs labels."}},"need":{"one":{"s":-1,"why":"Penalises correct sparse results."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Unstable when relevant counts vary."}}},"judged":{"labels":{"graded":{"s":-1,"why":"Ignores labels you paid for."},"binary":{"s":0,"why":"Noisier than labels."},"none":{"s":2,"why":"The only option without qrels."}},"need":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Adequate."},"many":{"s":1,"why":"Checks claim coverage across chunks."}}}},"takeaway":"Labels decide the family. The evidence your prompt needs decides the member."}],"whereToUse":["Comparing BM25, dense and hybrid retrieval, or two embedding models","Tuning chunk size and overlap before touching the prompt","Measuring a reranker's lift in nDCG and recall at the real k","Regression gates after an index or schema change"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Labels exist or can be pooled for a query set","Retrieval and generation must be scored separately","k equals the number of chunks the prompt receives"],"avoid":["Using a single k=10 by habit when the prompt gets 4 chunks","Comparing nDCG across libraries without checking the gain formula","Treating high retrieval scores as proof the answer is right"],"flips":["No labels: use judged context metrics and start labelling a small set","Graded relevance exists: prefer nDCG over precision","Several documents must be combined: weight recall@k more than MRR"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Rank metrics with qrels","Labels exist, you compare retrievers","Labels are missing or incomplete","Labelling cost, holes"],["Judged context metrics (RAGAS, Foundry Retrieval)","No labels yet","You need exact regression numbers","Judge noise and cost"],["End-to-end answer correctness","You care about the final verdict","You must locate a retrieval fault","Hides the failing layer"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"nDCG differs between two tools for the same run","cause":"Linear versus exponential gain, or a different log base or cutoff","fix":"Fix one implementation, record its formula and re-baseline when you change it"},{"symptom":"Recall looks fine but answers lack detail","cause":"k in the metric is larger than the chunks actually sent to the prompt","fix":"Set k to the prompt's real chunk count and report recall at that k"},{"symptom":"Scores swing when you add new retrieved documents","cause":"Unjudged documents count as non-relevant, so holes distort the metric","fix":"Track `holes_ratio`, judge the top results of every system and pool them"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t06-s1","title":"FAQ lookup, one right answer","situation":"A help-centre assistant answers from a corpus where each question maps to one article. Binary labels exist for 400 queries. The prompt receives three chunks.","constraints":["One chunk is enough","Binary labels","k = 3 in production"],"unknowns":["Are there near duplicate articles?","How often is the right article unlabelled?"],"question":"Which metrics do you gate on?","options":[{"name":"Hit rate@3 and MRR","verdict":"best","why":"One right chunk is enough and both metrics reward finding it early at the real k.","tradeoff":"They ignore the second and third chunk, so they hide poor filler context."},{"name":"Precision@10","verdict":"poor","why":"With one relevant article the score is capped at 0.1 and k does not match the prompt.","tradeoff":"Precision suits result pages where several relevant items are expected."},{"name":"nDCG@3","verdict":"good","why":"Rank aware and fine for binary labels.","tradeoff":"Harder to explain, and adds little when grades do not exist."}],"flips":[{"if":"Answers combine several articles","then":"Add recall@k at the prompt's chunk count."},{"if":"No labels exist yet","then":"Use judged context metrics while labelling."}],"takeaway":"One right chunk points to hit rate and MRR at the real k."},{"id":"w14-t06-s2","title":"Research assistant, graded evidence","situation":"An assistant answers analyst questions by synthesising four to six passages from a large archive. Experts graded relevance from 0 to 4 on 150 queries. The prompt receives eight chunks.","constraints":["Several chunks needed","Graded labels","k = 8"],"unknowns":["How many relevant passages does a typical query have?","How large is holes_ratio?"],"question":"Which retrieval metrics and settings do you choose?","options":[{"name":"nDCG@8 plus recall@8, with holes_ratio tracked","verdict":"best","why":"Graded labels suit nDCG, coverage needs recall, and holes show where judgments are missing.","tradeoff":"Two numbers to explain, and labelling must keep pace with new documents."},{"name":"MRR only","verdict":"poor","why":"It scores only the first relevant passage while the task needs several.","tradeoff":"MRR is right when one passage answers the question."},{"name":"Judged context metrics only","verdict":"good","why":"No qrels upkeep.","tradeoff":"Noisier, and it ignores graded labels you already paid for."}],"flips":[{"if":"holes_ratio is high","then":"Judge the top results of each system and pool them before trusting the numbers."},{"if":"The prompt gets 3 chunks, not 8","then":"Move k to 3."}],"takeaway":"Graded labels and multi-document answers point to nDCG and recall."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t06-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Four queries have the first relevant result at ranks 1, 3, none (not retrieved) and 2. What is MRR to three decimals?","answer":0.458,"tolerancePct":1,"hint":"Reciprocal ranks are 1, 1/3, 0, 1/2.","explain":"(1 + 0.333 + 0 + 0.5) / 4 = 0.458. A query with no relevant result contributes 0."},{"id":"w14-t06-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"A query has 8 relevant documents. A perfect retriever is scored at k=20. What is the maximum precision@20?","options":["1.0","0.8","0.4","0.2"],"answer":2,"explain":"At most 8 of the 20 results can be relevant, so 8/20 = 0.4. Precision depends on how many relevant documents exist, which is why it is unstable across queries."},{"id":"w14-t06-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A retrieval evaluator reports holes_ratio = 0.35 after you add a new retriever. What does it mean?","options":["35% of queries failed","35% of retrieved documents have no relevance judgment, so rank metrics are distorted","The index lost 35% of documents","The judge disagrees with humans 35% of the time"],"answer":1,"explain":"Holes are retrieved documents with no judgment. Unjudged items are usually counted as non-relevant, which understates the new retriever."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"MRR in one line?","back":"Mean over queries of 1 divided by the rank of the first relevant result. Ranks 1, 3, none, 2 give 0.458."},{"front":"Why is precision@k unstable?","back":"It depends on how many relevant documents exist per query. With 8 relevant, a perfect system scores at most 0.4 at k=20."},{"front":"Why is nDCG not one number across tools?","back":"Linear gain uses rel_i, exponential gain uses 2^rel_i - 1. The same ranking can score 0.985 or 0.993."},{"front":"What are holes in Foundry's Document Retrieval evaluator?","back":"Retrieved documents with no relevance judgment. Track holes_ratio because unjudged documents distort rank metrics."}],"related":["w14-t07","w14-t03","w14-l3","w05-t17","w05-t04"],"refs":["r25","r46","r4","r42","tr10"]},{"id":"w14-t07","order":7,"group":"Metrics","title":"RAGAS metrics","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"RAGAS defines LLM-judged metrics for RAG: faithfulness and answer relevancy for the generator, context precision, context recall and entities recall for the retriever, and noise sensitivity for how irrelevant context corrupts answers. Each has inputs it needs and a blind spot.","useIf":"You need readable RAG metric definitions and have few or no relevance labels.","avoidIf":"You expect a faithfulness score to prove the answer is true, or you compare scores across different judge or embedding models.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"RAGAS splits RAG quality into the retriever half and the generator half, and scores most items with an LLM judge [[r20]]. Read each metric by what it divides by what."},{"t":"table","caption":"RAGAS metrics (docs for ragas 0.4.x, checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Metric","Score","Needs","Blind spot"],"highlightCol":0,"rows":[["**Faithfulness**","Claims in the response supported by retrieved context / claims in the response","`user_input`, `response`, `retrieved_contexts`","Consistency, not truth: wrong context gives a faithful wrong answer"],["**Answer (Response) relevancy**","Mean cosine similarity between the question and N questions an LLM generates from the response (default 3)","Response, plus an embedding model","Does not judge facts, and the score can leave 0 to 1"],["**Context precision**","Mean of precision@k over ranks that hold a relevant chunk, so order matters","Reference answer (`ContextUtilization` uses the response)","Needs a reference, judged by an LLM"],["**Context recall**","Claims in the reference attributable to the retrieved context / claims in the reference","Always a reference","Cannot run without references"],["**Context entities recall**","Entities shared by reference and context / entities in the reference","Reference","Suited to fact-heavy use cases only"],["**Noise sensitivity**","Share of response claims that are wrong against the reference while attributable to the context. Lower is better. `mode` is `relevant` (default) or `irrelevant`","Input, response, reference, contexts","Needs all four inputs"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Map symptoms to metrics. Low **context recall** says the evidence never reached the prompt: fix retrieval. High recall with low **faithfulness** says the generator ignored or invented beyond the context. High **noise sensitivity** says irrelevant chunks led the model to wrong claims. Low **answer relevancy** says the response drifted off the question."},{"t":"p","md":"Run each metric where its inputs exist. Faithfulness and answer relevancy need only the question, the response and the retrieved contexts (plus an embedding model for relevancy), so they also work on live traces. Context recall, context entities recall and noise sensitivity need a reference, so they belong to the offline golden set. Because an LLM judge and an embedding model produce the numbers, pin both and re-baseline when either changes."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"API and naming drift","md":"ragas 0.4.3 was released on 2026-01-13. The docs show the collections API (`ragas.metrics.collections`, `ascore` and `score`). The legacy `SingleTurnSample` API is deprecated in 0.4 and removed in 1.0. The docs title says Response Relevancy while the class is `AnswerRelevancy` [[r20]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Foundry's evaluators label the same two halves a **precision aspect** (Groundedness) and a **recall aspect** (Response Completeness, preview, needs `ground_truth`) [[r4]]. DeepEval offers Contextual Relevancy, Precision, Recall, Answer Relevancy and Faithfulness [[r28]]. Names differ across tools, so compare definitions, not labels."}],"whereToUse":["Diagnosing whether a bad RAG answer is a retrieval or a generation fault","Regression runs after a chunking, retriever or prompt change","Sampling production traces for faithfulness without references","Teaching teams a shared vocabulary for RAG quality"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Few relevance labels exist but reference answers do","You want one metric per failure type","You can afford judge calls on a sample"],"avoid":["Using faithfulness alone as a correctness gate","Mixing scores across different judge or embedding models","Running reference metrics on traces that have no reference"],"flips":["No references: use faithfulness, answer relevancy and ContextUtilization only","Exact regression numbers needed: prefer rank metrics with labels for retrieval","High stakes: add a reference-based correctness check and human review"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["RAGAS metrics","You want readable definitions and open source","You need an Azure-native portal and SLA","Judge cost, API changes between versions"],["Foundry RAG evaluators","You live in Foundry and want portal results","You need open definitions you can fork","Several are preview"],["Rank metrics with qrels","You have retrieval labels","Labels are missing","Labelling cost"],["DeepEval RAG metrics","You want pytest-style tests and thresholds","You need Azure integration","Default threshold 0.5 may not fit your bar"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Faithfulness is 0.95 but the answer is wrong","cause":"The retrieved document was wrong or outdated, and the answer matched it","fix":"Pair faithfulness with context recall and a reference-based correctness check"},{"symptom":"Scores jumped after a library upgrade","cause":"Metric implementation or default prompts changed, or the judge model changed","fix":"Pin the library, the judge and the embedding model, and re-baseline after any change"},{"symptom":"Answer relevancy is high for a confident wrong answer","cause":"It measures topical match to the question, not accuracy","fix":"Never use it as a correctness gate"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t07-s1","title":"Hallucination complaints, labelled references","situation":"Users report invented details in a policy assistant. A reference answer exists for 300 test questions. The team suspects retrieval but cannot prove it.","constraints":["References exist","Need a quick diagnosis","Judge budget for 300 items"],"unknowns":["Is context recall high or low?","Do wrong claims appear in the retrieved text?"],"question":"Which metrics split the cause?","options":[{"name":"Context recall, faithfulness and noise sensitivity on the 300 items","verdict":"best","why":"Low recall points to retrieval, high recall with low faithfulness points to the generator, and noise sensitivity shows whether irrelevant chunks mislead.","tradeoff":"Three judged metrics cost judge calls for 300 items."},{"name":"Answer relevancy alone","verdict":"poor","why":"It measures topical match and ignores both facts and context.","tradeoff":"Useful for catching answers that dodge the question."},{"name":"Faithfulness alone","verdict":"good","why":"Detects claims unsupported by context.","tradeoff":"It cannot say whether the context itself was complete or right."}],"flips":[{"if":"No reference answers exist","then":"Use faithfulness and ContextUtilization, and start writing references for the worst cases."},{"if":"Context recall is high and faithfulness is high","then":"Check that the reference and corpus are not outdated."}],"takeaway":"Pair a generator metric with a retriever metric to localise the fault."},{"id":"w14-t07-s2","title":"Production monitoring, no references, tight budget","situation":"A support assistant handles 50,000 conversations a day. There are no reference answers. Finance caps judge spend, and security allows reading only a redacted sample.","constraints":["No references","Spend cap","Redacted samples only"],"unknowns":["What sampling rate fits the budget?","Which topics are riskiest?"],"question":"Which RAGAS-style metrics do you run on live traffic?","options":[{"name":"Faithfulness and answer relevancy on a sampled, redacted slice","verdict":"best","why":"Both run without references and flag unsupported or off-topic answers at a cost you control by sampling.","tradeoff":"They cannot tell when retrieval missed the right document."},{"name":"Context recall and noise sensitivity on all traffic","verdict":"poor","why":"Both need references that live traffic lacks, and full coverage breaks the budget.","tradeoff":"They suit the offline golden set where references exist."},{"name":"Sample 1% for human review only","verdict":"good","why":"High-quality labels and no judge cost.","tradeoff":"Slow, and low coverage of rare failures."}],"flips":[{"if":"The budget grows","then":"Increase the sample and add ContextUtilization for retrieval health."},{"if":"Traffic is sensitive and cannot be read","then":"Score metadata and safety signals only."}],"takeaway":"Without references, use the reference-free metrics and sample."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does RAGAS faithfulness actually measure?","options":["Whether the answer is true in the world","Whether the retrieved chunks were ranked well","Whether the answer addresses the question","Whether the response's claims are supported by the retrieved context"],"answer":3,"explain":"Faithfulness divides context-supported claims by total claims. It checks consistency with context, not truth, ranking or relevance to the question."},{"id":"w14-t07-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Context recall is 0.35, faithfulness is 0.92. Where do you look first?","options":["Retrieval: the needed evidence rarely reaches the prompt","The generation prompt","The embedding model used for answer relevancy","The judge temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"Faithful answers over missing evidence mean the generator is following weak context. Low recall means the reference claims are not in the retrieved context."},{"id":"w14-t07-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which RAGAS metrics need a reference answer? Select all that apply.","options":["Context recall","Faithfulness","Noise sensitivity","Context precision (reference variant)"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"Context recall, noise sensitivity and the reference variant of context precision need a reference. Faithfulness needs only the input, response and retrieved contexts."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Faithfulness formula?","back":"Claims in the response supported by the retrieved context divided by total claims in the response. It checks consistency with context, not truth."},{"front":"How is answer (response) relevancy computed?","back":"An LLM generates N questions (default 3) from the response. The score is the mean cosine similarity of their embeddings to the user question. It needs an embedding model and ignores facts."},{"front":"What does noise sensitivity measure?","back":"The share of response claims that are wrong against the reference yet attributable to retrieved context. Lower is better. Modes: relevant (default) and irrelevant."}],"related":["w14-t06","w14-t08","w14-t09","w14-l3","w06-t02"],"refs":["r20","r4","r28","r14"]},{"id":"w14-t08","order":8,"group":"Metrics","title":"Foundry and Azure AI Evaluation evaluators","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Foundry ships built-in evaluators for quality, RAG, safety and agents, plus custom evaluators, through two SDK paths. Many are preview. Choose by what you can label, which tools your agent calls and whether a preview dependency is acceptable.","useIf":"You build on Foundry or Azure and want portal-visible scores, safety evaluators and CI-friendly runs.","avoidIf":"You need a feature with an SLA today and the evaluator you want is preview, or your agent uses tools the evaluators barely support.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The built-in catalog has families: general purpose (Coherence, Fluency), textual similarity (Similarity, F1, BLEU, GLEU, ROUGE, METEOR), RAG, risk and safety, agent, rubric (preview), Azure OpenAI graders and custom (preview). Microsoft suggests RAG = Retrieval + Groundedness + Relevance + Content Safety, and agents = Output Quality + Tool Use Quality + Rubric + Content Safety [[r2]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Families and status as checked on 2026-10-13","head":["Family","Examples","Needs ground truth?","Status note"],"rows":[["RAG","Retrieval, Document Retrieval, Groundedness, Relevance","Document Retrieval needs qrels, Retrieval does not","Groundedness Pro and Response Completeness are preview [[r4]]"],["Risk and safety","Violence, sexual, self-harm, hate and unfairness, protected material, code vulnerability, ungrounded attributes, indirect attack","No","Prohibited actions and sensitive data leakage (agents only) are preview. Output is a defect rate [[tr1]]"],["Agent, system level","Task Completion, Task Adherence, Intent Resolution, Customer Satisfaction, Task Navigation Efficiency","Navigation efficiency needs a ground-truth tool sequence","Most are preview [[r3]]"],["Agent, process level","Tool Call Accuracy, Tool Selection, Tool Input Accuracy, Tool Output Utilization, Tool Call Success","No","Composite `builtin.tool_use_quality` is preview"],["Custom","Code-based, prompt-based, endpoint-based","Your choice","Preview [[r41]]"]]},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**Composite evaluators.** `builtin.output_quality` batches Fluency, Coherence, Intent Resolution, Task Adherence, Groundedness and Task Completion into one judge call. `builtin.tool_use_quality` batches five tool metrics. Default pass thresholds are 3 on 1 to 5 scales and 1 on 0/1 scales, and the primary result passes only if every applicable component passes [[r3]].","**Levels.** `evaluation_level` is `turn` (default) or `conversation`. Conversation level needs the `messages` format and skips turn-only evaluators [[r2]].","**Custom evaluators (preview).** Code-based returns a float from Python `grade(sample, item)`. Prompt-based returns JSON with `result` and `reason`. Endpoint-based returns `score`, `reason`, `status` and optional `properties` [[r41]]. Foundry also recommends a **rubric evaluator** (preview) as the primary agent measure: weighted dimensions applied by an LLM judge, hand-written or generated from the agent's name, instructions and tools [[tr5]].","**Two SDK paths.** Classic `azure-ai-evaluation` (1.18.8 released 2026-10-09) with `evaluate()`, and new cloud evaluation through `azure-ai-projects` (2.2.0 or later, latest 2.8.0 on 2026-10-05) using `AIProjectClient` and `get_openai_client()`. The classic agent article does not apply to the new portal, so check each page's banner [[r38]]."]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Tool coverage and a deprecated grader","md":"Agent evaluators fully support File Search, Function tool, MCP and Knowledge-based MCP. For Azure AI Search, Bing Grounding, Bing Custom Search, SharePoint Grounding, Code Interpreter, Fabric Data Agent and Web Search, avoid `tool_call_accuracy`, `tool_input_accuracy`, `tool_output_utilization`, `tool_call_success` and `groundedness`. Quality Grader is deprecated even though one catalog page still lists it as preview [[r3]]."}],"whereToUse":["Foundry projects that want results in the portal with run comparison and t-tests [[r39]]","Agent Framework apps, with `LocalEvaluator` checks in CI and `FoundryEvals` for depth [[r10]]","Pre-launch safety measurement across hate, violence, self-harm and indirect attack","Scheduled and continuous evaluation of deployed agents (preview)","Custom evaluators where policy rules are domain specific"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Your models and agents are already on Foundry","You want safety evaluators backed by hosted models","You accept preview status for agent evaluators"],"avoid":["Relying on preview evaluators for a hard compliance gate without a fallback","Using tool evaluators on tools listed as limited support","Mixing classic and new-portal docs and code"],"flips":["Tools have limited evaluator support: use outcome checks and custom code evaluators","Fast deterministic CI needed: use local code checks first, cloud judges nightly","Portal not needed: open-source frameworks (RAGAS, DeepEval, promptfoo) are alternatives"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Foundry evaluators","Azure native, portal, safety, red teaming","You need no cloud dependency","Many preview, region limits, two SDK paths"],["Agent Framework `LocalEvaluator`","Fast code checks such as `keyword_check`, `tool_called_check`","You need semantic judgement","Only the checks you write"],["RAGAS","Readable RAG metric definitions","Agent and safety coverage","API changes between versions"],["DeepEval","Pytest style, many metrics and trajectory metrics","Azure integration","Default threshold 0.5, mostly judge based"],["promptfoo","Config-file evals and red teaming in CI","Rich portal analytics","OpenAI agreed to acquire it on 2026-03-09, open source continues [[r45]]"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Tool Call Accuracy returns odd scores for an agent that uses Azure AI Search","cause":"That tool has limited evaluator support","fix":"Avoid the four tool metrics and groundedness there, grade the outcome or write a custom evaluator"},{"symptom":"Code samples fail against the new portal","cause":"A classic SDK article was followed (`azure-ai-evaluation`) for a new-portal project","fix":"Read the banner, and use `azure-ai-projects` 2.x for new cloud evaluation"},{"symptom":"A build breaks after an evaluator was renamed or withdrawn","cause":"Preview feature, or Quality Grader which is deprecated","fix":"Pin versions, keep a fallback code check and watch the docs for status changes"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t08-s1","title":"Agent that calls Azure AI Search and Bing","situation":"A Foundry agent answers questions using Azure AI Search and Bing Grounding. The team wants a tool-quality score in the dashboard and a CI gate.","constraints":["Tools have limited evaluator support","Foundry-hosted","Needs a CI gate"],"unknowns":["Can the outcome be checked in code?","Is a rubric evaluator acceptable as preview?"],"question":"How do you score tool use?","options":[{"name":"Outcome check plus rubric or custom evaluator, with tool metrics skipped for those tools","verdict":"best","why":"The docs say to avoid the four tool metrics and groundedness for these tools, so grade what the agent achieved and encode your own rules.","tradeoff":"More effort than built-ins, and rubric and custom evaluators are preview."},{"name":"Use `builtin.tool_use_quality` anyway","verdict":"poor","why":"The composite includes metrics that are documented as unsupported for these tools, so scores mislead.","tradeoff":"Right for agents that use Function tool, MCP or File Search."},{"name":"Skip tool evaluation and rely on thumbs up and down","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap and shows user sentiment.","tradeoff":"No gate and no step-level signal."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent switches to function tools or MCP","then":"Use the built-in tool evaluators."},{"if":"Preview status is unacceptable","then":"Write code-based checks and keep them local."}],"takeaway":"Check tool coverage before you trust a tool metric."},{"id":"w14-t08-s2","title":"Fast CI on an Agent Framework app","situation":"A team builds with Agent Framework in Python and merges 20 times a day. Cloud judge runs take minutes and cost tokens. They still want safety coverage before release.","constraints":["Fast CI","Limited judge spend","Need safety evidence before release"],"unknowns":["Which checks are deterministic?","How often do releases happen?"],"question":"How do you split local and cloud evaluators?","options":[{"name":"`LocalEvaluator` code checks per PR, Foundry evaluators nightly and before release","verdict":"best","why":"Deterministic checks (`keyword_check`, `tool_called_check`) give fast feedback and cloud judges and safety evaluators run where cost and time are acceptable.","tradeoff":"Two systems to maintain."},{"name":"Cloud evaluators on every PR","verdict":"poor","why":"It slows merges and burns judge spend.","tradeoff":"Fine with a handful of merges a week."},{"name":"Safety evaluators only at launch","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap.","tradeoff":"Safety regressions between launches go unseen."}],"flips":[{"if":"Merges drop to a few a week","then":"Run cloud evaluators on each PR."},{"if":"A policy needs semantic judgement per change","then":"Add a small prompt-based custom evaluator to the PR suite."}],"takeaway":"Local checks for speed, cloud judges for depth."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t08-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"A composite evaluator such as `builtin.tool_use_quality` reports its primary result as pass when...","options":["The average component score is above the threshold","Any applicable component passes","Every applicable component passes","Tool Call Success passes"],"answer":2,"explain":"The primary result passes only if every applicable component passes. Averaging or any-pass would hide a failing component."},{"id":"w14-t08-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"This plan uses an evaluator on an agent that calls Bing Grounding and Code Interpreter. What is wrong?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"agent tools: Bing Grounding, Code Interpreter\nevaluators: tool_call_accuracy, tool_output_utilization, groundedness"},"options":["Too few evaluators","Evaluators cannot run on agents","tool_call_accuracy only works offline","Those tools have limited support, so these evaluators should be avoided"],"answer":3,"explain":"The docs list Bing Grounding and Code Interpreter as limited support and say to avoid these four tool metrics and groundedness for such conversations."},{"id":"w14-t08-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each custom evaluator kind to what it returns or runs.","pairs":[{"l":"Code-based","r":"Python `grade(sample, item)` returning a float from 0.0 to 1.0"},{"l":"Prompt-based","r":"A judge prompt returning JSON with `result` and `reason`"},{"l":"Endpoint-based","r":"Your HTTP endpoint returning `score`, `reason` and `status`"}],"explain":"These are the three custom evaluator kinds, all labelled preview as checked on 2026-10-13."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which two SDK paths exist for Foundry evaluation?","back":"Classic `azure-ai-evaluation` with `evaluate()`, and new cloud evaluation via `azure-ai-projects` 2.x with `AIProjectClient` and `get_openai_client()`. Docs mix both, so read the banner."},{"front":"Default pass thresholds for composite agent evaluators?","back":"3 on 1 to 5 scales and 1 on 0/1 scales. The primary result passes only if every applicable component passes."},{"front":"Which agent tools have limited evaluator support?","back":"Azure AI Search, Bing Grounding, Bing Custom Search, SharePoint Grounding, Code Interpreter, Fabric Data Agent and Web Search. Avoid four tool metrics and groundedness there."}],"related":["w14-t07","w14-t11","w14-t13","w12-t14","w11-t26","w14-l4"],"refs":["r2","r3","r4","r10","r38","r39","r41","r45","tr1","tr5"]},{"id":"w14-t09","order":9,"group":"Judges","title":"LLM-as-judge setups","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"An LLM judge scores outputs against a rubric. The setup, not the model alone, decides reliability: pass/fail with a critique, pairwise with order swapping, reference-guided grading, rubric prompts, or several judges. Pick by the decision you are making and the stakes.","useIf":"Quality is about meaning, tone or completeness that code cannot check, and you can calibrate the judge against human labels.","avoidIf":"A code check or an exact reference comparison already answers the question, or you cannot spare any human labels to validate the judge.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Strong judges agree with humans often. In the MT-Bench study GPT-4 exceeded 80% agreement, matching human-human agreement, and reached 85% against 81% among humans in the non-tie setup [[r21]]. That is a 2023 result, so validate your own judge. The setup matters more than the headline number."},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["**Pass/fail with a written critique.** Hamel Husain's critique shadowing has one principal domain expert give pass/fail judgments with critiques, then iterates the judge prompt until it agrees. He warns against uncalibrated 1 to 5 scales [[r16]].","**Pairwise.** Ask which of two answers is better. OpenAI's guidance says pairwise or pass/fail is more reliable than fine-grained scores, and to have the judge reason before it scores [[r18]].","**Reference-guided.** Give the judge the gold answer. On math questions in MT-Bench the judge failed 14 of 20 by default, 6 of 20 with chain-of-thought and 3 of 20 with a reference-guided judge [[r21]].","**Rubric or G-Eval.** Chain-of-thought plus a form-filling prompt reached a Spearman correlation of 0.514 with humans on summarization with GPT-4, with a noted bias toward LLM-generated text [[r22]].","**Several judges.** More cost, less single-model bias. Use it when stakes are high."]},{"t":"dials","id":"w14-t09-setup","title":"Pick a judge setup","prompt":"Flip the decision and the stakes. The ranking updates.","dials":[{"id":"decision","label":"Decision","values":[{"id":"gate","label":"Gate one system"},{"id":"rank","label":"Rank two candidates"},{"id":"ref","label":"Check against a reference"}]},{"id":"stakes","label":"Stakes","values":[{"id":"screen","label":"Screening"},{"id":"high","label":"High stakes"}]}],"options":[{"id":"point","name":"Pointwise 1 to 5","summary":"One absolute score per answer."},{"id":"binary","name":"Pass/fail with critique","summary":"Binary verdict plus written reason."},{"id":"pair","name":"Pairwise with order swap","summary":"Pick the better of two, both orders."},{"id":"refg","name":"Reference-guided","summary":"Judge sees the gold answer."},{"id":"panel","name":"Panel of judges","summary":"Several judges, vote or agreement."}],"scores":{"point":{"decision":{"gate":{"s":-1,"why":"Threshold is arbitrary on a loose scale."},"rank":{"s":0,"why":"Works if means are compared with intervals."},"ref":{"s":-1,"why":"Ignores the gold answer."}},"stakes":{"screen":{"s":1,"why":"Cheap trend signal."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"Too noisy to defend."}}},"binary":{"decision":{"gate":{"s":2,"why":"A threshold maps to a pass rate."},"rank":{"s":0,"why":"Coarse for close calls."},"ref":{"s":1,"why":"Add the reference to the prompt."}},"stakes":{"screen":{"s":1,"why":"Cheap and readable."},"high":{"s":0,"why":"Needs calibration on expert labels."}}},"pair":{"decision":{"gate":{"s":-1,"why":"Needs a baseline answer each time."},"rank":{"s":2,"why":"Built for comparing two versions."},"ref":{"s":0,"why":"Adds little over a reference."}},"stakes":{"screen":{"s":0,"why":"Order swap doubles calls."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Swap removes position bias."}}},"refg":{"decision":{"gate":{"s":1,"why":"Stable when gold answers exist."},"rank":{"s":0,"why":"Both answers vs one gold."},"ref":{"s":2,"why":"Designed for this."}},"stakes":{"screen":{"s":0,"why":"Needs gold answers per case."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"Cuts judge errors on checkable tasks."}}},"panel":{"decision":{"gate":{"s":0,"why":"Strong but costly."},"rank":{"s":1,"why":"Reduces single-judge bias."},"ref":{"s":0,"why":"Rarely needed with a reference."}},"stakes":{"screen":{"s":-2,"why":"Cost not justified."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"Best defence for contested calls."}}}},"takeaway":"Gating favours pass/fail, ranking favours pairwise, checkable tasks favour a reference. Panels earn their cost only at high stakes."},{"t":"code","lang":"text","title":"A pass/fail judge prompt (template)","code":"You are grading an answer for correctness against the policy excerpt.\nPolicy: {context}\nQuestion: {question}\nAnswer: {answer}\nFirst write a two sentence critique that cites the policy.\nThen output JSON: {\"verdict\": \"pass\" | \"fail\", \"reason\": \"...\"}\nFail if any claim is not supported by the policy."},{"t":"p","md":"Cost is roughly items x evaluators x trials x tokens per judge call (an estimate, not a vendor formula). Levers: batch criteria in one call (Foundry `output_quality` and `tool_use_quality`), sample, validate a cheaper judge and swap order only for pairwise. Foundry's target cost view excludes evaluator model usage, so budget the judge separately [[r39]]."}],"whereToUse":["Grading open-ended answers for correctness, completeness or tone","Comparing two prompt or model versions in a pairwise tournament","Online scoring of sampled production traces where no reference exists","Agent transcript checks against a policy rubric"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Meaning, not form, decides quality","You can label 50 to 100 cases to calibrate","Binary criteria can be written clearly"],"avoid":["Using a judge for something a regex or end-state check can verify","Trusting a 1 to 5 mean difference without an interval","Changing the judge prompt or model without re-checking agreement"],"flips":["Gold answers exist: add them to the judge prompt","Two candidates, one decision: switch from pointwise to pairwise","Stakes rise: add order swapping, a second judge family and human review of disagreements"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["LLM judge (this topic)","Meaning and tone decide quality","A deterministic check exists","Biases, drift, token cost"],["Code-based graders","Outputs are checkable: end state, schema, tool call","Free text with many phrasings","False failures"],["Human review","Calibration and high-stakes calls","Volume is large","Slow and expensive"],["Similarity metrics (BLEU, ROUGE, F1)","Short answers close to a reference","Paraphrase is valid","Weak correlation with meaning"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Judge scores cluster at 4 and 5 and never separate versions","cause":"An uncalibrated 1 to 5 scale","fix":"Use binary criteria with critiques and check true positive and negative rates against expert labels"},{"symptom":"Scores shift after a provider update with no code change","cause":"The judge model alias or prompt changed","fix":"Pin the judge version, store the judge prompt in version control and re-run a calibration set after any change"},{"symptom":"The judge favours one candidate regardless of quality","cause":"Position or self-preference bias","fix":"Swap order, use a different model family and see the bias topic"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t09-s1","title":"Choosing between two prompt versions weekly","situation":"A team iterates on a summarisation prompt and wants to know, each week, which of two candidate prompts is better. There are no reference summaries, and 300 test documents.","constraints":["Two candidates","No references","300 items","Moderate stakes"],"unknowns":["How close are the candidates usually?","How many judge calls fit the budget?"],"question":"Which judge setup do you use?","options":[{"name":"Pairwise comparison with both orders, counting a win only if it holds both ways","verdict":"best","why":"It matches a ranking decision and removes position bias, at twice the calls.","tradeoff":"Doubled cost and ties when the swap disagrees."},{"name":"Score each summary 1 to 5 and compare means","verdict":"poor","why":"A loose scale plus no interval means small differences are noise.","tradeoff":"Fine for a quick trend check when decisions are cheap to reverse."},{"name":"A panel of three judges per item","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces single-judge bias.","tradeoff":"Three times the cost for a moderate-stakes decision."}],"flips":[{"if":"The decision becomes a release gate for one system","then":"Move to pass/fail criteria with a threshold."},{"if":"Reference summaries become available","then":"Use a reference-guided judge."}],"takeaway":"Ranking two candidates is a pairwise job."},{"id":"w14-t09-s2","title":"Release gate over policy answers, capped budget","situation":"An assistant answers from a policy library. A CI job gates every release on 200 items. Gold answers exist for all, and the judge budget is capped.","constraints":["Gate a single system","Gold answers exist","Budget cap"],"unknowns":["What pass rate is acceptable?","Which cheaper judge has been validated?"],"question":"How do you set up the judge?","options":[{"name":"Reference-guided pass/fail judge with critique, calibrated on an expert-labelled sample","verdict":"best","why":"A gate wants a binary verdict, and gold answers cut judge errors. Calibration keeps it honest.","tradeoff":"Needs gold answers and an initial labelling effort."},{"name":"Pairwise against last release","verdict":"good","why":"Detects regressions relative to the baseline.","tradeoff":"Does not give an absolute pass rate and doubles calls."},{"name":"A panel of five judges on every item","verdict":"poor","why":"Cost blows the cap for a routine gate.","tradeoff":"Panels suit rare, contested, high-stakes decisions."}],"flips":[{"if":"No gold answers exist","then":"Use pass/fail criteria derived from the policy text with critique."},{"if":"Stakes become regulatory","then":"Add a second judge family and sampled human review."}],"takeaway":"A gate wants binary verdicts and references, not a panel."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t09-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"You must rank two prompt versions with no reference answers. Which judge setup fits best?","options":["Pointwise 1 to 5 mean difference","Panel of five on every item","Pairwise comparison with both orders","Similarity to the old output"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pairwise matches a ranking decision and swapping removes position bias. A 1 to 5 mean is noisy, a panel is costly for a moderate decision, and similarity to the old output rewards sameness."},{"id":"w14-t09-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"In the MT-Bench math grading test the default judge failed 14 of 20 problems and a reference-guided judge failed 3 of 20. By how many percentage points did the failure rate fall?","answer":55,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"points","hint":"14/20 and 3/20 as percentages.","explain":"14/20 = 70% and 3/20 = 15%, a fall of 55 percentage points."},{"id":"w14-t09-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which choices follow the published guidance for reliable judges? Select all that apply.","options":["Prefer pass/fail or pairwise over fine-grained scores","Let the judge reason before it scores","Optimise judge cost before checking agreement with humans","Control for response length"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"OpenAI advises pairwise or pass/fail, reasoning before scoring, controlling length, and validating agreement with human labels before optimising cost."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why prefer pass/fail or pairwise over a 1 to 5 score?","back":"Fine-grained scales are noisy and uncalibrated. Binary or pairwise decisions are easier to define, calibrate and gate on."},{"front":"What is critique shadowing?","back":"One principal domain expert gives pass/fail judgments with written critiques, and the judge prompt is iterated until it agrees with them."},{"front":"What does a reference-guided judge change?","back":"The judge sees the gold answer. In MT-Bench math it cut judge failures from 14 of 20 to 3 of 20."}],"related":["w14-t10","w14-t07","w14-t14","w14-l4","w06-t02"],"refs":["r21","r22","r16","r18","r39"]},{"id":"w14-t10","order":10,"group":"Judges","title":"Judge biases and calibration","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"LLM judges have measurable habits: they favour the first position, longer text and their own outputs. You defend with order swapping, references, a different judge family and, above all, calibration against labels from one accountable human expert, reported as true positive and true negative rates.","useIf":"A judge score will gate a release or rank candidates, so its errors must be measured and bounded.","avoidIf":"You only need a rough trend and the judge's absolute accuracy does not matter.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Treat a judge as an instrument with known error. The 2023 MT-Bench study measured several habits, and newer judges are likely better, but the mechanisms remain."},{"t":"table","caption":"Biases, evidence and defences","head":["Bias","Evidence","Defence","Cost"],"rows":[["**Position**","Consistency after swapping answer order: GPT-4 65.0%, GPT-3.5 46.2%, Claude-v1 23.8% with the default prompt. Reordering let Vicuna-13B beat ChatGPT on 66 of 80 queries [[r21]] [[r31]]","Judge twice with swapped order and count a win only if it holds both ways, or randomise order","2x calls"],["**Verbosity**","A repetitive-list attack fooled Claude-v1 and GPT-3.5 91.3% of the time and GPT-4 8.7% [[r21]]","Control for length, grade against criteria, not length","Prompt design"],["**Self-preference**","GPT-4 won about 10% more often and Claude-v1 about 25% more on their own outputs, though the paper called it inconclusive. Judges can recognise their own text, and self-recognition tracks self-preference [[r32]]","Judge with a different model family","Second deployment"],["**Many more**","The CALM framework quantifies 12 biases [[r44]]","Red team your judge with perturbations","Eval effort"]]},{"t":"p","md":"A few-shot judge lifted GPT-4's swap consistency from 65.0% to 77.5% at about 4x prompt cost, so mitigation has a price [[r21]]. Proposed calibration schemes include multiple evidence, balanced position and human-in-the-loop calibration [[r31]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Do not quote these numbers for your own judge: measure it. Two cheap probes work on any judge. A **swap test** runs each pair in both orders and counts how often the verdict flips, and a high flip rate means position is driving the result. A **padding test** adds filler to one answer and checks whether the verdict moves toward the longer text. Both are reasoning-based probes, not vendor features."},{"t":"flow","title":"Calibrate to a human","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Label","md":"One principal expert gives pass/fail plus a written critique on 50 to 100 cases.","tag":"human"},{"title":"Judge","md":"Run the judge on the same cases with the draft prompt.","tag":"model"},{"title":"Compare","md":"Build a confusion matrix. Report true positive and true negative rates.","tag":"measure"},{"title":"Fix","md":"Read the disagreements, edit the rubric or add examples, rerun.","tag":"iterate"},{"title":"Freeze","md":"Pin judge model and prompt. Re-check on a held-out labelled set.","tag":"version"}]},{"t":"p","md":"With imbalanced classes, raw agreement misleads. If 95% of outputs pass, a judge that always says pass scores 95% agreement and a true negative rate of 0%. Hamel Husain recommends reporting true positive and true negative rates and reached over 90% agreement in three iterations on one example [[r16]]. EvalGen research names **criteria drift**: people need criteria to grade outputs, but grading outputs helps them define criteria, so expect to revise the rubric [[tr3]]."}],"whereToUse":["Before a judge score becomes a CI gate","When ranking models from the same vendor family as the judge","When switching judge model or prompt, to prove parity","In regulated settings where evaluation evidence must be defensible"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A judge score will gate or rank","An expert can label a calibration set","Class balance is skewed"],"avoid":["Calibrating on rows you also tuned the prompt against","Reporting raw agreement alone","Treating 2023 bias numbers as current for your judge"],"flips":["Pairwise ranking: swap order every time","Judge and generator share a family: add a different-family judge for the final call","Stakes high: use two judge families and human review of disagreements"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Order swapping","Pairwise comparisons","Pointwise scoring","2x calls, tie cases"],["Reference-guided grading","Gold answers exist","Open-ended tasks","Reference upkeep"],["Different-family judge","Self-preference is a concern","One vendor only","Second deployment, inconsistent styles"],["Human-calibrated judge","Stakes and gates","No expert time","Labelling effort"],["Multiple judges","Contested high-stakes calls","Routine screening","Cost multiple"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The judge reports 92% agreement yet misses most bad outputs","cause":"90% of outputs pass, so agreement is dominated by the majority class","fix":"Report true positive and true negative rates and oversample failures in the calibration set"},{"symptom":"Judge agreement fell after a provider update","cause":"The judge model changed behind an alias, or the prompt drifted","fix":"Pin the version, keep a frozen calibration set and re-run it on every change"},{"symptom":"Calibration looks perfect but production disagreement is high","cause":"The prompt was tuned on the same cases used to report agreement","fix":"Hold out a labelled set the prompt never saw"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t10-s1","title":"Same vendor for generator and judge","situation":"A team has one approved model deployment and uses it for both generation and judging. They compare two prompt versions and one wins by 6 points. Budget allows one extra deployment.","constraints":["Same model family","Moderate stakes","One extra deployment possible"],"unknowns":["Do both candidates have similar style?","How large is the calibration set?"],"question":"How do you reduce self-preference risk?","options":[{"name":"Add a judge from a different family and require both to agree, with order swapping","verdict":"best","why":"A different family removes the shared-style advantage, and swapping removes position bias.","tradeoff":"Second deployment, and disagreements need a tie-break rule."},{"name":"Keep the same judge, lower temperature","verdict":"poor","why":"Temperature does not remove a preference for one's own style.","tradeoff":"It helps only run-to-run noise."},{"name":"Ask a human to review all 300 pairs","verdict":"good","why":"Unbiased.","tradeoff":"Slow and costly for a routine comparison."}],"flips":[{"if":"Only one deployment is allowed","then":"Use reference-guided criteria and human review of a sample."},{"if":"Candidates differ strongly in length","then":"Control for length before comparing."}],"takeaway":"Break shared-family preference with a different judge."},{"id":"w14-t10-s2","title":"High-stakes gate, small expert budget","situation":"An assistant gives guidance in a regulated domain. An expert can label 80 cases once, and the release gate must be defensible to an auditor.","constraints":["High stakes","80 labelled cases","Audit trail required"],"unknowns":["What is the pass rate in the labelled cases?","Can the expert write critiques?"],"question":"How do you build a gate you can defend?","options":[{"name":"Expert pass/fail with critiques, judge calibrated on a train and a held-out split, TPR and TNR reported, versions pinned","verdict":"best","why":"It is auditable, measures the error that matters and avoids overfitting the prompt.","tradeoff":"Expert time and a small held-out set that gives wide intervals."},{"name":"Report raw agreement only","verdict":"poor","why":"A majority-pass dataset makes the number meaningless.","tradeoff":"Raw agreement is fine when classes are balanced."},{"name":"A panel of three judges and no human labels","verdict":"good","why":"Reduces single-model bias.","tradeoff":"No evidence that the panel agrees with the expert."}],"flips":[{"if":"Failures are rare in labelled data","then":"Oversample failures so TNR has enough cases."},{"if":"The expert can label more cases later","then":"Grow the held-out set to tighten intervals."}],"takeaway":"Defensible means calibrated, held out and version pinned."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t10-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"An expert labels 200 outputs: 180 pass, 20 fail. The judge says pass on 175 of the 180 passes and fail on 8 of the 20 fails. What is the judge's true negative rate (fails caught), in percent?","answer":40,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"%","hint":"Caught fails divided by all true fails.","explain":"8 / 20 = 40%. Raw agreement is (175 + 8) / 200 = 91.5%, which looks good and hides that most bad outputs slip through."},{"id":"w14-t10-q2","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A pairwise judge picks the first-listed answer 70% of the time on random pairs. What is the cheapest valid fix?","options":["Raise temperature","Judge both orders and count a win only if it holds both ways","Use a longer rubric","Use only the second position"],"answer":1,"explain":"Swapping order and requiring agreement cancels position preference at the price of twice the calls. Temperature and rubric length do not address position, and always using the second position only flips the bias."},{"id":"w14-t10-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which are reasons to calibrate a judge on a held-out labelled set rather than the set used to tune the prompt? Select all that apply.","options":["The prompt can overfit the tuning cases","It gives an honest estimate of true positive and negative rates","It removes position bias","It reveals drift after a judge model update"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Held-out data guards against overfitting, gives honest rates and exposes drift when rerun. It does not remove position bias, which needs order swapping."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is raw agreement misleading for judges?","back":"With imbalanced classes it is dominated by the majority. If 95% pass, an always-pass judge has 95% agreement and a 0% true negative rate. Report TPR and TNR."},{"front":"How do you fix position bias in pairwise judging?","back":"Judge both orders and count a win only if it holds both ways, or randomise order. Cost is about double the calls."},{"front":"What is self-preference bias?","back":"A judge favouring its own model family's outputs. MT-Bench saw about 10% (GPT-4) and 25% (Claude-v1) higher win rates but called it inconclusive. Use a different judge family."}],"related":["w14-t09","w14-t14","w14-t12","w14-l4"],"refs":["r21","r31","r32","r44","r16","tr3"]},{"id":"w14-t11","order":11,"group":"Agents and safety","title":"Agent trajectory and tool-call evaluation","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"An agent run is a trajectory: model steps, tool calls with arguments, tool results and a final answer. Grade the outcome first, then add process checks on tool selection, arguments and output use so a failure points at a step. Rigid expected sequences punish valid alternatives.","useIf":"An agent chooses tools and takes multiple steps, and you need to know both whether it succeeded and where it went wrong.","avoidIf":"The task has many valid paths and you are tempted to demand one exact tool sequence.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **trajectory** is the ordered record of an agent run. You can grade it at two levels. **Outcome** grading checks the end state: did the booking exist, was the file changed, was the ticket closed. tau-bench grades by comparing the final database state with the annotated goal state [[r23]]. **Process** grading checks steps: was the right tool chosen, were the arguments correct, did the agent use the tool's output."},{"t":"p","md":"Anthropic recommends grading the outcome, and the transcript where useful, with code-based, model-based or human graders. It warns that a rigid grader can mark a valid alternative wrong: a model found a policy loophole on a tau2-bench flight task and was scored as failing. It also says to isolate each trial in a clean environment, because shared state causes correlated failures [[r17]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-t11-d1","title":"A trajectory and its checks","alt":"A task leads to a plan, tool calls and a final answer. Process checks attach to tool selection, arguments and output use. An outcome check compares the end state.","src":"flowchart LR\n  T[Task] --> P[Plan]\n  P --> C1[Tool call 1]\n  C1 --> C2[Tool call 2]\n  C2 --> A[Final answer]\n  C1 -.-> K1[Selection and arguments]\n  C2 -.-> K2[Output use]\n  A -.-> K3[Outcome check]"},{"t":"table","caption":"What to grade","head":["Check","Catches","Misses","Example tooling"],"rows":[["**Outcome (end state)**","Real task failure, loopholes handled fairly","Which step failed","Code check on environment state"],["**Tool selection**","Wrong tool for the intent","Right tool, wrong inputs","Foundry Tool Selection; DeepEval Tool Correctness"],["**Argument accuracy**","Bad parameters, missing fields","Poor use of results","Tool Input Accuracy; Argument Correctness"],["**Output utilisation**","Ignoring or misreading a result","Wrong tool choice","Tool Output Utilization"],["**Efficiency and plan**","Loops, wasted steps","Outcome quality","Task Navigation Efficiency, Step Efficiency"],["**Transcript judge**","Policy or tone violations","Mechanical errors","Task Adherence, Intent Resolution"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Foundry splits agent evaluators into system level (Task Completion, Customer Satisfaction, Task Adherence, Task Navigation Efficiency, Intent Resolution) and process level (Tool Call Accuracy, Tool Selection, Tool Input Accuracy, Tool Output Utilization, Tool Call Success). Navigation efficiency needs a ground-truth tool sequence. Most of these are preview as checked on 2026-10-13, and some tools have limited support [[r3]]. DeepEval offers trajectory metrics (Task Completion, Step Efficiency, Plan Adherence, Plan Quality) and component metrics [[r28]]. Agent Framework's `LocalEvaluator` has `tool_called_check` for a cheap deterministic test [[r10]]."}],"whereToUse":["Tool-using assistants where a wrong call changes real data","Multi-agent workflows where you must find which agent or step failed","Regression suites for agents with stable, checkable tool schemas","Policy-bound flows (refunds, access requests) where order matters","Production traces, to pick runs with loops or errors for triage"],"whenToUse":{"use":["An end state is checkable in a sandbox","A step-level failure would otherwise be hidden","The tool set has a stable schema"],"avoid":["Requiring one exact trajectory when several are valid","Reusing mutable environment state across trials","Running tool evaluators on tools with limited support"],"flips":["The policy mandates an order (identity check before refund): assert the sequence","Many valid paths: grade outcome and a few invariants (never call X before Y)","Tools unsupported by built-in evaluators: write code-based graders"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Outcome-only grading","Several valid paths","You must locate the failing step","Low debuggability"],["Exact trajectory match","A policy fixes the order","Paths vary","False failures"],["Step-level evaluators","You need to localise faults","Tools lack evaluator support","Judge cost, preview status"],["Transcript LLM judge","Policy and tone","Mechanical correctness","Judge bias"],["Human trace review","Finding new failure types","Volume is high","Slow"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A correct, creative run is marked a failure","cause":"The grader demanded one expected tool sequence","fix":"Grade the outcome and invariants, not the exact path"},{"symptom":"Trials pass or fail together, in clusters","cause":"Shared mutable state between runs","fix":"Start each trial from a clean environment"},{"symptom":"Tool metrics look strangely high or low","cause":"The agent uses tools with limited evaluator support","fix":"Check the tool coverage list and fall back to outcome or custom checks"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t11-s1","title":"Refund agent with a mandatory policy order","situation":"A refund agent must verify identity, check the order, then issue the refund, in that order, by company policy. Real money moves, and a sandbox mirrors the order system.","constraints":["Strict order required","High harm","Sandbox available"],"unknowns":["Can the refund state be read in the sandbox?","Which violations are unacceptable?"],"question":"What do you grade?","options":[{"name":"Outcome check on sandbox state plus assertions on the required order and arguments","verdict":"best","why":"Policy fixes the order, so the order is a requirement, and the outcome check confirms the right refund occurred.","tradeoff":"Brittle if policy changes, so update assertions with the policy."},{"name":"A transcript judge scoring overall helpfulness","verdict":"poor","why":"A helpful but non-compliant run could pass.","tradeoff":"Good for tone or satisfaction checks on top of the hard rules."},{"name":"Outcome only","verdict":"good","why":"Catches wrong refunds.","tradeoff":"A correct refund issued without verifying identity would pass."}],"flips":[{"if":"Policy allows verification in any order","then":"Drop the order assertion and keep invariants."},{"if":"Refunds cannot be sandboxed","then":"Mock the tool and check arguments only."}],"takeaway":"When policy fixes the order, assert it."},{"id":"w14-t11-s2","title":"Open-ended research agent with Bing and search tools","situation":"A research agent searches the web and an index, then writes a briefing. Many tool sequences are valid. Foundry evaluators have limited support for these tools, and judge budget is modest.","constraints":["Many valid paths","Limited tool evaluator support","Modest budget"],"unknowns":["How is briefing quality defined?","Which failures are most costly?"],"question":"How do you evaluate it?","options":[{"name":"Rubric judge on the briefing plus invariants (no unsupported claims, cites sources) and sampled trace review","verdict":"best","why":"It grades what matters without forcing a path and avoids evaluators documented as limited for these tools.","tradeoff":"Judge cost and calibration work, and step-level debugging is manual."},{"name":"Exact expected trajectory per query","verdict":"poor","why":"Valid alternative paths would be marked wrong.","tradeoff":"Right for a fixed-workflow agent."},{"name":"Built-in `tool_call_accuracy` on every run","verdict":"good","why":"A step-level signal.","tradeoff":"Documented as limited for these tools, so scores can mislead."}],"flips":[{"if":"The agent moves to function tools or MCP","then":"Add the built-in tool evaluators."},{"if":"A source list is required","then":"Add code checks that cited URLs were retrieved."}],"takeaway":"Many paths mean grade outcomes and invariants."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t11-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"An agent solves a task by an unexpected but valid tool sequence and fails your exact-trajectory test. What is the better design?","options":["Add the new sequence to the expected list forever","Lower the pass threshold","Remove the test","Grade the outcome, plus invariants such as never calling a tool before verification"],"answer":3,"explain":"Outcome and invariants allow valid alternatives while enforcing what matters. Listing sequences forever does not scale, and lowering the threshold or removing the test hides real failures."},{"id":"w14-t11-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Trial results cluster: when one trial fails, the next several fail as well, though the agent code is unchanged. What is the likely cause?","options":["Trials share mutable environment state","The judge is biased toward short answers","Tool Selection is mislabelled","The dataset is too small"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shared state creates correlated failures. Isolating each trial in a clean environment is the fix. The other causes do not create clustered failures across trials."},{"id":"w14-t11-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each check to what it catches.","pairs":[{"l":"Outcome check","r":"The task did not reach the right end state"},{"l":"Tool selection","r":"The agent picked the wrong tool for the intent"},{"l":"Tool input accuracy","r":"Right tool, wrong or missing arguments"},{"l":"Tool output utilisation","r":"The agent ignored or misread a tool result"}],"explain":"Process checks localise a step. The outcome check tells you whether the user got what they needed."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Outcome vs process grading for agents?","back":"Outcome checks the end state. Process checks tool selection, arguments and output use. Grade outcome first, add process checks to localise failures."},{"front":"Why avoid demanding one exact tool sequence?","back":"Several paths can be valid, and a rigid grader marks correct runs wrong. Anthropic cites a model finding a policy loophole on a tau2-bench task and being graded wrong."},{"front":"Why isolate each trial in a clean environment?","back":"Shared state creates correlated failures between trials, so results stop being independent."}],"related":["w14-t08","w14-t12","w14-t03","w10-t06","w14-l5"],"refs":["r3","r10","r17","r23","r28"]},{"id":"w14-t12","order":12,"group":"Agents and safety","title":"Non-determinism, pass^k and significance","minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Run each task several times. Report pass@k when one success is enough and pass^k when every run must succeed. Attach a confidence interval to every pass rate and compare versions paired, because small eval sets cannot see small differences.","useIf":"An agent or prompt change moves a score by a few points and you must decide whether it is real.","avoidIf":"The system is deterministic and fully checkable, where one run is the truth.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Scores vary because sampling, tools, the environment and the judge are all noisy. So run each task n times. Two summaries answer different questions: **pass@k** is the chance at least one of k trials succeeds, and **pass^k** is the chance all k succeed. tau-bench defines pass^k and gives unbiased estimators from n trials with c successes per task, averaged over tasks [[r23]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Unbiased estimators (tau-bench)","expr":"pass^k = mean over tasks of C(c, k) / C(n, k);  pass@k = 1 - mean over tasks of C(n - c, k) / C(n, k)","vars":[{"k":"n","v":"trials run per task"},{"k":"c","v":"successful trials for that task"},{"k":"k","v":"trials that must (pass^k) or may (pass@k) succeed"}],"example":"Ten trials, seven successes, k = 3: pass^3 = C(7,3) / C(10,3) = 35 / 120 = 0.292. pass^1 equals pass@1 [[r23]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Reliability decays fast. If every trial succeeded with the same probability p, pass^k would be p^k. This is a computed estimate, because real tasks vary in difficulty."},{"t":"table","caption":"pass^k = p^k (computed)","head":["Per-trial p","k = 3","k = 5","k = 8"],"rows":[["0.90","0.729","0.590","0.430"],["0.75","0.422","0.237","0.100"]]},{"t":"p","md":"In tau-bench, gpt-4o with function calling scored about 61% in retail and 35% in airline at pass^1, and about 25% in retail at pass^8 (2024 model) [[r23]]. Use pass@k when one success is enough, for example code the user can retry, and pass^k when consistency matters, such as a customer-facing agent [[r17]]. The gap can be large: the Codex paper's abstract reports 28.8% of problems solved with one sample and 70.2% with 100 samples per problem, and its Table 2 lists 72.3% at 100 samples for the 12B model [[tr8]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"95% half-width for an 80% pass rate (computed: 1.96 x sqrt(p(1-p)/n) [[r24]])","head":["Items n","50","100","200","400","1,000"],"rows":[["Half-width (points)","11.1","7.8","5.5","3.9","2.5"]]},{"t":"p","md":"At n = 100, an 80% pass rate is anywhere from about 72% to 88%, so a 3-point gain is noise. Anthropic's error-bar guidance adds clustered standard errors when questions are grouped (they can exceed 3x the naive value), paired question-level differences when comparing two models, and power analysis [[r24]]. For small n or rates near 0 or 100%, the Wilson interval behaves better than the normal approximation, and the rule of three says zero failures in n runs bounds the failure rate near 3/n [[tr9]]. Foundry's compare view marks a change strong at p <= 0.001 and weak at 0.001 < p <= 0.05 [[r39]]."}],"whereToUse":["Customer-facing agents, where pass^k with k near the number of repeat uses matters","Code or search assistants, where pass@k shows the value of retry or best-of-n","Release gates that must say a change is real, not noise","Model upgrade decisions between two close candidates"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Outputs vary between runs","A decision hinges on a few points","Reliability, not peak ability, is the requirement"],"avoid":["Reporting one run of a stochastic agent as the score","Comparing two means without an interval or pairing","Computing p^k from an average p and calling it measured"],"flips":["User can retry or pick the best: pass@k","Every use must succeed: pass^k","Eval set under about 100 items: report intervals and treat small deltas as unproven","Gap target is small: size the set with a power analysis"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["pass^k","Consistency is required","One success is enough","k times the runs"],["pass@k","Retry or best-of-n is real","Reliability matters","Overstates dependable ability"],["Mean pass rate (k = 1)","A single quick estimate","Judging reliability","Hides run-to-run variance"],["Paired comparison with interval","Comparing two versions","You only have one version","Needs the same items for both"],["Significance test (t-test)","A defensible yes or no","Small noisy sets","False alarms with many metrics"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"A prompt tweak raised the score 2 points then lost it next run","cause":"A single run on a small set, so the difference is inside the noise","fix":"Repeat trials, report an interval and compare paired against the baseline"},{"symptom":"pass^5 is far lower than predicted from the mean pass rate","cause":"Tasks differ in difficulty, so failures concentrate on some tasks","fix":"Estimate pass^k per task from repeated trials, not as p^k"},{"symptom":"Many metrics, and some always regress","cause":"More metrics mean more chances of a false alarm","fix":"Name one or two primary metrics per gate and correct for multiple tests"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t12-s1","title":"Refund agent, every call must be right","situation":"An agent processes customer refunds. A customer may interact with it several times a month. Each wrong refund costs money and trust. A 100-task eval is available and runs are affordable.","constraints":["Reliability is the requirement","100 tasks","Runs are affordable"],"unknowns":["How many repeat interactions per customer?","What does one failure cost?"],"question":"How do you report the agent's reliability?","options":[{"name":"Run 8 trials per task, report pass^k for k near real repeat use, with an interval","verdict":"best","why":"Consistency is the product requirement, and pass^k exposes decay that a mean hides.","tradeoff":"Eight times the runs and judge cost."},{"name":"Report the mean pass rate from one run","verdict":"poor","why":"It hides variance and makes a 75% agent look fine when three in a row succeed only about 42% of the time.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for a first rough screen."},{"name":"Report pass@8","verdict":"good","why":"Shows ceiling ability.","tradeoff":"Overstates reliability when users cannot pick the best of eight."}],"flips":[{"if":"A human reviews every output and picks","then":"pass@k better reflects the workflow."},{"if":"Budget limits runs to 3 per task","then":"Report pass^3 and say so."}],"takeaway":"Customer-facing reliability is a pass^k question."},{"id":"w14-t12-s2","title":"Two prompts, 40 items, a 3-point gap","situation":"A team compares two prompt versions on a 40-item set. Version B scores 3 points higher. Labelling more items is slow.","constraints":["40 items","3-point gap","Slow labelling"],"unknowns":["Can trials be repeated cheaply?","Are the same items used for both?"],"question":"What do you conclude and do next?","options":[{"name":"Treat it as unproven, run repeated trials with a paired comparison and grow the set","verdict":"best","why":"At n=40 the interval is about plus or minus 12 points, so 3 points is noise. Pairing the same items cuts variance.","tradeoff":"More runs and labelling time."},{"name":"Ship B because it scored higher","verdict":"poor","why":"The gap sits well inside the interval.","tradeoff":"Acceptable if the change is free, reversible and harmless."},{"name":"Compute a t-test on the single run and ship if p < 0.05","verdict":"good","why":"Better than nothing and gives a defensible number.","tradeoff":"One noisy run on 40 items gives a fragile test."}],"flips":[{"if":"Labels exist for 400 items","then":"A 3-point gap becomes measurable but still needs pairing."},{"if":"B costs less and is not worse","then":"A non-inferiority framing may justify shipping."}],"takeaway":"Small sets cannot see small gaps."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t12-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A task is tried 10 times and succeeds 7 times. Using the unbiased estimator, what is pass^3 for this task? Give a decimal.","answer":0.292,"tolerancePct":2,"hint":"C(7,3) / C(10,3).","explain":"C(7,3) = 35 and C(10,3) = 120, so 35 / 120 = 0.2917. That is far below the 0.7 per-trial rate."},{"id":"w14-t12-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"An agent succeeds on each independent trial with probability 0.75. Roughly what is the chance of three successes in a row, in percent?","answer":42,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"%","hint":"0.75 cubed.","explain":"0.75^3 = 0.4219, about 42%. This assumes identical independent trials, which real tasks only approximate."},{"id":"w14-t12-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Which change makes pass@k the better headline metric than pass^k?","options":["The agent calls tools","A user can retry or pick the best of several outputs","The eval set doubled in size","The judge became cheaper"],"answer":1,"explain":"pass@k credits at least one success, which matches workflows where retry or best-of-n exists. Tool use, set size and judge cost do not change which question you ask."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"pass@k vs pass^k?","back":"pass@k: at least one of k trials succeeds. pass^k: all k trials succeed. pass^1 equals pass@1. Use pass^k when consistency matters."},{"front":"95% half-width at n=100 for an 80% pass rate?","back":"About 7.8 points (1.96 x sqrt(0.8 x 0.2 / 100)). At n=400 it is 3.9 and at n=1,000 it is 2.5."},{"front":"Why compare two models paired?","back":"Question-level differences on the same items cancel item difficulty and shrink variance. Anthropic's error-bar guidance recommends it, plus clustered standard errors and power analysis."}],"related":["w14-t11","w14-t14","w14-t10","w14-l5","w01-t05"],"refs":["r23","r17","r24","r39","tr9","tr8"]},{"id":"w14-t13","order":13,"group":"Agents and safety","title":"Red teaming and safety evaluation","minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Red teaming probes a system with adversarial inputs to find harms and security failures. Start with manual expert probing, then automate with PyRIT and the Foundry AI Red Teaming Agent, score attack success rate, and review results by hand. Scans are non-deterministic and cover only the targets and categories each mode supports.","useIf":"You are about to expose a model or agent to users, tools or untrusted content and must measure how it fails under attack.","avoidIf":"You expect one scan to certify safety, or to run attacks against production systems that have real side effects.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Microsoft's guidance is that red teaming is not a replacement for systematic measurement: complete an initial round of manual red teaming first to list the harms, then measure and mitigate. Mix experts with an adversarial mindset and ordinary users, assign testers to harms, and test the model, the application and both before and after mitigations [[tr2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"The **AI Red Teaming Agent** uses Microsoft's open-source PyRIT [[r26]] together with Foundry risk and safety evaluations to scan, score and report. Its key metric is **attack success rate (ASR)**: successful attacks divided by total attacks. Limits: risk categories are text only, data is synthetic, mock tools only return data, and ASR is scored by a generative model, so it can be non-deterministic and produce false positives. Review before mitigating, and run in a purple environment (non-production with production-like resources) [[r5]]. Local mode is preview and not compatible with the new Foundry portal and SDK."},{"t":"flow","title":"From manual probing to a scheduled scan","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Manual round","md":"Experts and ordinary users probe for harms. Record input, output and date.","tag":"discover"},{"title":"Harm list","md":"Decide what to measure: categories, tools, languages.","tag":"scope"},{"title":"Automated scan","md":"Risk categories x objectives x attack strategies against the target.","tag":"PyRIT"},{"title":"Review ASR","md":"Humans check hits for false positives before mitigating.","tag":"triage"},{"title":"Mitigate and rescan","md":"Fix, rerun on a schedule, keep results per version.","tag":"operate"}]},{"t":"code","lang":"python","title":"Local scan (preview, azure-ai-evaluation[redteam], Python 3.10 to 3.13)","code":"from azure.identity import DefaultAzureCredential\nfrom azure.ai.evaluation.red_team import RedTeam, RiskCategory\n\nred_team = RedTeam(\n    azure_ai_project=project,                 # required\n    credential=DefaultAzureCredential(),      # required\n    risk_categories=[RiskCategory.Violence, RiskCategory.HateUnfairness],\n    num_objectives=5,                         # default is 10\n)\nresult = await red_team.scan(target=my_app_callback)","caption":"Targets can be a callback, an Azure OpenAI config or a PyRIT chat target [[r6]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where each mode applies (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Mode","Covers","Does not cover"],"rows":[["Local SDK scan (preview)","Text risk categories, single-turn only; complexity groups EASY (Base64, Flip, Morse), MODERATE (Tense), DIFFICULT (Tense with Base64). 4 categories x 1 objective x 4 strategies = 20 attack-response pairs [[r6]]","New Foundry portal and SDK"],["Cloud agentic scan","Prohibited actions, sensitive data leakage, task adherence, indirect prompt injection (XPIA). Targets: Foundry prompt agents and hosted container agents with Azure tool calls [[r5]]","Workflow agents, non-Foundry agents, function tool calls, browser automation, connected agents, computer use"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Regions differ between two Learn pages (five regions on the concept page, two on the limits page), so check both before promising a region [[r37]]. Public suites help too: HarmBench compares 18 red-teaming methods on 33 target models [[r43]], and AgentDojo has 97 tasks and 629 security test cases for prompt injection against tool-using agents [[r47]]."}],"whereToUse":["Before launching a chatbot or agent to external users","After adding tools or retrieval sources that carry untrusted text","On a schedule, because models, prompts and tools change","To produce evidence for a safety review or risk register","To seed regression cases from confirmed attack successes"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Harm from a bad output is significant","Untrusted content reaches the model","You can run in a non-production environment"],"avoid":["Treating ASR as ground truth without human review","Scanning production where tool calls have real effects","Assuming coverage for tools and agent types the scan does not support"],"flips":["Agent uses function tools: add manual and custom prompt-injection tests","Region unavailable: run local mode (preview) or manual testing","Non-English users: set the `language` option (six non-English languages are listed) and add native-speaker testers"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Manual expert red team","Discovering new harms","Repeating on every release","Expert time"],["Automated PyRIT scan","Coverage and repeatability","Novel attacks and context","Judge noise, false positives"],["Public benchmarks (HarmBench, AgentDojo)","Comparable baselines","Your tools and data","Leakage, generic"],["Safety evaluators on your dataset","Measuring defect rate on real prompts","Attack discovery","Needs representative data"],["Platform guardrails only","Baseline filtering","Application-specific abuse","Gaps unmeasured"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Attack success rate changes between identical scans","cause":"ASR is scored by a generative model and attacks are sampled","fix":"Review hits by hand, track trends across several scans and keep failing prompts as fixed regression cases"},{"symptom":"A scan reports zero risk for an agent with function tools","cause":"Function tool calls are not supported by the cloud agentic scan","fix":"Add manual tests and custom indirect-injection cases for those tools"},{"symptom":"Scan side effects appear in a real system","cause":"The target was production with live tools","fix":"Use a purple environment with production-like resources and mock or sandboxed tools"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t13-s1","title":"Public text chatbot, no tools","situation":"A consumer chatbot answers general questions with no tools or retrieval. The harm concern is offensive and unsafe content. The team has a Foundry project and no red team.","constraints":["Text only","No tools","Public users","Small team"],"unknowns":["Which languages do users speak?","Which harm classes matter most?"],"question":"What do you run before launch?","options":[{"name":"A short manual round, then an automated scan across the four content categories and difficulty groups, with human review of hits","verdict":"best","why":"Manual probing lists the harms and the automated scan adds repeatable coverage. Review handles false positives.","tradeoff":"Local mode is preview, and scans are single-turn and text only."},{"name":"Rely on platform content filters only","verdict":"poor","why":"Application-specific abuse and filter gaps stay unmeasured.","tradeoff":"Reasonable as a baseline layer, never as the only evidence."},{"name":"Run the public benchmark and ship","verdict":"good","why":"Comparable baseline numbers.","tradeoff":"It does not reflect your prompt, users or mitigations."}],"flips":[{"if":"Users are mostly non-English","then":"Add native-speaker manual testing."},{"if":"A retrieval source is added later","then":"Add indirect prompt injection tests."}],"takeaway":"Manual first, automated for repeatability, humans review."},{"id":"w14-t13-s2","title":"Agent with function tools and customer data","situation":"A support agent calls function tools to read orders and send emails. Customer data and untrusted email text reach the model. The cloud agentic scan does not support function tool calls.","constraints":["Function tools","Untrusted content","Customer data"],"unknowns":["Which tools can change data?","Can tools be sandboxed?"],"question":"How do you test for injection and data leakage?","options":[{"name":"Custom indirect-injection and leakage test cases against sandboxed tools, plus manual red team, with fixed regression cases","verdict":"best","why":"The automated agentic scan does not cover function tools, so you build the cases yourself and keep confirmed attacks as regressions.","tradeoff":"Considerable authoring effort and no vendor ASR number."},{"name":"Run the cloud agentic scan and report the result","verdict":"poor","why":"Function tool calls are not supported, so the result would not cover the real risk.","tradeoff":"It fits prompt agents or hosted agents using Azure tool calls."},{"name":"Run the text-only local scan on the underlying model","verdict":"good","why":"Measures model-level harms.","tradeoff":"It misses tool misuse and XPIA through your tools."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tools move to Azure tool calls on a Foundry agent","then":"Use the cloud agentic scan."},{"if":"Tools cannot be sandboxed","then":"Mock tool outputs and test the model decision only."}],"takeaway":"Know what the scan does not cover, then fill the gap."},{"id":"w14-t13-s3","title":"Weekly changes, regulated sector","situation":"An assistant in a regulated sector changes prompts and tools weekly. Compliance requires evidence for every release. A Foundry hosted agent uses Azure tool calls.","constraints":["Weekly change","Evidence required","Hosted agent with Azure tools"],"unknowns":["Which region is supported?","Who reviews findings?"],"question":"How do you schedule red teaming?","options":[{"name":"Scheduled cloud scans each release, human triage, confirmed attacks added to a regression suite","verdict":"best","why":"Scheduling catches regressions from weekly change and triage controls false positives.","tradeoff":"Preview features, regional limits and reviewer time."},{"name":"One scan before launch","verdict":"poor","why":"The system changes weekly, so the scan describes a past version.","tradeoff":"Fine for a frozen system."},{"name":"Manual red team every release","verdict":"good","why":"Highest insight.","tradeoff":"Too slow and costly weekly."}],"flips":[{"if":"Releases slow to quarterly","then":"Add a full manual round each release."},{"if":"Region unsupported","then":"Use local scans in a purple environment."}],"takeaway":"Change frequency sets scan frequency."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t13-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"How is attack success rate (ASR) defined in the AI Red Teaming Agent?","options":["Successful attacks divided by total attacks","Blocked attacks divided by total attacks","Attack strategies divided by risk categories","Findings reviewed by humans divided by findings"],"answer":0,"explain":"ASR is successful attacks over total attacks. It is scored by a generative model, so it can be non-deterministic and needs review."},{"id":"w14-t13-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A local scan generates one attack objective for each of 4 risk categories and converts each baseline prompt with 4 attack strategies. The baseline prompts are sent too. How many attack-response pairs result?","answer":20,"tolerancePct":0,"hint":"Baseline prompts plus converted prompts.","explain":"4 baseline prompts + 4 x 4 converted prompts = 20, which matches the documented total of 20 attack-response pairs."},{"id":"w14-t13-q3","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which are documented limits of the AI Red Teaming Agent? Select all that apply.","options":["ASR is scored by a generative model and may produce false positives","Mock tools only return data","It can only attack models, never agents","Cloud agentic scans do not support function tool calls"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Judge noise, mock tools and unsupported function tools are documented limits. It also supports agents in the cloud, so option three is wrong."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is ASR?","back":"Attack success rate: successful attacks divided by total attacks. Scored by a generative model, so it can be non-deterministic and needs human review."},{"front":"Which targets does the cloud agentic red teaming support?","back":"Foundry prompt agents and hosted container agents with Azure tool calls. Not workflow agents, non-Foundry agents, function tool calls, browser automation, connected agents or computer use."},{"front":"What is a purple environment?","back":"A non-production environment with production-like resources, used to run red team scans safely."}],"related":["w14-t08","w14-t11","w14-t14","w07-t17","w07-t19","w06-t05","w14-l6"],"refs":["r5","r6","r26","r37","r43","r47","tr2"]},{"id":"w14-t14","order":14,"group":"Operating","title":"CI gates and regression testing","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A gate is a rule plus a noise budget. Compare against a baseline with an interval or significance test instead of a fixed threshold, run a small fast suite per pull request and a large one nightly, block on safety and regression suites, and control evaluation cost.","useIf":"Prompts, models, retrievers or tools change often and you need to stop regressions before they ship.","avoidIf":"You gate on one noisy run against a fixed score, or you block merges on capability suites that are meant to start low.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Three gate types exist. A **fixed threshold** fails below a score. A **baseline comparison** fails only if the candidate is significantly worse than the current version. An **advisory** gate reports without blocking. Because outputs vary, a fixed threshold on one run flakes; the baseline plus an interval is steadier. Foundry's compare view runs t-tests and marks a change strong at p <= 0.001 and weak at 0.001 < p <= 0.05 [[r39]]. The `microsoft/ai-agent-evals` GitHub Action (preview, checked 2026-10-13) outputs confidence intervals and a significance test and, if you give no `baseline-agent-id`, treats the first agent as the baseline [[r9]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"yaml","title":"Foundry agent evaluation step (preview action)","code":"- name: Run Evaluation\n  uses: microsoft/ai-agent-evals@v3-beta\n  with:\n    azure-ai-project-endpoint: \"<project-endpoint>\"\n    deployment-name: \"<judge-deployment>\"\n    agent-ids: \"my-agent:2,my-agent:1\"   # name:version, comma separated\n    baseline-agent-id: \"my-agent:1\"      # optional\n    data-path: ${{ github.workspace }}/evals/golden.jsonl","caption":"An Azure DevOps version is also preview [[r9]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w14-t14-d1","title":"Which suite runs where","alt":"A pull request runs a fast suite of assertions and a small golden slice. A nightly run uses the full set with repeated trials and a baseline comparison. A release gate adds safety and regression suites and blocks on failure.","src":"flowchart LR\n  PR[Pull request] --> F[Fast suite]\n  F --> M{Pass?}\n  M -->|yes| N[Nightly full run]\n  M -->|no| X[Block merge]\n  N --> B[Baseline compare]\n  B --> R[Release gate: safety and regression]"},{"t":"p","md":"Block on **safety and regression** suites. Keep **capability** suites advisory: Anthropic notes capability evals start at a low pass rate and regression evals should sit near 100% [[r17]]. Hamel Husain adds that unlike unit tests you do not need 100%, since the pass rate is a product decision about tolerated failures, and that fast assertions should run on every change [[tr6]]. Tools such as promptfoo (config-driven, CI integration [[r27]]) and DeepEval (`deepeval test run`, default metric threshold 0.5 [[r28]]) fit the pipeline. OpenAI announced on 2026-06-03 that its Evals platform becomes read-only on 2026-10-31 and is scheduled to shut down on 2026-11-30, and points to Promptfoo as the migration path [[r19]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Flaky gates.** Repeat trials and compare paired. Remember that clean runs are weaker evidence than they look: with zero failures in n runs, the rule of three bounds the 95% upper limit on the failure rate near 3/n, so 30 passes in 30 allows a failure rate up to about 10% [[tr9]]. **Cost.** Judge calls scale with items x evaluators x trials. Levers: a small PR suite, a large nightly one, composite evaluators that batch criteria, sampling, a validated cheaper judge and caching unchanged targets. Foundry's target cost view excludes evaluator model usage [[r39]]."}],"whereToUse":["Pull request checks on prompts, tool schemas and retrieval settings","Nightly regression runs with repeated trials and baseline comparison","Model-version upgrades, to prove parity before switching","Release gates that combine safety and regression suites","Rollback criteria, using the same suite on the previous version"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Changes ship weekly or faster","A stable regression set exists","Failures have a defined cost"],"avoid":["Hard-failing on a fixed score from a single noisy run","Running every evaluator and trial on every PR","Blocking on capability suites"],"flips":["Judge cost dominates: shrink the PR suite, batch criteria and run the full set nightly","A safety rule is absolute: zero tolerance on named cases, no statistics","Sets are tiny: widen the tolerance band or gather more cases before blocking"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Baseline comparison with significance","Noisy outputs, close versions","Tiny sets","Needs repeated runs"],["Fixed threshold","Deterministic checks and safety cases","Stochastic scores on small sets","Flaky, trust erodes"],["Advisory dashboard","Capability suites, early work","Safety regressions must stop","Ignored in practice"],["Manual release review","Rare, high-stakes releases","Frequent changes","Slow, inconsistent"],["Canary with rollback","Real-user signal matters","Harm before detection","Users see regressions"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Engineers rerun the pipeline until it goes green","cause":"A flaky gate: one noisy run against a fixed threshold","fix":"Repeat trials, use a baseline with an interval and quarantine flaky items instead of retrying blindly"},{"symptom":"A segment regressed but the gate passed","cause":"Only the overall average was gated","fix":"Report per-segment results and gate the highest-risk slices separately"},{"symptom":"The evaluation bill rivals inference cost","cause":"Every PR runs all items, evaluators and trials","fix":"Run a small PR suite, a nightly full suite, batch criteria and sample"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t14-s1","title":"30 merges a day, noisy judge","situation":"A team has a 150-item golden set scored by a judge model. Thirty pull requests merge daily. The nightly run takes 40 minutes. The judge noise makes scores move by 3 points between identical runs.","constraints":["High merge rate","Judge noise about 3 points","Capped judge spend"],"unknowns":["Which checks are deterministic?","What regressions have hurt before?"],"question":"How do you design the gate?","options":[{"name":"Deterministic and code checks per PR, full set nightly with repeated trials and a baseline comparison","verdict":"best","why":"Fast checks catch common breakage and the noisy judge is only used where an interval can absorb its noise.","tradeoff":"Some regressions show up a day later."},{"name":"Full judged suite on every PR with a fixed 85% threshold","verdict":"poor","why":"3 points of noise will flake the gate and the cost is high.","tradeoff":"A fixed threshold suits deterministic code checks."},{"name":"Advisory dashboard only","verdict":"good","why":"Zero friction.","tradeoff":"Nothing stops a bad change from merging."}],"flips":[{"if":"Merges drop to a few a week","then":"Run the full suite on every PR."},{"if":"Judge noise is cut by calibration and a binary rubric","then":"A tighter threshold becomes feasible."}],"takeaway":"Match the gate to its noise."},{"id":"w14-t14-s2","title":"A prompt change touches the safety policy","situation":"An engineer edits the system prompt section that refuses harmful requests. A set of 60 confirmed attack prompts exists from earlier red teaming. A single bypass is unacceptable.","constraints":["Absolute safety requirement","60 named attack cases","Fast feedback needed"],"unknowns":["How are bypasses detected?","Can the attacks be replayed deterministically?"],"question":"What gate protects this change?","options":[{"name":"Hard block if any named attack case is bypassed, plus a scheduled red team scan after merge","verdict":"best","why":"An absolute requirement on named cases needs no statistics, and the scan looks for new bypasses.","tradeoff":"Occasional false blocks from a noisy attack classifier need human triage."},{"name":"Block only if the average attack score drops significantly","verdict":"poor","why":"Averaging allows individual bypasses to pass.","tradeoff":"Averages suit broad quality metrics, not absolute rules."},{"name":"Review the diff by hand and skip the suite","verdict":"good","why":"Humans can see intent.","tradeoff":"It misses behavioural regressions that a replay catches."}],"flips":[{"if":"Attack classification is noisy","then":"Rerun failing cases several times and triage by hand."},{"if":"New attacks are found","then":"Add them to the named set."}],"takeaway":"Absolute rules need hard gates, not averages."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t14-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A candidate passes 30 of 30 gate runs with no failures. By the rule of three, what is the approximate 95% upper bound on its failure rate, in percent?","answer":10,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"%","hint":"About 3 divided by n.","explain":"3 / 30 = 0.10, so a failure rate up to about 10% is still consistent with 30 clean runs."},{"id":"w14-t14-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Engineers keep rerunning a failing evaluation job until it passes. The suite scores a stochastic agent with a fixed 85% threshold on one run. What is the root cause?","options":["Too many unit tests","The golden set is too large","The agent has too few tools","A fixed threshold on a single noisy run makes the gate flaky"],"answer":3,"explain":"A single stochastic run compared with a fixed cut-off will cross it by chance. Repeat trials and use a baseline with an interval. Set size or tool count does not explain intermittent failures."},{"id":"w14-t14-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which sound gate practices fit a pipeline with a noisy judge? Select all that apply.","options":["Block releases on safety and regression suites","Keep capability suites advisory","Gate on a single run against a fixed 90% score","Run a small suite per PR and the full suite nightly"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Safety and regression suites protect existing behaviour, capability suites are exploratory, and a small PR suite plus a nightly full run controls cost. A single-run fixed threshold flakes."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which suites should block a release?","back":"Safety and regression suites. Capability suites start at a low pass rate, so keep them advisory."},{"front":"What does the rule of three say?","back":"With zero failures in n runs, the 95% upper bound on the failure rate is about 3/n. Thirty clean runs still allow roughly a 10% failure rate."},{"front":"How does the microsoft/ai-agent-evals action report results?","back":"It compares agent versions (name:version, comma separated) and outputs confidence intervals and a significance test. The first agent is the baseline unless baseline-agent-id is set. Preview."}],"related":["w14-t12","w14-t09","w14-t13","w18-t03","w16-t01","w14-l6"],"refs":["r9","r17","r19","r27","r28","r39","tr6","tr9"]},{"id":"w14-t15","order":15,"group":"Operating","title":"A/B, shadow, canary and feedback signals","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Online experiments test a new version on live traffic. Shadow traffic hides it from users, a canary exposes a small share, an A/B test randomises users to measure outcomes, and feedback signals feed triage. Pick by harm if the new version is worse, traffic volume and what quality signal exists without references.","useIf":"Offline results look good but only real traffic can show user outcomes, drift or rare failures.","avoidIf":"Harm from the worse version is high and you have no safe exposure path, or traffic is too low to detect the effect you care about.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Online methods trade safety for realism. **Shadow** copies live requests to the new version and discards its answers, so users see nothing, but you get no user reaction. **Canary** sends a small share of users to the new version. An **A/B test** randomises users between versions and compares outcome metrics. **Continuous evaluation** scores sampled traffic with judges. **Feedback** (thumbs, edits, retries, escalations) is cheap, biased toward vocal users and best used to find new test cases. LangSmith automation rules, for example, can add low-feedback traces to a dataset [[r29]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Progressive exposure","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Offline gate","md":"Golden set, safety and regression suites pass.","tag":"no users"},{"title":"Shadow","md":"Mirror a share of traffic. Score outputs. Users see nothing.","tag":"no exposure"},{"title":"Canary","md":"A small share of users. Stop-loss on safety, escalation, latency and cost.","tag":"limited"},{"title":"A/B","md":"Randomised split to measure outcomes with enough samples.","tag":"measured"},{"title":"Roll out or roll back","md":"Decide on the pre-agreed guardrails, not the best-looking chart.","tag":"decide"}]},{"t":"p","md":"Azure Machine Learning traffic mirroring copies a share of live requests to a new deployment without changing client responses. The mirrored share is capped at 50%, an endpoint can hold one shadow deployment, it is not supported on Kubernetes online endpoints, and the default endpoint bandwidth quota is 5 MBps [[r36]]. No Foundry-native shadow feature turned up in a Microsoft Learn search on 2026-10-13, so for LLM apps expect to mirror at the gateway or in your own router. Foundry continuous evaluation (preview) uses Random or Intelligent sampling, defaults to 100 runs per hour and needs the Foundry User role on the project's managed identity [[r7]]. Intelligent sampling dedupes, filters broken traces and picks diverse ones without extra model calls [[r40]]. Online scoring runs asynchronously and uses judges because live traffic has no ground truth [[r30]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Samples per arm for an A/B test (two proportions, unpaired)","expr":"n = 2 x (1.96 + 0.84)^2 x p(1 - p) / d^2","vars":[{"k":"p","v":"baseline success rate"},{"k":"d","v":"smallest difference worth detecting"},{"k":"1.96, 0.84","v":"z for alpha 0.05 two-sided and for power 0.80"}],"example":"Near p = 0.80: d = 10 points needs about 251 per arm, d = 5 points about 1,004, d = 3 points about 2,788 (computed estimate with the same variance in both arms; 80% against 70% with each arm's own variance needs about 290; paired designs need fewer)."},{"t":"table","caption":"Choosing a method","head":["Method","User exposure","Gives you","Cannot tell you"],"rows":[["Shadow","None","Output quality on real inputs","User reaction, outcomes"],["Canary","Small share","Early harm detection","Small effects"],["A/B","Half or a chosen share","Causal outcome effect","Anything without enough samples"],["Sampled online scoring","None","Drift and quality trend","Causality"],["Feedback signals","None","Triage candidates","Representative quality"]]}],"whereToUse":["Swapping a model version where harm is possible","Changing prompts, retrieval or tools that alter user outcomes","Drift monitoring through sampled online scoring","Turning thumbs-down and retries into new offline cases","Cost and latency comparisons under real load"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Offline gates pass and you need real-world evidence","You can define stop-loss metrics and rollback","Traffic is large enough for the effect you care about"],"avoid":["Shadowing agents with write tools without mocking the side effects","Stopping an A/B test early because the chart looks good","Using thumbs-up rate as the only success metric"],"flips":["Harm if worse is high: shadow first, then a tiny canary","Traffic is low: skip A/B, use canary plus sampled human review","Content cannot be read: score metadata and safety signals only"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Shadow traffic","Harm is high, you want zero exposure","You need user outcome data","Doubled model cost, tool side effects"],["Canary","Early warning on a limited share","Detecting small effects","A small group sees the worse version"],["A/B test","Measuring real outcomes","Low traffic or high harm","Needs thousands of samples for small gaps"],["Sampled online scoring","Watching drift without references","Content is unreadable","Judge tokens and privacy review"],["Feedback signals","Triage and new test cases","Measuring quality overall","Skewed toward vocal users"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"The shadow agent sends duplicate emails or writes duplicate records","cause":"Mirrored requests executed real write tools","fix":"Run the shadow with read-only or mocked tools, and compare tool calls instead of executing them"},{"symptom":"Thumbs-down jumped after a release, but offline metrics are flat","cause":"A UI or latency change moved feedback, not the model","fix":"Segment by version, check latency and compare with a control group"},{"symptom":"An A/B test declared a winner after two days, then reversed","cause":"Peeking and stopping early on noisy data","fix":"Fix the sample size and duration in advance, and decide against guardrail metrics"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w14-t15-s1","title":"Financial guidance, new model version","situation":"An assistant gives account guidance to retail customers, 20,000 requests a day. A new model version passes the offline suite. A wrong answer has regulatory consequences.","constraints":["High harm if worse","20,000 requests a day","Rollback must be fast"],"unknowns":["Can requests be mirrored at a gateway?","Which stop-loss metrics apply?"],"question":"How do you roll the new version out?","options":[{"name":"Shadow with sampled scoring, then a 1% canary with stop-loss, then A/B if needed","verdict":"best","why":"Shadow exposes no one, and the canary adds real outcomes behind guardrails.","tradeoff":"Slower rollout, doubled model cost during shadow and gateway work to mirror."},{"name":"A/B test at 50% from day one","verdict":"poor","why":"Half the customers could see a worse version of regulated guidance.","tradeoff":"Right when harm is low and rollback is instant."},{"name":"Big-bang release with monitoring","verdict":"good","why":"Fast and simple.","tradeoff":"All users exposed before any real evidence and slow to detect harm."}],"flips":[{"if":"Mirroring is not possible","then":"Start with a very small canary and sampled human review."},{"if":"The change is only a cost-saving model swap with equal offline results","then":"A shorter canary may be enough."}],"takeaway":"High harm means shadow first, then a tiny canary."},{"id":"w14-t15-s2","title":"Copy assistant, millions of requests, low harm","situation":"A marketing copy assistant handles two million requests a day. Rollback is a flag flip. The team wants to know whether a new prompt raises the share of drafts users accept.","constraints":["Low harm","Huge traffic","Instant rollback","Outcome metric exists"],"unknowns":["What acceptance rate and difference matter?","Is assignment sticky per user?"],"question":"Which online method do you use?","options":[{"name":"A/B test with sticky assignment, preset sample size and guardrail metrics","verdict":"best","why":"Traffic supports small effects, the outcome metric is direct and rollback is cheap.","tradeoff":"Some users see the worse prompt for the test duration."},{"name":"Shadow only","verdict":"poor","why":"It cannot measure acceptance because no user sees the draft.","tradeoff":"Shadow is right when exposure is unacceptable."},{"name":"Judge-scored sampling only","verdict":"good","why":"No exposure and gives a quality trend.","tradeoff":"Judge preference may not match what users accept."}],"flips":[{"if":"Drafts could contain legal claims","then":"Add a canary stage and safety guardrails first."},{"if":"Traffic is 200 requests a day","then":"An A/B test would take too long, so use a canary and sampled review."}],"takeaway":"Big traffic and low harm make A/B the best-evidence method."},{"id":"w14-t15-s3","title":"Internal tool, 200 requests a day","situation":"An internal HR assistant gets about 200 requests a day from 80 employees. The team wants to know if a new retrieval setting helps. Mistakes are embarrassing but not costly.","constraints":["Low traffic","Low harm","Small user base"],"unknowns":["What effect size matters?","Are users willing to give feedback?"],"question":"How do you evaluate the change online?","options":[{"name":"Canary by user group for two weeks with sampled review and an offline replay on logged queries","verdict":"best","why":"Traffic is too low for a powered A/B, so combine a controlled exposure with offline replay of real queries.","tradeoff":"It will detect only large differences."},{"name":"A/B test aiming to detect a 3-point difference","verdict":"poor","why":"It needs thousands of samples per arm, far beyond 200 requests a day.","tradeoff":"Useful at millions of requests."},{"name":"Roll out and read thumbs-down counts","verdict":"good","why":"Cheap.","tradeoff":"Sparse, biased feedback."}],"flips":[{"if":"The team only needs to avoid harm","then":"Run a shadow replay and skip the live exposure."},{"if":"User base grows tenfold","then":"Revisit a powered A/B test."}],"takeaway":"Low traffic cannot power small-effect tests."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w14-t15-q1","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A new model version must be tested on real inputs, but no customer may see its answers. Which method fits?","options":["A/B test","Canary","Shadow traffic","Thumbs-up collection"],"answer":2,"explain":"Shadow traffic mirrors requests and discards the new version's responses. A/B and canary expose users, and thumbs need users to see answers."},{"id":"w14-t15-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Using n = 2 x (1.96 + 0.84)^2 x p(1 - p) / d^2 with p = 0.80 and d = 0.10, about how many samples per arm are needed?","answer":251,"tolerancePct":3,"hint":"2 x 7.84 x 0.16 / 0.01.","explain":"2 x 7.84 x 0.16 = 2.509, divided by 0.01 gives about 251 per arm. Halving the difference to 0.05 quadruples it to about 1,004."},{"id":"w14-t15-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"This shadow plan mirrors production traffic to a new agent version. What is the risk?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"mirror: 20% of requests -> agent v2 (shadow)\nagent v2 tools: send_email, update_ticket, search_kb\nresponses from v2: discarded"},"options":["Mirroring above 10% is disallowed","Discarding responses breaks logging","v2 will execute its write tools, causing real side effects","Shadow requires fewer tools"],"answer":2,"explain":"Discarding the response does not stop write tools from running. Use read-only or mocked tools in shadow and compare tool calls instead. The mirror share is not the problem here."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Shadow vs canary?","back":"Shadow mirrors requests to the new version and discards its answers, so users see nothing. Canary sends a small share of users to the new version, so some are exposed."},{"front":"Azure ML traffic mirroring limits?","back":"Maximum mirrored share 50%, one shadow deployment per endpoint, not supported on Kubernetes online endpoints. No Foundry-native shadow feature found on 2026-10-13."},{"front":"What are feedback signals good for?","back":"Triage. Thumbs, edits, retries and escalations are cheap but biased toward vocal users, so use them to find new test cases, not as the only metric."}],"related":["w14-t02","w14-t14","w14-t12","w15-t11","w16-t01","w14-l6"],"refs":["r36","r7","r40","r30","r29","tr6"]}]});
